      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>VERY DAY</head>
        <p>VERY DAY</p>
        <p>IS A</p>
        <p>Bargain Day t Dutton's Sale.</p>
        <p>As a means whereby a whole host of useful and fashionable articles of every day use are available at very substantial reductions in price Dutton's Sale takes first place in any well-considered scheme of economy. The Bargains placed at your disposal are certainly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PRACTICAL ECONOMIES</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL ECONOMIES</p>
        <p>that no one can well afford to miss. With holidays so close at hand the savings on Summer Costumes, Washing Frocks, Skirts, Blouses, Hats and Coats, to say nothing of utilities such as Hosiery and Gloves, are more than welcome. Every article bought at a Dutton Bargain Price means less expense and con- sequently more money to spend on the Holiday itself. But remember this, EVERYTHING is reduced and WHATEVER you require is easier to buy at Saletime than it will be later on.</p>
        <p>J.&amp;W.DUTTON,LTD.</p>
        <p>SANKEY STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>"Called or Known."</p>
        <p>'A place ,known as Stockton Heath." That was a delicious touch in Niece Doris Calvert's letter on the best kind of school treat to take the place of Walking Day. So I thought, at least; possibly there were "Heathen " readers who did not appreciate it just as highly. I refer to it here as recalling a common expres- sion in the mouths of market quack doctors, cheap jacks, gutter merchants, et hoc genus omne. I think I have previously confessed a weakness for the society of such people, and my experience is that whenever they refer to a well-known. town, they invariably prefix it with the phrase "a place called," as if we had never heard the name before. There is one old quack whom I have come across in various places in the course of the last quarter of a century, and this example may serve for the class of itinerants generally. He is fond of confiding to listeners particulars of his round of the current week, and he will tell them how "yesterday I was at a place called Wigan, and the day before at a place called Southport; to-morrow I shall be at a place called Stock- port, and the day after at a place called Burn- ley." . This curious trick of speech is common to the travelling fraternity; I have often won- dered at its perpetuation.</p>
        <p>Blind Quartet. I note that at a musical service at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday, the quartet "God is a Spirit" constituted one of the items. One has, of course, heard it may a time and oft, but one of the best as well as decidedly the most impressive renderings 1 remember was by a quartet of blind persons. The performances of sightless musician::. vocalists and instrumentalists alike always affect me deeply, though apart from the pathetic circumstances, many of these—organ recitalists especially—judged solely on their merits in comparison with those in full possession of the precious faculty in question, were of a decidedly superior order. It would indeed appear that there are compensations for those afflicted with visual blindness; as Shirley puts it (I am aware that neither Milton—who was himdeli blind—nor Shakespeare shares this optimism). "Nature recompenseth the defects of one part with redundance in another."</p>
        <p>Disciples of Isaai.</p>
        <p>" Critic " writes in Lymm "Facts and Fancies" of local anglers daily pursuing their favourite "pastime." That strikes me as some- what singular—it is singular as a matter of fact; the point I venture to raise is—should it not be plural? There are two books bearing upon it, both published a comparatively short time ago. There is Corryeen's "The gentle art of angling, a practical handbook," which is what it claims in all seriousness to be; and then there is W. Carter Platts' "Light lines and tight lines," which has been well described as as much a kind of Joe Miller Joke Book vs a fishing manual, though it is practical, too. But does not the average angler's sport cnn- sist almost as muob in spinning yarns as in fishing? Then, with all due diffidence, I sub- mit that those enthusiastic Lymm sportsmen may well be regarded as "pursuing their favourite pastimes." Anyhow, I like to, think of them as at one and the same time casting the line and drawing the long bow. The Eternal Feminine.</p>
        <p>Anglers have been accustomed to speak Old write of their beloved " Isaak " as the founder of their cult, but Mr. R. B. Marston, who has gone very deeply into the matter and sifted the evidence thoroughly, has arrived at the startling conclusion (vide his "Walton and the earlier fishing waters ") that Dame Juliana Berners actually taught Isaak the, theory and some of the practice of the business, and "has a better claim to be considered the compiler of the first English work on fishing than anyone else." Moreover, the handicapped sex are still in the running. Mr. Platts points out that the Florida tarpon score is held by n lady with a fish of 1781 b., that of a skate of 150 Th. was captured by a feminine expert off the Irish coast lately, and that in 1907 the Duchess of Roxburghe got the heaviest rod-caught salmon on the Tweed. We speak of women coming to the front to take the places of men who have gone to the Front; but they have really been there in spirit all the while, bless their hearts, and have been laughing in their sleeves at our clumsy conceit, and now, these abnormal times having afforded every oppor- tunity to show what they can do, they are beating "mere man" hands down:</p>
        <p>Recreation. Angling was the favourite recreation of the late Mr. John Bright. The late Mr. Joseph Chamberlain was wont to avow his contempt for all forms of physical exercise, and for years before he died he was a pitiable wreck. Yet recreation has played but an infinitesimal part in the lives of other distinguished men. Keats declared he "had no enjoyment in the world but the eternal drinking of knowledge"; W. B. Yeats that "a right understanding of life is more important than amusement"; and Renan that he felt no need for external amuse- ment. It remains true that "games, gaieties, sports, spectacles there will be as long as men have limbs or eyes or ears"; yet should' one's recreations be "manly, moderate, seasonable and lawful." As Anna Maria Porter puts it, "The habit of dissipating every serious thought by a succession of agreeable sensations is as fatal to happiness as to virtue."</p>
        <p>" Free S2=endary Education." This is what co-operators in congress assembled lately resolved to demand almost to a man. I once lived in a district where there was actually "free secondary education for all." The local authorities were wont proudly to boast of their unique position. Cotton lords sent their children to the feeless school, and the young men and women holding the best positions in the place had all passed through it. However, before I left, the County Education Committee, 'who controlled the purse strings, had effectually put an end to all this " non- sense." The old school was condemned and the erection of new premises was insisted upon—as was also the imposition of a fee of six guineas per annum, with the concession of a few free seats only for scholarship holders of the pooret class, Bald and Sensitive.</p>
        <p>I temporarily overlooked the case of the'appli- cant to a local tribunal who professed that his premature and extreme baldness made military service on his part impossible, as he would be the'butt of his comrades. Did I ever tell you of a man of that sort whom I met in a hair- dressers' shop? He was being shaved when I entered, but he had not removed his headgear. He was unmercifully chaffing an acquaintance who was waiting to be operated upon. The latter submitted good-naturedly for a while, and then, apparently rather annoyed that the "fun" at his expense should be persisted in after a stranger' had entered, quietly retorted: "Well, I dar tey mi cap off to be shaved an' that's moor nor tha dar." The rest was silence. In due course I asked the barber about it, and hp said it was a fact /that the customer in question was so conscious of his baldness that he always retained his head-covering while being shaved.</p>
        <p>Narrow Escape. The mere mention of a gun in recent Stockton Heath news sufficed to send my mind back to a period when as a youth I spent my. holidays on a farm near Ripon kept by an uncle of mine. One of his three sons was fond shooting, and would steal forth with an old gun that was a positive danger to himself. lie would pot at any small bird that came within range, so that I soon found myself solemnly pledged not to report his unheard-of crime in killing a robin. Then came the crisis. Stand- ing a good distance away, my cousin called upon me to witness his rare cleverness with the rifle. Holding the ancient " jiggerum " in his right hand, and extending his arm to the limit, he pulled the trigger, whereupon the gun-barrel burst. He had the wonderful luck to escape injury, though if he had been holding the gun in the usual position, his left arm would no doubt have been shattered.</p>
        <p>A turtle weighing a ton has been caught off the Scilly -Isles. The animal, which made no attempt to resist capture, stated that it was tired of being mistaken for a submarine.— "Punch."</p>
        <p>Optimism. The Judge: "Three years." Optimistic Prisoner: "Couldn't you make it three years or the duration of the war,' me lud."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SMALL DIFFERENCE.</head>
        <p>A SMALL DIFFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Education Committee's Dispute</p>
        <p>With Health Committee.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL MEDICAL APPOINTMENTS. The dispute between the Health Committee and the , Medical Inspection Committee is not yet over, but, as the Chairman of the Educa- tion Committee (Councillor Henry Roberts) remarked at Monday's meeting, the matter has now resolved itself into a vezy small difference, "and there is no longer any serious bone of contention between us."</p>
        <p>As to who had the right of appointment of officials to undertake medical work in the schools was the question which led to the dis- pute between the two committees. The Chair- man of the Education Committee, of which the Medical Inspection Committee is an important part, wrote to the Board of Education on the matter, with the result that a communication was sent to the Town Clerk, in which it was observed that "the Board note that the ap- pointment of all officers wholly employed in school medical work will in future• be made by the Education Committee, and that, as regards the appointment of the assistant medical officer of health and school medical officer, the Education Committee will be effectively -con- sulted, and no appointment will be made with- out their views being considered."</p>
        <p>Before the Educiition Committee met cn Monday, they assembled as a General Pur- poses Committee, and resolved: "That the Education Committee approve of the terms as to the appointments of officers for school medi- cal work as set out in the foregoing extract from the letter of the Board of Education to the Town Clerk, dated May 5, 1916," and also decided: "That a copy of the committee's proceedings as recorded above be forwarded to the Health Committee, and that they be in- formed that this committee is of opinion that the proposals contained in the resolution of the Health Committee, approved by the Town Council on April 4, 1916, in so far as they refer to the appointment of the assistant medical officer of health and school medical officer do not provide that the Education• Committee shall be 'effectively consulted' within the terms of the Board of Education's letter of May 5, 1916." ,The resolution of the Health Committee referred to was as follows: •</p>
        <p>"That the selection of the deputy medical officer of health, so long as he shall carry ott duties directly in connection with the inspec- tion of school children, be made by a joint committee, consisting of the members of the Health Committee and three members of the Medical Inspection Sub-committee of the Education Committee, and that the appoint- ment of all other officers wholly employed in school medical work be made by the Education Committee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Mr. Kingdon Not Satisfied With Health Ccmmittee's Offer.</head>
        <p>Mr. Kingdon Not Satisfied With Health Ccmmittee's Offer.</p>
        <p>In moving that the resolutions of the General Purposes Committee be adopted by the Education Committee, Mr. Holman Kingdon, who is chairman of the Medical Inspection Committee, said he wanted to make it per- fectly clear that the resolution passed by the Health Committee was not and never would be a satisfactory settlement of the difficulty be- tween the two committees. The Board of Education had acted wisely in not interfering more than they possibly could in the details of the matter. He was perfectly satisfied with their verdict, which was that the Medical In- spection Committee had to be effectively con- sulted in making those appointments which had been in dispute. The suggestion of the Health Committee was that the so-called joint ' committee to deal with the appointments should consist of the whole of the Health Committee and three members of the Medical Inspection Committee. No one could possibly say that that was effective consultation, and he regretted that, after all that had happened, the Health Committee could only see their way to give the Medical Inspection Committee what he might fairly and reasonably describe as the bare legal minimum which they were advised by their own legal adviser the latter committee could de- mand. It did not show the spirit which he hoped would have been shown to settle that regrettable dispute once and for all. If the Health Committee would meet them in a reasonable way, they would not find the Medical Inspection Committee behind-hand in stepping over their side of the line. They all had the interests of the children at heart, and this ought to be the last ever heard of any dispute between the two committees. If it were not, it certainly would not be the fault of the Medical Inspection Committee. Councillor Dickinson (who is a member of the Health as well as of the Education Com- mittee): If the Health Committee have given all to the Medical Inspection Committee that belongs to them, as Mr. Kingdon says they have done, I would like to know how much more Mr. Kingdon wants. Mr. Kingdon: I said they had given us the bare legal minimum—in other words they did not give us anything. They only allowed us to have what they had no right to keep. Councillor Dickinson recollected that when Councillor Roberts appeared before the Health Committee he suggested that the Education Committee would be satisfied if the appoint- merits came before the Health Committee and three or four members of the Medical Inspec- tion Committee.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: And you very politely kicked me out. (Laughter.) Councillor Dickinson: I don't know anything about that. • (More laughter.) If memory stTves me rightly, that was the thet•ry you advanced on that occasion.</p>
        <p>The CP.airman: What I mean was that I'got absolute/1y no support in my effort to effect a settlement.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dickinson: Now Mr. Kingdon wants the Health Committee kicked out.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kingdon: There is nothing further from my desire.</p>
        <p>"Mr. A. J. Jolley said he understood that those who voted for the minutes did not com- mit themselves to any expressions of opinion or to any sentiments except those embodied in the minutes. Some of them would have preferred the minutes to be passed in silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>No Serious Bone of Contention Now.</head>
        <p>No Serious Bone of Contention Now.</p>
        <p>Concluding the discussion, the Chairman re- marked that the matter now resolved itself into a very small difference. They had secured, by the authority of the Board of Education, and with the consent of the Health Committee, the appointment of all the officers who were exclusively employed in work connected with the medical inspection of schools. The only two appointments left outside were those of medical officer of health for the borough, and of the assistant medical officer of health, who was also the medical officer for the schools. 7n regard to those two appointments the Board cf Education said the Education Committee ought to be in effective consultation with the Health Committee when the appointment was made. The only difference now was as to the meaning of effective consultation. The Health Com- mittee proposed that when the appointments were made they, as a Health Committee, should sit accompanied by three members cf the Education Committee. The only difference that could arise was as to whether the consti- tution of an appointing committee on those lines would give to the Education Committee effective consultation within the meaning of the words used by the Board of Education. There was no longer any serious bone of con- tention among them, and they could well afford to wait until the time arose when .probably on both sides there might be a disposition to look more broadly on these appointments. The minutes were then approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>" SANITAS " COMPANY, LTD.</head>
        <p>THE " SANITAS " COMPANY, LTD.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the " Sanitas " Com- pany was held at Winchester House, London, E.C., on Wednesday, under the chairmanship of Mr. C. T. Kingzett, when a final dividend of five per cent per annum was de- clared, making up the usual annual 'distribu- tion that has been paid for so many years of 71 per cent. The business of the company has been well maintained and, in spite of the un- favourable war conditions, " Sanitas " line's retain their high popularity. The company have recently introduced several new prepara- tions, including " Sanitas Anti-Vermin Paste," which is reputed to be a simple, safe and pre- ventive remedy for body vermin, and therefore eminently useful to our soldiers at the Front; " Kaytor," an application for use in cases of nasal catarrh and hay fever; and " Potex," a scientific potato remedy, which has been proved to afford great relief in cases of rheumatism, lumbago and allied painful affections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ROYAL SHOW.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL SHOW.</p>
        <p>Warrington &amp; District Winners</p>
        <p>At Manchester.</p>
        <p>SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S FINE PIGS.</p>
        <p>All that is best in the agricultural, horticul- tural and canine world has been assembled en masse at Alexandra Park, Manchester, this week; and all those who are interested, either professionally or spectacularly, have found profit and pleasure in visiting the scene. Although, unfortunately, other duties have prevented the attendance of the King, it is an ideal Royal Show in every respect. Opened on Tuesday, it concludes to-day, and everything portends that it will beat all records. And this in war time, when only a couple of months ago doubt was expressed as to the advisability of holding it! But, after all, it takes us in Lanca- shire to make things go. Liverpool created a record in 1910; since then the show has been held in various parts of the country, and still Liverpool held the blue riband. Now Manches- ter is likely to capture it, but Lancashire still retains the honour.</p>
        <p>Lancashire has an interesting record in farm- ing. Within its boundaries are to be found some of the finest as well as some of the poorest soils in the country, and almost every class of land between these two extremes. The methods of cropping and stocking are correspondingly varied except in so far as they are controlled by market influences. The great shipping and in- dustrial centres that abound in parts of the county have a visible effect on the character and constitution of Lancashire farming. The land near the cities and towns, almost irrespec- tive of its fertility, is utilised intensively for the production of crops or animals. An insati- able local demand makes profitable methods that in remoter districts would be impracticable. The stimulating effect of ready markets is reflected in the intensive system of tillage and dairying, not only on the rich soils bordering the Mersey estuary, but in areas possessing no pronounced natural advantages. There are many keen agriculturists around Warrington, and many in the town who are in- terested in the industry; and these have gone in their hundreds to the show. A considerable number of members of the Warrington and Dis- trict Agricultural Society visited the exhibition at the invitation of their President (Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.), who generously gave them tickets.</p>
        <p>Some have exhibited there and gained valuable prizes, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., setting up a fine standard. In the Large Whites (pigs), as usual, he obtained many successes, including five first prizes. He also won a gold medal for the best boar and one for the best sow. Mr. P. Davies, of Warburton, was second with a mare, foaled in or before 1911 (with foal at foot), and was also third with a Shire gelding, foaled in or before 1913, Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., obtaining the second prize. In the hackney pony class, Mr. J. Ball, of Warrington, was first with a stallion, foaled in or before 1913, not exceeding 14 hands. In the Shorthorn Dairy .section, Mr. T. War- burton, of Warrington, gained the second prize for a cow in calf.</p>
        <p>Training Girls for Farms.</p>
        <p>Some hundreds of visitors have watched with interest the work of the women on a sector of land in the show grounds, and in view of the labour problem this is proving one of the most attractive sections of the show.</p>
        <p>With several girls ploughing, harrowing, diseing, and hoeing at the same time, it forms a splendid rural picture in the bright sunshine. Amidst the crowd are always young ladies, evi- dently picking up points. Miss Frances Knowles, one of the joint secre- taries of the Lancashire and Cheshire County Committees, who are placing women on the land; says that the comments of the farmer spec- tators had been on the whole distinctly favour- able, and numerous inquiries had been made for women farm hands.</p>
        <p>"We are training them as fast as we can," Mths Knowles adds, "but not fast enough to meet the demand. We• have already placed eleven hundred girls on farms in the two coun- ties, and they are doing extraordinarily well!' The setting out of the showyard was admir- able, the Royal Pavilion being allotted the place of honour in the Park. It looked splendidly attractive within and without, having been beautifully decorated and furnished by Messrs. Kendal, Milne and Co., of Manchester, under the direction of Lady Greenall, who came over from France a few days before the show opened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Messrs. Gartons' Stand.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Gartons' Stand.</p>
        <p>At a Royal Show, or any agricultural exhibi- tion, a great feature is the stands occupied by dealers in implements and raw materials for the fields. This week there is almost an in- numerable array of them. On Stand No. 10, to the left of the main entrance, Messrs. Gartons, Limited, the scien- tific farm plant breeders and experts, of War- rington, whose famous breeds of farm plants are so well known in practically every civilised part of the world where agriculture is practised, have a most impressive and instructive exhibit of their new and regenerated breeds of farm plants. In times like the present when, for the security of our island country, it is necessary to obtain every ounce of food, whether for man or animal, from the land, the use of the heaviest yielding farm seeds is a matter of supreme importance. The world's record crops of all farm seeds are claimed to have been grown from the new and regenerated breeds raised by Messrs. Gartons, Limited, and very fine samples of the firm's new and regenerated grains were to be seen on their stand. Of outstanding importance was the firm's new breed of barley for introduction next spring, which has been named "1917," in the hope that next year will see peace declared. This is claimed to be an excellent breed of strong straw and a complete absence of necking, which results in a serious loss in many barley crops every year. This new breed is sure to have a great reception next spring, as in those districts where Messrs. Gartons' growers have been handling this particular variety during the last two or three years it already holds a posi- tion of high esteem. Wheats for Autumn.</p>
        <p>Other leading features on the stand are the firm's new and regenerated wheats for the com- ing autumn, their new Benefactor and new and regenerated Victor being especially promi- nent. These two wheats are claimed to be the heaviest yielding wheats in cultivation, having cropped up to 1O quarters per statute acre, and in the latest trials of the Leeds University College, where Victor has taken premier posi- tion for five years in succession, it was just beaten this year in yield by Benefactor, which, however, produced a grain of much better quality. This increased quality has represented a. difference in its trials of no less than 3s. 6d. and 4s. per quarter, and when this extra price is made on a big crop, such as Victor and Bene- factor are capable of growing, this is a feature which will be espeeially welcome to all wheat growers. Red-grained wheats in this exhibit include the firm's new and regenerated breeds of Squareheads Master, Browick, and Rivetts Bearded, all of which have a high reputation in different parts of the country for varying classes of soil. - Although Messrs. Gartons' staff has been much depleted for military purposes, the firm is spar- ing no effort to maintain its very high reputa- tion, and intending customers can look forward to being served in the future quite as well, if not better, than in the Past. Non-Alcoholic Beverages.</p>
        <p>Those who realise the value of a high stan- dard in the production of non-alcoholic beverages have found much tointerest bury and Brown's ex.hibits. Nowadays, when efficient work is especially needful, their drinks make a considerable appeal, being refreshing and invigorating. The Cw ..t.-- . Spring in the Stretton Hills, owned by this firm, supplies a wonderfully pure water, and " Stretton " table water is ouite equal to the best obtainable from the much-lauded Continental spas. There is abundant evidence of the genuineness of this claim awaiting anyone who can get round to Jewsbury and Brown's stand.</p>
        <p>A woman is apt to believe everything her husband tells her until she has been married at least two weeks.</p>
        <p>Not Always. Doctor (examining recruit): "And do you always stutter like that?" Recruit: "N -n-no, Sir. Only w-w-w_when. t-t-talk."---" Punch."</p>
      </div>
