      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>THE ENGLISH LAKES. It needs no words of mine to tell of the beauties of the English Lakes, for they have been described over and over again by far more capable pens. But I can speak of them from the cyclist's point of view, since I have cycled throughout the district in every month of the year. For a cyclidk holiday the Lake country cannot be beaten. Make Kendal your starting point, and the following round will take you through the best of the scenery :—Newby Bridge, 111; Coniston, 10i; Ambleside, 71; Thirl- mere, 81 • Keswick, 91; the Buttermere round, 241; Penrith, 181; Pooley, 51; Patterdale, 81; Ambleside, 7; Windermere, 4.1• Kendal, 71; total 123 miles. The roads, on the whole, are very good. Of course, they are hilly, but one can't traverse moun- tains and valleys without hills. Here and there the surfaces are vile—over the Honister and Kirk- stone Passes, for instance—but these are the ex- ceptions. I can't attempt in a single paragraph to describe this lovely district, but there are a few places that stand out, perhaps, more prominently than others. Coniston, where Ruskin lived, and died, is beautifully situated on the lake of that name. Ambleside, the centre of Lakeland, and the site of a Roman camp, is surrounded on all sides by mountains, and affords a good centre for easy climbs. Stockgill Force is well worth a visit. Grasmere, to reach which a slight detour is neces- sary, was the home of Wordsworth, and he is buried in the churchyard. Coleridge and De Quincy also lived there. There is a fide run by Thirlmere, slumbering at the foot of Helvellyn. From Keswick, itself a fascinating town at the foot of Skiddaw, the Buttermere round should be taken, passing en route Lodore Falls, Grange (just beyond which is the Bowder Stone), Seatoller; over the wild Honister Pass (the steepest ascent and descent in England); then to Buttermere and the stern and forbidding Crummock Water, and back to Keswick by the Newlands Vale. From Keswick the Penrith road is taken, passing en route Threl- keld, Troutbeck and Penruddock. The run to Ambleside, along the side of Ullswater, is one of the grandest in the Lake district. Kirkstone Pass, nearly 1,000 ft.. in height, is a stiff climb, and the descent has to be negotiated with care. The Traveller's Rest, beyond the summit, is claimed to be the highest inhabited licensed house in England, but it is only a local claim. In returning to Ken- dal the Windermere and StaVeley road may be taken, but with the addition of only threequarters of a mile a prettier and better road can be followed. When a quarter of a mile south of Windermere the left hand turning should be taken, riding along the west side of the lake. A CHEAP CHAIN LUBRICANT.</p>
        <p>I have before pointed out in these columns that Russian tallow is about the finest chain lubricant one can have, but it seems to be difficult to get hold of nowadays, especially in smaller places. At none of my usual shops was I able to renew my supply, so I had to cast round for a substitute. I found this in mutton suet, and up to the present there is not a pin to choose between it and the tallow. Also it is far cheaper. I melted the fat in a frying pan, putting in just sufficient to cover the chain when coiled up and placed flat. The pan, containing the chain, was allowed to remain on a</p>
        <p>REDUCTION OF SCHOOL AGE IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Board 01 Education's Fears.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire County Educa- tion Authority at Crewe a communication was received from the Board of Education upon the, subject of the relaxation of the school attend- ance bye-laws and the reduction of the age for leaving school to thirteen years. The letter stated that the Board of Education felt con- siderable difficulty in assenting to the view that the local authority are justified by the special circumstances in permitting a general relaxation of their bye-laws so as to allow chil- dren to leave school at the age of thirteen without regard to the peculiar needs of parti. cular districts or particular industries the maintenance of which is important in the national interest. The Board feared that unless Isome definite rules are adopted limiting the lexemptions to organised industries and to dis- tricts which are in special need of labour there was a serious risk that many children woUld</p>
        <p>be exempted for work of a merely casual char' atter which possessed no national importanAc: or which could be carried out by other kix't., of labour. The Board suggested that the in,,ais ter should be carefully investigated from Plit, point of view, and that rules be framed lilrbie lug the exemptions as much as was Poss' consistently with national requirements. (J)r. On the proposition of the Chairman .c.` le. Hodgson) the subject was referred to the •KA_A mentary Schools Committee, under the Pr.. dency of Professor Gonner.</p>
        <p>Would Have Interfered with the Courtinfl• Shortly after the coming of Darwin's toOk student from Aberdeen University wen t o his parents' farm. The father, anxious, get news, said, " Weel, Donald, fat aro tedit sayin' at the coalege aboot this new theery we're a' come frae the monkeys?" oak ° "It er nae difference tae me," said Donald, " vibeth my grandfather was a baboon or no." Said the old farmer: "It mebbe rankPs na° .AA al) differ tae you, Donald, but it wad hae rod"— awfu' differ tae yer gran'mither."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>aburrli grills.</head>
        <p>aburrli grills.</p>
        <p>CHURCH AND WAR.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Pastor On Facing Responsibilities. _ The Rev. T. T. James, in the second of his sQIIS of sermons on "The Church and the War," at Wycliffe Church last Sunday evening, 61)04 ujaon "The Church and the Trenches," an expression which, he said, covered in a general way the consideration of the selation- • iP of the Church to the lads who were away. I A" home was not the only place where there were Rltl3B. The home was the place where separation is-a 8 most keenly felt; but the Church felt its • esses and its separations, too. With all these ten who were away in unfamiliar situations e Church had a relationship, and to them 7▪ e -had a responsibility. It was one of the etementary facts of the present situation that tf" Church should see that that relationship °and practical expression and that that "Vonsibility was honestly and loyally faced. The Duty of Carrying On. o,The first thing to be said was that the Church :ruld guard and keep all Christian work that ;,aeir lads were doing betore they left us- -o‘;tllurch, school, and boys' organisations had 4ell enOugh been denuded of those who were 'fleir chief strength and stay. If it were true, wae had been said, that the best in our Churches e among the first to go, it was clear that Church of Christ had found itself early in "Is conflict suffering from the absence of many vho 11 ere prominent and efficient in its work. fact put upon those remaining at home a, elementary duty of carrying on. We must carry on. The Christian Churr:ll za'nust look to this in all its work. Its scholars 44M be taught; its young must be cherished; be Y 8 must be trained; its organisations must et:llan/led. We owe it to the Lord, Whose —.Lurch I 0 those Derfor it is, and N t se who had , at theee we owe *t 1-t"(1 leave their tasks to face danger nafilljait of duty, to see that no position goes goes A a‘nd no task lies neglected and no fire Chureil-T,' in all the organisations of the</p>
        <p>Keep Watch! iii second place the Churches must keep over the untold numbers of her sons who --tre away from her. "This Church," he said, a long list upon her Roll of Honour. We 11st keep watch with them for the living God. th k that binds the Church at home -to Os away t se away must be strongly forged in every Prt to-day. The power of prayer is to be exer- ''sed to its fullest. There are other ways in Vtti You may help these lads. Those who are Vat.'„L,ing to them regularly are rendering great iee- Do not trouble too much if answers (40 a),L come! Do not trouble if answers are short siulple. Remember it is a lot easier for us u aw up our chairs to the table in our, com- a r,table homes and write a letter than it is for ejad to sit in a ditch and answer it. Let the -4ristian Church send her greetings to those mho atid are away, for every message means hope it encouragement and happiness to those whom aelles. 1 see many of you are entertaining ho Your homes the wounded who are back 'n NrsDitkal. It is a gracious thing, for which e n's in other towns and cities will be thank- t, h 7: You from the bottom of their hearts. But th:,snprerae thing we shall not forget is this, IC we are to keep watch in prayer for those taiL are away. The Church that is not doing it I,4ll'°e(l its way. The Christian men and women for° are not doing it are refusing the first and eraost duty of the moment." The Mighty Spiritual Army. th, 8 third point concerned the Church and 144,1,4ati0n. Our security at home had been scd with the life blood of those who would butyl* return. Strange it would be if we did not bt° ourselves the question: "What sort of see„,44ngland ought this to be for which no life 448—,8 to good to be laid on war's destructive What kind of a home should this be of ttsCtand mine for which the bravest and the TraD.:u.,ave spent their strength and blood?" haer9,A'''3' indeed would all this be, and wasted „Are unless it became to us a sacrificial nart:enning and purifying (cur national life, out the wrong and evil in our midst. 'orrcal:4,e church is the Army of the Lord, 11Q88'.1Sed for the Kingdom of God. Its busi- (l4 is to keep alive the soul of the nation. 14. 01l crying evils she has the right and the oer"bi`n speak. At all impurities she must aim veto s"nws. We are here to offer defiance to all until the last stronghold of sin is level the ground. We are here as a great ~44triselve forte, whose end is the victory of 'lor man(' love. The Church must be mobilised --4ristian service and for the salvation of</p>
        <p>societSr. We say that in poetry and we sing it in hymns</p>
        <p>'Like a mighty army Moves the Church of God.' If we do not believe it we shall have to destroy niany of our Christian songs. If we do believe it we shall have to live up to it. I know of no power which is going to sweeten and purify the life of the nation, to redeeur it from its follies and lift it to a higher plane, unless the Church of Jesus Christ shall fulfil the task."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>W</p>
        <p>•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••• • ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••• •• •</p>
        <p>if WOMAN'S WORLD. if</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women Workers in Ships.</p>
        <p>In the shipyards on the Clyde and the Tyne, women are working for their country, side by side with the men, and they have shown such aptitude that hundreds, 'perhaps it would be nearer the truth to say thousands, have become highly-skilled mechanics, for whose work there will be a great demand when the war is over. The ship-women wear trousers, precisely simi- lar to those of the men; over these long over- alls reach below the knees. A cap resembling a sponge bag is worn as a covering for the hair. The colour of the suit is a dull drab. The reason given for adopting masculine clothes was the fact that it is both safer and more comfortable, although not becoming. The authorities of Newcastle forbade the women to wear their working dress in the streets, although the rule is not strictly kept .;r enforced.</p>
        <p>Women in Gas Works. No fewer than two thousand five hundred men employed by one of the biggest London gas companies have joined the King's Forces, and but for the fact that women have come for- ward to fill their places, the company would have been unable to "carry on." Failure to do this would have caused the greatest incon- venience to at least a million helusewives. it is gratifying to find that in every department women show that they have brains, and know how to use them, and are doing the work of the men remarkably well. The lady superin- tendent has shown great discrimination in choosing the applicants for the various depart- ments, and this accounts in part for the suc- cess of the women employes, many of whom are widows of men who have fallen in the war. The woman lamplighter is tlie latest arrival, but the collector of pennies from slot meters, and the driver of the company's Motor .van are now quite old hands. Young women have been trained to repair 'and adjust meters. One im- portant enterprise is the entrance of the woman chemist, who is doing good research and test- ing work in the company's laboratories.</p>
        <p>Ancient and Modern Playthings. The Whitechapel Art Gallery has in former times arranged a series of remarkably interest- ing exhibitions, but not one has proved as fas- cinating to old and young alike, as the play- things gathered from all' the ages, and every clime, and now on view until the end of July. Mr. Edward Lovett's collection of dolls, is the feature .of the exhibition. The most ancient</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>•_•• • • • • •• • • • • • t:. • • • • • 1 4: • • .: 9</head>
        <p>•_•• • • • • •• • • • • • t:. • • • • • 1 4: • • .: 9</p>
        <p>. .:. . •:. • ,ChildrensCorner.• . .:.</p>
        <p>Conducted by UNCLE JACK. ;:*</p>
        <p>4. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .41. 4. .:. .:. 4. -.:. .:. .:. 0 4. .:. .:. 4. .:. .:. 0 .:.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S TRIBUTE TO KITCHENER.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— I think I am right in saying that my in- vitation to you to write your opinion about the late Lord Kitchener has produced the largest number of letters received during the whole history of the Corner. There is no doubt that could he bait express an opinion himself he would like to know that the children of the land thought so well of him: it would please him better than all the other opinions put together. It is a glowing tribute to the memory of a great and good man that you pay this week. The letters, full of praise, if I printed them would more than fill a page of "The Examiner," and I have been .able to give only a few lines from each, but if you read all that I have asked the printers to set you will gee the kind of praise my nephews and nieces have for one who did his best for his country, and whose best was so successful.</p>
        <p>I cannot do less than give two prizes this week; and these I give to 768 Niece Edna Fellows, 44, Bewsey-road, who attends Evelyn-street School; and who has won 'previously; and 787 Nephew Harry Heywood, 8, Matthews-street, who is only nine years of age, and attends the Parochial School. This week I will ask you to write me a letter on</p>
        <p>What I Should Like To Do In My Summer Holidays. ,</p>
        <p>GET NEW MEMBERS FOR THE CORNER!</p>
        <p>Next week I shall open a competition which you will all enjoy very much. I shall offer two prizes for the boys or girls who get the greatest number of new members for the Corner. I want to make my nieces and nephews into 1,000 before the holidays, and I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>doll is four thousand years old, and contem- porary with Moses, for it is to Egypt that we e the oldest specimens ci this class of play- thing. .From Africa, and the Red Indian Com- pounds, come the dolls that played so important a part in religion and magic. Very pathetic are Mr. Lovett's specimens that he calls emergency dolls. These came from the slums and are made of a mutton shank-bone, the sole of an old shoe or clog, and a stiek with a head stuffed with hay, and each is dressed in old rags. The new industry that has sprung up since Germany ceased to sulapiy our children with toys is well represented, as are also Japan and Russia. That Japan will make ,a Lig effort to take the place of the enemy as a producer of cheap toys, is quite evident.</p>
        <p>Petticoats From Narrow Skirts. What to do with narrow summer skirts that are still in good condition has puzzled many women anxious to be economical in war time. A very good idea is to turn them into petti- coats, and this can be done quite successfully if narrow gores are let in on each side to give the necessary width. A good silk dress skirt can be converted into a handsome petticoat with the addition of a few frills, or a ruche at the hem. In order that the petticoat may stand out, a very fine wire should be inserted. An old-fashioned cotton petticoat may be brought up to date by means of a deep silk flounces of Japanese white washing silk, but- toned, or sewn on if preferred. Tussore silk is the very best material for wearing under light summer skirts, and can be had in many beautiful shades to match suits. For hard wear moirette is unsurpassed, whilst everyone acknowledges the many virtues of glace silk in this connection. The Princess petticoat in its many phases, is deservedly one of the most popular of this year's styles.</p>
        <p>The Vogue of the Ribbon Necklet. The present fashions afford many opportuni- ties to the girl or woman with time and clever fingers, of making pretty trifles that give a touch of colour, or a dainty finish to a blouse or frock of a severe type. Odds and ends of ribbons, jewellery,, lace, &amp;c., that before the war would have been thrown away as worth- less, have now assumed considerable value in their 9wner's estimation, •dnd are clevely utilised in .a variety of ways. One of these dainty trifles is the necklet of black or coloured ribbon, or ribbon velvet, but all sorts of broken jewellery, sueh as chains and beads can be used up at home, and original ideas carried out, that always have a charm for the designer. Old-fashioned pear-shaped ear-rings make charming pendants, and pieces of ribbon' about three or four inches long, and a half-inch wide, may be used alternately with the same lengths of chain and beads, a charm- ing necklet resulting.,</p>
        <p>know you will help me to do it. I am giving you a fortnight's notice instead of a week's. Tell all your school friends of the pleasure you get from reading the Corner and being a mem- ber, and ask them to join. Write down their name, address, age and school, and send in your lists next week but one, by Wednesday, July 12th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Your loving, UNCLE JACK. • THE WINNING LETTERS. By Niece Edna Fellows, age 12.</head>
        <p>Your loving, UNCLE JACK. • THE WINNING LETTERS. By Niece Edna Fellows, age 12.</p>
        <p>"Lord Kitchener was everything that could be expected. He was stern and silent, but just. He was devoted to his country, and he put his whole energy into the task of helping his King. It is said that he even asked if, it was possible for him to have his bed in his office, he was so eager to be at his work. Naturally his request was not granted, but it just shows his spirit. We could do with a few Lord Kitcheners now. Kitchener spent p, great deal of his time in Egypt, and the people worshipped him as a white god. When Kitchener sailed for England he told the Egyptians that he would come back to them, and now that he is dead the Egyptians will not believe it, but say he will come back to them. Kitchener was characteristic in his humbleness. He did not like to be praised in public, and, I am sure that he would rather be buried in the ocean than with the other brave and noble men who are buried in St. Paul's Cathedral or West- minster Abbey. In nay opinion, a better and a more hardworking man never breathed, and I hope that the people who criticised him feel ashamed of themselves."</p>
        <p>By Nephew Harry Heywood, aged 9.</p>
        <p>"Lord Kitchener was a great soldier, one of the finest leaders the British Army ever had. I am sure it gave our Tommies ' a great shock when they heard of the sad and sudden end of such a glorious career, and also our allies, who joined with us in our great sorrow. He was known all over the world as K of K, which means Kitchener of Khartoum. If I write all night I could not praise him too much. Good old Kitchener of Khartoum."</p>
        <p>writers may be successful, as they deserve be a little later. From these letters I give the following extracts : Nephew Leo P. Fox, 70, Fox-street (Sacred Heart).—" His wOrks will always live after him, and England will always regret his un- timely end." Nephew Wm. Fox, 70, Fox-street.— " Kitchener's new army may well be proud of him and mourn his loss. He went throug_h many ordeals, but always came out on top." ""</p>
        <p>Niece Lucy Hankinson, 393, .Wellfield-street (St. Barnabas').—" He never wanted to appear in the limelight, such as having his photograph in the paper. He never made a fuss about any- thing, but took everything calmly and quietly. He showed himself not only a soldier but a statesman. Even in his busy life he found time to worship God."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Whittle, 123: Cartwright- street (St. Barnahas').—" Lord Kitchener wa. a sincere patriot and a model military organiser."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Bird, 317, Lovely-Itanl (Arp- ley-street).—" Lord Kitchener was a very eeter- mined man, and when once he set hi.. 3 mind on doing a thing he usually carried it through. and this is why I wish he had lived to see the end of his greatest task—victory for the country he loved so well."</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Pugh, 17, Ship:•street, Brecon.— "It is largely due to his administrative genius and unwearying energy that the country .has been able to create and place in the t eld the armies which are to-day uphaiTng the traditional glories of the Empire." Nephew Jack Briggs, 105; Kimberley-street (Evelyn-street).—" He was a man of honour and steadiness; all the people praised bim for his deeds."</p>
        <p>Nephew Stanley Hulse, 33, Fairclough- avenue (Parochial), who sends me the names of two new members, say that Kitchener "loved his country." Niece Edith Kettle, 52, Parr-street, says Lord Kitchener was the greatest personality of the day, and by his death the country lost one of its best men.</p>
        <p>Nephew Walter Richardson., 6, Harbord- street, says Lord Kitchener's death was a grave loss for England*. Nephew Arthur Williams. 1, Gladstone- street (Heathside School).—" Lord Kitchener was practically the greatest man England ever had, and his death has brought much sorrow on Englana and France. He had the sphinx. like gift of silence, and lived in deeds and not in words. He is one of the best, if not the best organiser of victory in the annals of war- fare."</p>
        <p>Niece Flora H. Overend, 74, Lythgoes-laue.— "He has left a true, worthy and honourable name behind him, which shall never die but always be remembered." Niece Emily Kenwright, 8, Duffey-street.-- " Lord Kitchener is one of our bravest heroes. He was a good soldier, for he- put duty first." Nephew Reggie Jones, 8, Dudley-street (Bea- mont School).—" Lord Kitchener was a very clever lad and he was always carrying a grin. He died while doing his duty." Niece Doris Calvert, 47, Grafton-street (Evelyn-street School).—" We have lost one of our noblest heroes since the time of Nelson and Wellington. England will have much trouble in finding another man 'to fill his place." Nephew Geo. Emery, 69, Lythgoes-lane, one of our new members this week, praises Lord Kitchener for the excellent character he bore throughout his life, and' adds that "his name shall never die for the work he did!"</p>
        <p>Nephew Jack Pickstock, 92, Bramhall-street (St- Barnabae School)..—" Lord Kitchener was the nation's best soldier friend. He was also the King's best friend, and the people's favourite. How did he get so many recruits? Because of his good name among the poor people. He had a good name with all people, rich and poor. He was a good man, and true to his God, King and country." Nephew Frank Carbyshire, 369, WeMeld- street (St. 13arnabas' School).—" Lord Kitchener was a great and noble man, worthy of every praise. He should be an example to everybody, not only in the military life, but i the Church life:"</p>
        <p>Nephew Leo Eastens, 156, Winwick-road.- - " The nation mourns the loss of a great and good man. It also feels that no other man will be worthy to take his place." Nephew Jesse Worrall, 22, Grafton-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Lord Kitchener was great in thinking out most of the things which have done the Empire a lot of good. He carried out his duties as an Englishman should do; he has faced many difficulties, and he has mastered them. I think Kitchener deserved credit, which he always received; we have lost the greatest man in the world."</p>
        <p>Nephew Ernest Darbyshire, 369, Wellfield- street (St. Barnabas' School).—" Lord Kitchener was the finest man England ever possessed. He was a sincere patriot." Nephew J. Ryder, 46, Elizabeth-street.— "Lord Kitchener raised three million men to fight against the enemy, and had put Britain in a position-to achieve victory. His work was one of the most arduous undertakings that was ever laid upon a human being. He was a brave soldier, a great British gentleman, and a mar- vellous organiser, who worked his own way up from the bottom by his own merit. The people called him the man of miracles." Niece Mabel Wright, 68, Winwiek-street</p>
        <p>(Wycliffe School)-.—" Lord Kitchener was a very brave soldier. He did his duty." Niece Ethel Shaw' Sunnyside, Chester New; road (Bolton Council School).—"Lord Kitchener, who did a great many noble deeds for his coun- try, was always honest, industrious, and brave. His loss came as such a shock that no one at first could believe the news, but we,found that it was true that the 'Sea had claimed him for her own."</p>
        <p>Niece Emma Terry, 95, Cartwright-street.— "Both Sing and nation had great faith in him, and it will be hard to find another soldier to take his place.."</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson, 323 Lovely-lane (Wycliffe School).—" Lord Kitchener was born a soldier, and he was bred in the ways of one. It has been of a great mockery in German towns, for a day's holiday is given at any bit of trash, but this time it has been a great 'bit' for us to lose, and I suppose they will have a week. Nearly all men fochis sake enrolled themselves, and there- fore he made us a nation in arms.. As long as the world revolves his name will be of great importance to all the Allied nations."</p>
        <p>Nephew Ernest W. Sankey, 99,. Kimberley- street (St. Barnabas' School).—" Everything he did he did well, and always with a right spirit. When he was told to, do a thing- he did not say 'lt is nearly impossible, and I will never do it,' but he did his best to do it."</p>
        <p>Nephew John Webb, 27, Catherine-street.— "He has served his country well' and richly de- serves his famous name, Kitchener of Khar- toum."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Freaney, 107, Cartwright- street (Sacred Heart School).—"Kitthener was one of England's greatest heroes; I think that without Kitchener we would have lost this war."</p>
        <p>Nephew Jeffery Benion, 164, Lovely-lane (St. Barnabas' Schopl).—" He was a good man and a good general; and we would do well to follow MS example. He loved his men and his men loved him."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harry Risley, 22; Palmlough-avenue (Parochial School).—"Lord Kitchener 4 could understand men of all nations. HO was a man of God and his work was soffliering; Lord Kitchener was the greatest man of war." Niece Isabella Sankey, 99, Kimberley-street. —"Re was very firm in his speeches and made his men love him."</p>
        <p>Niece Mary Molloy, 5, Edward-street (St. Alban's School).—"I think that it he was alive the war would soon be over:"</p>
        <p>Niece Kathleen Smith, 43, Mill-lane (Evelyn- street School).—" Lord Kitchener. was one of England's greatest men that ever used, not only in war time, but in peace time. He loved to work in silence, and without men's. cheers. He was a glorious soldier, yet a finer man. It seemed we could not spare him, but GOd knows best.'</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Howard, 26, Grafton-street (Evelyn-street School).—"Nobody served his King more faithfully than Earl Kitchener, and his death was- a great loss to the British Empire."' Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road.— " It was often said that Lord Kitchener was the 'right man in the right place,' and so he was.'' Nephew Harry Dixon, 19, Nora-street (Parochial School).—" Lord Kitchener was a good man. ?le has done good work in this war, also in the South 'African war."</p>
        <p>Niece Dorothy Wright, 67, Winwick-street, wishes that Lord Kitchener's body could be found, so that we should know where he was buried and be able to erect a monument to his memory.</p>
      </div>
