      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HODGKINSON LTD., BRIDGE STREET, IN Av INGTON.</head>
        <p>HODGKINSON LTD., BRIDGE STREET, IN Av INGTON.</p>
        <p>MUSICAL SERVICE AT BOLD STREET.</p>
        <p>The evening service at the Bold- street Wesleyan Church on Sunday was mainly devoted to the rendering of 'special music. The singing was of a praise- worthy and beautiful character. "God is a Spirit" was sung by a quartet consisting of Mrs. Warburton, Miss Plumpton, and Messrs. Mills and Warburton. Miss E. Taylor sang a solo, "Come unto Me," and Mr. E. H. Pearce gave "Arm, arm, ye brave." Three anthems were splendidly rendered by the choir, viz., "0 how amiable" (Faning), "Lovely apbear " (Gounod) and "0 Father Whose Almighty Power" (Handel). Miss Whitmore sang a verse of a special war hymn. A chorus, "The Snow " (Elgar), was given by female voices. The ser- vice terminated with the singing of the National Anthem. Mr. F. Hickman ably pre- sided at the organ. The Rev. Percy meson was the preacher, and delivered an address on "Vanity Fair."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WyCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>WyCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Farrar presided over the meeting on Sunday, and spoke upon the catholicity of the Brotherhood. All were welcome, he said. No denomination was mentioned. All were for Christ. Rev. T. T. James spoke upon what the P.S.A. stood for—not simply to spend a pleasant hour, which was good, but to learn the deeper meaning of fellowship with Christ. The improvement of character, and one's life in general to help to make happy homes, and to imitate the life of Christ. Mr. Scholey, the secretary of Wycliffe Church Brotherhood, Sheffield, was present, and extended the greet- ings from Sheffield Brotherhood. He hoped that they, with all Brotherhoods, would prepare for giving our absent brethren, on their return, a most earnest and loving welcome. Mr. Years- ley read the lesson. The Chairman led the prayer. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. The Chairman expressed the hope that they would give a hearty welcome to Captain Mathams, the speaker for to-morrow '(Sunday).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>(*ROL \DS-WALKER.</p>
        <p>The wedding took place at the Parish Church, Warrington, last Saturday, of Sapper W. N. Grounds, R.E., son of Mr. and Mrs. Grounds, of the School House, Fearnhead, and Miss Bertha Walker, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Henry Walker, 21, Marsh-street, Warrington. The Rector performed the ceremony, and Sergeant C. H. Walker, of the South Lancashires, who has seen service in France, officiated as best man. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a fawn costume, with hat to match, and had as her bridesmaid Miss Ethel Grounds, the bridegroom's sister. A reception was held at the School House, Fearnhead, the guests including Mr. and Mrs. Henry Walker, Sergeant C. H. and Mrs. Walker, Mr. and Mrs. Vernon (of Barrow), and Mr. and Mrs. Allen (Orford). The bridegroom has seen service in Gallipoli. He was wounded in three places, and has been in hospitals in England since last December. He rejoins his regiment on Thurs- day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FREE SITES AND EXEMPTION FROM TAX.</head>
        <p>FREE SITES AND EXEMPTION FROM TAX.</p>
        <p>Quebec's Offer To Engineers, Ship-</p>
        <p>builders And Others.</p>
        <p>We have all heard of the land for emigrants, but in these strenuous times of overworked 'n- dustries it is strange to hear of the offer of free sites, exemption from tax and other in- ducements to shipbuilders, engineers and other firms to lay down plant. Three rivers, Quebec, which has a population of over 20,000, is situated on the North Shore of the St. Law- rence, midway between Montreal and Quebec, the distance being 75 miles each way. The municipality are willing to send plans and all details to bona-fide inquiries. The wharves are more than two miles in extent and the water has a depth of over 50ft., with a tide water rise of from 12in. to 14in. Railway communication is provided by the C.P.R., St. Maurice Valley and the Grand Trunk Railway Companies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JULY 1, 1916.</p>
