      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LAST WEEK'S PRIZE.</head>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S PRIZE.</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Freaney, 107, Cartwright- street, who won last week's prize, writes to say he has read part of the book, which is very nice, and one he will take care of in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>"THE WOMAN AT HOME."</head>
        <p>"THE WOMAN AT HOME."</p>
        <p>The Special Russian Number forming the July issue of "The Woman at Home" and "Girls' Realm," is certain to be much appreciated in thousands of homes. It in- cludes an absorbing article by Lady Mary cn the Russian Royal Family, with several illus- trations from photographs: Readers are intro- duced to the simplicity of the homely life of the Tsar and Tsarina, and it is with interest we learn that "the Grand Duchess Marie—the prettiest of the Tsar's daughters—who was sweet seventeen on the 26th of June, is the likeliest of brides among Russian Grand Duchesses for the Prince of Wales."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>HOME AND HEALTH NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME AND HEALTH NOTES.</p>
        <p>Plain Short Crust.</p>
        <p>Put 2oz. of butter into a saucepan; add two tablespoonfuls of cold water, and make it boil. Take Ib. of flour, heat it in the oven, and when hot mix it with a knife with 2oz. of cold butter; then pour the melted butter and water into it, and mix it altogether. Roll it out once, put it over the fruit, and bake immediately. Two lumps of sugar may be added to the boiling water and butter.</p>
        <p>Drugging And Its Dangers. At an inquest on Ellen Denyer, aged seventeen months, at Southwark, the mother stated that she was ill in bed, and her doctor placed two blue tabloids of perchloride of mercury on the window ledge behind the head of the bed. Next morning she observed that the baby's lips were blue, and it was found that the child had swal- lowed both the tabloids.—Dr. Stratton said that each tabloid contained 81 grains of mercury, and three grains was a fatal dose. The jury re- turned a verdict of "Death by misadventure." Irish. Potato Cake.</p>
        <p>Half the success of the cake depends on the potatoes. They must be floury and freshly boiled. Mash them into a smooth paste with a potato-bruiser; then mix with them about the same quantity of flour and a good pinch of salt, and knead them as bakers do, mixing flour in this way from time to time until it is of smooth, firm consistency, but not too solid (a little experience soon teaches one to know just when the dough is right, as some potatoes take more flour, some Jess}. Then roll them out on a well-floured board, and cut into whatever shapes you please. Bake on a girdle until of a rich golden-brown on each side, turning the cakes only once when baking. They are delicious eaten hot, sprit open, and well buttered; but they are almost equally good, especially in summer, when cold, with plenty of butter. A third way of eating these cakes is to allow them to get cold, and then split them and fry quickly in boiling lard or butter. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>How To Reduce Your Meat Bill!</head>
        <p>How To Reduce Your Meat Bill!</p>
        <p>Make delicious and nutritious puddings with apples, figs, dates, golden syrup, jam, SEc., and Shredded ATORA Beef Suet. Always ready for use and saves all the trouble of chopping. Ask your grocer for it; refuse substitutes. Sold in 11b. cartons mid. and Slb. cartons 6d.</p>
        <p>iiP-41r"</p>
      </div>
