      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SUMMER</head>
        <p>SUMMER</p>
        <p>SALE</p>
        <p>STOOK</p>
        <p>DR ERY</p>
        <p>COMMENCE S</p>
        <p>MONDAY NEXT, JULY 3rd.</p>
        <p>NO CATALOGUES</p>
        <p>DEANSGATE, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>:(5: I.°.p. :6*.\******** •C,</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Girls' Guild. In connection with the above Guild, a rum- mage sale was held on Friday evening last week in the Latchford Baptist Schoolroom, in aid of the funds of the Y.M.C.A. huts and the British Red Cross Society motor ambulance. The sale proved very successful, the handsome sum of nearly £l9 being realised. £lO of the surplus has been sent to the Y.M.C.A., and ,28 to the British Red Cross Society. In view of the recent appeal of the Y.M.C.A., this highly satisfactory effort was very opportune. The committee of the Guild tender their sincere thanks to all friends who so generously contributed to the success of the sale.</p>
        <p>"Where Are Our Dead Heroes ? "</p>
        <p>This question was dealt with on Sunday at the meetings of the local Spiritualist Society, when good congregations assembled to hear Mr. Tom Tyrrell, the well-known clairvoyant medium from Blackburn, in the evening, the hall being crowded. A large number .-,f descriptions of spirit forms were given ,at both services, in all cases accompanied by full namcs and other details which secured recognition, and amongst them were some of our boys who have been slain in the present terrible struggle. The service on Monday evening was conducted by Mrs. Anderson, of Manchester, who gave good clairvoyance to a large audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916. GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916. GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>BIRTIIS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Forty years' service at the feet of our Town Councillors has worn holes in the carpet in No. 1 Committee Room at the Town Hall, and though, perhaps, in other tithes it would be retired from active duty, the veteran is to be our stand-by (and standing place) until the war is over and prosperity. beams on us.</p>
        <p>To go through with us, however, it has had to be doctored, and upholsterers have been busy this week patching it up.</p>
        <p>Where the floor has become bald is directly below the edges of the table where our wise and faithful Councillors sit. The size of the holes in the carpet varies. In places where the hearts of the Councillors have sunk into their boots, the extra pressure and irritation on the carpet has had a great effect.</p>
        <p>The ladies who sometimes take tea with the- Diaybr on festive occasions will be interested to know that round the ,outskirts of the room where they usually stand the carpet, looks almost as good as new. That's because they are not so heavy-footed. On, festive occasions hearts don't sink into boots.</p>
        <p>One of my five colleagues who have been called away by the demands of the war has sent me what he calls "a few lines" from the Front.</p>
        <p>The lines are "few" but full. He could write a• page of "The Examiner" upon he least provocation, but now contents himself with:—</p>
        <p>"Am quite well and happy. Things are not at all bad out here. We shall all be back ue- fore November, so keep the home fires burn- ing?"</p>
        <p>That sounds like another Jutland "victory" or the Junkers!</p>
        <p>-Our looal poet, Alderman Bennett, was about to quote Longfellow at the Education Committee meeting, on Monday, and, fearing he would not he able to complete it, appealed to Councillor ITen.hall to assist him if he floundered.</p>
        <p>As the Alderman began, he looked through the ceiling Heavenwards, saw the vision, and was telling us all about it when it vanished. His quotation came to a full stop at a comma. The vision had gone from his view, evidently be- hind his back, for Councillor Ilenshall, who was sitting at his rear, saw it, and resumed the quotation, Alderman Bennett making up the duet.</p>
        <p>If Alderman Bennett is our local Poet, then, surely, Councillor Ilenshall may be described as our local. Bartlett.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee think .that all muni-</p>
        <p>tion workers who have not been vaccinated within the last ten years should be vaccinated. They have told the Local Government Board so, and have offered to provide facilities for doing the work gradually, so as to cause as little inconvenience as possible.</p>
        <p>Should smallpox occur "a serious condition might arise," and it is against this contingency that the Medical Officer proposes to stir betimes.</p>
        <p>The Markets Committee will recommend the Council next Tuesday night to appoint Mr. W. H. Wallington Market Superintendent and Chief Inspector of Weights and Measures at a salary. of £2OO a year, rising by £l.O yearly in- crements to £.250.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington is the natural successor to his old chief, and his appointment will give universal satisfaction.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Malone, the Markets Inspector,,is like- wise recommended for the post of Deputy Superintendent of the Markets and first 'Assist- ant Inspector of Weights and Measures.</p>
        <p>When John Richard Millar told Detective Anderson that as "his courage failed him" when required to report for military service, hp probably forgot that it required a great de:11 of courage to make the admission. Any old yarn would, have done well.. Ho should make a good sailor.. I'l •</p>
        <p>Last year the gross profit of the Gas Depart- ment was £ll,BOO and the net profit £4,606. 'There are features in the annual report to he presented to the Council on Tuesday in respect of which most interesting explanations might be made. . •</p>
        <p>The Water Department had a gross profit of 19,794 and a net loss of £724, the amount •!harged against net revenue . account for loan charges having been £20,518.</p>
        <p>The Council will be requested to ask the Government to take steps to provide such employment distinct from charity as will s?cure a living wage for every returned soldier at till close of the war.</p>
        <p>They will also be asked to call upon the Government to intern all enemy alien subjects resident in this country because of the clangor they are while they remain at liberty.</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>HUNDRED OUT-CAST</head>
        <p>A HUNDRED OUT-CAST</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS.</p>
        <p>Canon MORLEY STEVENSON, at the Education Committee on Monday night, drew attention to a state of affairs that is not very creditable to Wariington. Briefly, he said that this year for the sixty places vacant at the Secondary School there had been 168 applications, so that 'we., had a lack of accommoda,</p>
        <p>tion for those desiring to _continue their studies amounting to 108 places. Those figures are more eloquent of issues upon Which they are silent than of the bare facts they reveal. The Warrington Secondary School is a model of its kind. The reputation for the excellence of its teaching that it has built up of late is the direct cause of this excessive run upon its resources. But can anyone pretend that a town—a progres- sive, manufacturing town like Warrington— with a population of between 70,000 and 80,000 inhabitants, provides adequate educational facilities, when its puts sixty Secondary School places a year at the disposal of those inhabit- ants? Of course, we are not losing sight of the fact that the town is rapidly providing a set of fine schools for elementary instruction; but that is no defence to the charge of short- comings in the means of continuing the in- struction of children into the period of life when they begin to understand the true mean- ing of education. It is really an aggravation of the offence that we prepare so imany Mill- dren to be educated- and then deliberately negative most of the good work we have done by denying the children the facilities to pro- ceed along• the path, upon which we have placed theni. Hitherto parents have been to blame for not allowing their children to stay at school beyond the minimum period the State demands, so that they shall not remain in absolute ignorance. Only this week Sir</p>
        <p>HENRY HIBBERT, lamenting the want of' secondary education, particularly on the sci- entific side, said the fault lies not with the Board of Education, or the education authori- ties, but "with the people who do not take advantage of the magnificent opportunities offertd in most of the counties" However true this may have been in the past, or in other places, it is not so, and has not been so for years, in Warrington, where the " people " are clearly in advance of the education authority. The numl er of places availatle is</p>
        <p>not much more than a third of the numter of Its no are anxious to occupy them. uesides, there would doubtless he more candi- dates but for what appears to many the hope- less task of competing for a few places that have to be shared among so many.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON, commenting unon the figures to which Canon STEVENSON referred, said it was the greatest grief of the School Management Committee that they had not been aVie to come to an arrangement with the governors of the Grammar School, under which their new school could have been utilised as a secondary school for 300 boys of the town, while the present Secondary School could have been made availaLle for 300 girls. It is useless longing for that which you cannot get; and perhaps it is as well that the Grammar School should be allowed to continue in its own par- ticular groove and to maintain its own par- ticular and honourable traditions. What the Education Committee have to do is to meet, and meet as early as possible, the demand for that c!ass of education that can only keep England commercially in the forefront of the ' nations. We now accommodate about 200 , higher education students, the yearly vacan- cies, numbering about 60, being filled up by 30 girls and 30 boys from the elementary Schools. Surely that is mere tinkering with a vitally important question, and is just the kind of thing to make the Germans smile at the easy task they will have after the war in beat- ing us in that still more important conflict in trade and commerce that will follow the con- flict in arms.</p>
        <p>If it is not the lack of boys and girls willing to continue their studies that makes second- ary education languish, it is certainly not the lack of money necessary to provide the in- struction. It was lamented on Monday night that we could do nothing now but make plans for taking action after the war, because money is not available for anything but gunpowder. But there was a period before the war when it was as difficult as now to get money to spend on secondary or any education. It has been proved that we have an endless supply of money—that, as Alderman BENNETT said, we</p>
        <p>can spend £5,000,000 a day on war, and yet, in peace time, boggle at an infinitesimal sum needed for education. We can only hope that the war will have opened our eyes to essential things in other than the military sphere, and that our education authorities will not be un- duly hampered by financial considerations while laying their plans for the larger war that lies beyond the armed struggle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
