      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CAMEOS.</head>
        <p>CAMEOS.</p>
        <p>By The Schoolmaster.</p>
        <p>Laugh Wisely, Not Too Well. Laughter exercises its own set of muscles; it is also one of life's matchless polishes. The two girls had stayed behind to" lock up." " 1 said, "and Frances, you have aad a silly fit all this afternoon—get rid of it to- night: it's of no use to anyone." They looked at each other, then at inc. "Please, Sir," said Nellie, "have you ever felt as if you could just laugh like anything?" Then I laughed; so did they. "Of course I have," I said, "but there are times and places to laugh, and school hasn't to be one long laugh at any time." They seemed somewhat impressed—somewhat Teachers are human; Nellie's question was not a fair one.</p>
        <p>Sympathetic Neves.</p>
        <p>Sensory nerves, motor nerves: I have stadic:. physiology, and thr.: understood them: but I don't. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ The boy came out to „..n.•:_ished. I leaned him over my knee: gave him a light stroke with the cane. Then I wondered where the smart was: he went away blowing his hands. Have you ever felt as if you could just laugh like anything? That is the time to show the class your self-control. The " crows-feet " gathered: my mouth corners twinkled: I took out .niy handkerchief: wiped my nose: and retained my dignity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>When to Hurry.</head>
        <p>When to Hurry.</p>
        <p>In rendering" first-aid," hasten slowly. Life consists of first-aids: be wise, and hasten slowly. The class copied the extract from the black- board: I walked round. " Cissie," I said, "you are not giving proper time to your writ- ing. There's only One thing that should be done in a hurry." I held up an index finger zo emphasise the one. Another walk round—similar complaint— similar remark. The class was interested. Yet another perambulation—similar result. The class was more interested. " Please, Sir," ventured Cissie, "What is it we should do in a hurry?"—" Catching fleas, said I; "if you don't hurry, you don't catch them." The class smiled—audibly. Thereafter, at need, I rapped on the desk: held up one finger: the semaphore system worked admirably.</p>
        <p>Warrington Agricultural Show. Considerable interest attaches to the forth- coming twenty-seventh annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society, which will be held on Thursday, August 3rd, in the Brewery Field, Wilderspool. A number of valuable prizes, cups and medals for Pedigree Shires and dairy cattle are offered for competi- tion. Monday, July 24th,- is the last day for entries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EDUCATIONAL LADDER.</head>
        <p>EDUCATIONAL LADDER.</p>
        <p>108 Scholars Cannot Get On</p>
        <p>Next Rung.</p>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL SHORT OF PLACES.</p>
        <p>Year after year many more children than can be accommodated express their desire to enter the Secondary School. This fact was brought vividly before the Education Committee on Monday, and pleas for a development of secondary education were made by Canon Morley Stevenson, Mr. John Upson, and Alderman Bennett. In submitting the minutes of the School Management Committee, Canon Stevenson pointed out that the result of the examination recently held for entrance and free admission to the Secondary School had been submitted, and the provisional list of qualified candidates —3O boys and 30 girls—was approved. But, added the speaker, there were no fewer than 108 others who wanted to take advantage of the Secondary School, and had to be denied because of the lack of accommodation. That was a fact which would have to engag.o the very, earnest consideration of the committee before long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DEMAND CANNOT BE MET.</head>
        <p>DEMAND CANNOT BE MET.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'Upson, emphasising the Canon's appeal for the consideration of this matter, remarked that those who had sat on the School .Manage- ment Committee would have noticed how, year after year, there was a growing appreciation of Secondary School education in the borough, and how the number of applicants had increased. It was a cause of very deep regret to them that they were unable to meet the demand for places, for it was a great pity that generation after generation of children should pass into life without the opportunity of secondary educa- tion which their parents 'desired Ahem to have. It appeared to him that the greatest need of the Education Committee at the present time was to provide additional accommodation for secondary school education. It was a very great pity that owing to causes over which the committee had no control they were not able to utilise as they would like to have done, by co-operation with the Grammar School, their new, building. They ought to have a Secondary School there for 300 boys and another one for girls for an equal number. That they could not do that was one of the great griefs of the School Management Committee. He knew that now was a time ,when they could not extend, but, unless they pressed that great subject on the mind of the public immediately, it would be shelved when the war was over, because of so many other questions rising for solution. What he particularly desiied at present was for the Education Authority to bear in mind that they must act speedily if they were to do justice to those who were rising up in their midst. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WAR TIME PROVED MONEY CAN BE RAISED. • • .</head>
        <p>WAR TIME PROVED MONEY CAN BE RAISED. • • .</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett described it as being noth- ing less than lamentable- that they were not able to make adequate provision, and he regretted that, even apart from the war, they had not been as active and enthusiastic as they ought to have been. It was highly desir- able that they should sow the seed and prepare the ground for the effective achievement of any scheme which might be proposed. The ideal scheme would be to balance the Grammar School by the creation of a similar school for girls, still leaving the Secondary School as a mixed school. But that was a question of policy. . It was obviously not the time to launch out now in such a-policy at the present time, when the Government had prohibited the expenditure of public money. But when the war was- over they would have a different esti- mate of all values and capacity to spend. Whatever else the war had done it had shown that the country could produce millions in un- limited quantity for purposes of destruction and, therefore, they ought not to be par- simonious on what they spent on education. Secondary education was of extreme import- ance, he concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LOCAL CASUALTIES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL CASUALTIES.</p>
        <p>In the casualty lists published during the week there have appeared tha. following local names:—</p>
        <p>Killed. Lancashire Fusiliers.—J. Collins 23169. South Lancashires.—G. Plumpton 3019, A Pritchard 26048.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Wounded.</head>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>Royal Field Artillery.—J. G. Browning 122672. King's Royal Rifle Corps.—R. J. Smart 1200. South Lancashire Regiment.--D. Mullen 11947, Royal Scots Fusiliers.—Lance-Corporal S. Mould 19612.</p>
        <p>Rifle Brigade.—D. Anderson 2283. Shropshire Light Infantry.—Corporal J. J. Birch 12183.</p>
