        <p>As it is difficult for us to trace some of the casualties in the lists we should appreciate the assistance of friends and relatives. We insert the fullest information and photographs of local men on being supplied.</p>
        <p>LORD KITCHENER'S WILL.</p>
        <p>Earl Kitchener of Khartoum left estate to the gross value of £171,421, with net personalty £145,586. _ _</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Fitzgerald, to whom •a bequest of a large tract of land in East Africa was made, went down in the Hampshire with Lord Kitchener.</p>
        <p>KITCHENER OF KIP `.. OUNI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>London, 6th</head>
        <p>London, 6th</p>
        <p>We gathered proudly at the festive board In the proud heart of London, fain to hear From silver lips a trumpet word of cheer; But, even as the blood-red wine was poured ' Into the glasses, and our fancy soared On wings of hope, a leaden whisper drear Was passed from lip to lip, and icy fear Crept in by stealth, and smote us like a sword.</p>
        <p>A sudden silence froze our chattering tongues; A sudden shadow clouded all our eyes: " Kitchener is drowned. In vain," the rumour sped, We searched for him; his soul belongs To England and to heaven; his body lies Where the great eeep hides England's noble dead.'"</p>
        <p>Was it icr this that yonder in Khartoum • He toilcf! ;Le Mandi's might and 'backward</p>
        <p>Toiled His :,I:ziry tribes, then brought them to the</p>
        <p>-fold Of licther England, whose great heart hath</p>
        <p>room For ail tip world? For this that on the loom Of War he wove, with strands of purest gold, A pact of peace between two races old, And bound them to us fill the day of doom?</p>
        <p>Was it for this that, answering to his word, In serried millions, England's children sprang To arms, and kept the Kaiser's hosts at bay? For this he waited, his great hopes deferred, Unmoved amid the universal clang Of battle, till the dawning of "The Day"?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>India bewails, him; Egypt mourns his loss; A continent of comrades shares our pain; Great Russia calls for his great help in vain; FroM northern snowEi and 4neath the Southern</p>
        <p>Cross Men turn wet eyes, accounting all things dross In this dread hour when, gloating -o'er the</p>
        <p>slain, The ankry waters of the impetuous main Round the far Orcades their white arms toss.</p>
        <p>God must have willed it so. His task was done. His works do follow him. What if the mine Leagued with the malice of the hungry sea? His spirit lives, and, when the fight is won, "Wherever the bright sun of heaven shall shine, His honour and • the greatness of his name shall be."</p>
        <p>ARTUI:E. Dr.27NETT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>_</p>
        <p>C 4 L INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's School's New Manager. The Education Committee on Monday, decided to recommend Mr. W. E. Clegg as representative manager of St. Peter's School, in place of Mr. J. Jarman, who has resigned. '</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices. At Wednesday's Warrington Market the price of wheat ruled at 6s. 9d. to 6s. 104 d. pr 701 b., and 4s. 3d. to 4s. 4id. per 451 b. was realised for oats. Flour realised 335. to 375. per 2401 b., 2s. to 2s. 3d. per 201 b. being the price of thirds. The price of bran and other olials was is. 3d. to is. sd. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>Progress at Cottage Homes. Mr. Elias, Local Government Board Inspec- tor, reported to the Warrington Board cf Guardians on Wednesday, that he was pleased with the results of his. rece,it inspection of the Padgate Cottage Homes, which were in good order. There were distinct signs of progress about the institution, he said.</p>
        <p>• "In England's Name."'. The hundred and seventy-third anniversary of the birth of our great and patriotic local poetess was not allowed to pass unnoticed in this year of war. The Warrington Society sent a wreath, as usual, which was suspended over the tablet in Dial-street, and bore the follow- ing inscription:— Anna Letitia Aikin. - Born 20th June, 1743.</p>
        <p>"In England's name, enforced with loftiest tone, Our duty,—and too well fulfilled her own."</p>
        <p>Secondary School 01(1 Boy's Success</p>
        <p>At University.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Mason, an -old -scholar of the Secondary School, " who holds the Warrington University Scholarship and the Senior Dauntesey Medical Scholarship, has been awarded the Professor Tom Jones' Exhibition in Anatomy by the University of Man- chester. We understand that after his second M. 8., B.Ch. examination in December next, Mr. Mason proposes accepting a commission in the Royal Navy as Surgeon-Probationer. Mr. Mason is It son of the headmaster at Beamont Council School..</p>
        <p>Brewers' £620 Gift. Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. Ltd., WE! subscribed the sum of £620, being 'the cost of one car and its maintenance: for the first three months in France, to the funds of the British Brewers' Ambulance Convoy.</p>
        <p>Citizens' Temperance League. An open-air meeting is announced for next Friday evening in Bewsey-street. The chair will be taken by the Rev. A. J. Morton, of Padgate, and an address will be delivered by Mr. E. Boreland, of Liverpool.</p>
        <p>To-morrow's Park Band. The Pioneer Band will play the following selections of music in• the Park to-morrow (Sun- day) at 3.ls:—Hymn, "Nearer, my God, t° Thee" (Dykes) ; quick march, " Queensbury (Bailey); grand selection, " Herold " (Round); overture, "The Silver Star" (Greenwood); descriptive glee, " Hohenlinden " (T. Cook); fan- tasia, "Enchantment" (Rippin); "God save the King." Robert Smith, conductor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Women Eager, But Lack Stamina.</head>
        <p>Women Eager, But Lack Stamina.</p>
        <p>There is a steady call of men, to the Colours, and tanners feel they have about reached the limit if they are to keep up with anything like the demand for leather made by the Govern- ment, says the Warrington correspondent of the "Leather World.'s Women workers are eager and willing, but they have not got the physique or stamina for tanyard work. Still, if the country demands it, they will have to be utilised, and the business carried on with further makeshifts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Emmanuel Church.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Church.</p>
        <p>The Sunday School anniversary in connection with this church was held last Sunday in. the Church, Bold-street. There were good attend- ances at all the services. At the morning ser- vice the choir rendered very effectively the anthem 4.` Out of the depths," and in the evening the anthem "0 gladsome light" (Sullivan). Miss Dora Bibby sang the hymn "Save us, Holy Jesus," very sweetly, assisted by tile choir. The Curitte-in-charge, the Rev. F. L. Grosvenor, was the preacher, and in the evening gave for his subject "The weakness of a strong man." Mr. J. W. Jowett very ably presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Wood's College Successes.</head>
        <p>Wood's College Successes.</p>
        <p>The following students have been awarded certificates in connection with an examination held on the 18th May at the above institution, under the auspices of the National Shorthana Association (Incorporated) : Elementary Theory: L. J. Higgs, G. James, G. M. Gillow, N. Williamson, V. Taylor, B. Booth, E. Barnes, R. Leach, D. M. Rathbone, G. Prosser, I. Booth (Dist.), A. Lythgoe, A. Pye (Dist.), D. Richards, J. Atherton, R. Wilson. Int. Theory.: I. M. Lyon, H. Booth, H. Mer- rington, B. Parsonage, A. Dykes, A. Rough - sedge, E. Withenshaw, I. Cooper. Fifty words per minute: W. Arrowsmith. Sixty words per minute: M. L. Smethurst, H. Shirtcliff, P. Darbyshire. Seventy words per minute: B. Charnock, K. Eddowes. Ninety words per minute: E. Simkin. ' One hundred words Per minute: W. Hughes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Walking Day in Detachments.</head>
        <p>Walking Day in Detachments.</p>
        <p>Had Walking Day been held yesterday, as at first arranged, it would have been robbed of considerable amount of pageanty by the otnw.r.s which fell during the morning, althcugb later in the day the weather was all that could have been hoped for. The children attending the Sunday Schools. of the Church of England, Non- conformist and Roman Catholic Schools—all denominations in fact—are to-day (Saturday) to enjoy their treat, most of them proceeding to fields without meeting at the schools before- hand. Tea is to be served and sports held much as usual. We hope fine weather will be. in attendance. The scholars at the Roman Catholic Schools at St. Mary's, St. Alban's ancl St. Benedict's are to share a field at Padgate. On Sunday several of the schools of all denominations are to walk round the district in processions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>"ALL WOMEN" REVUE.</head>
        <p>"ALL WOMEN" REVUE.</p>
        <p>Lady Advance Agent On The Company And Archibald. "And how is the sucking pig, with the red, white and blue ribbons, getting along?" one asked Miss Shannon-Clyndes, as she settled down in an "Examiner" Office chair to speak about the "All Women "-revue which is coining to the Royal Court Theatre next week, and of which she is the advance agent. "All right, than,' " retilrn€ll. "but has retired from its stage ,r2.ree-,. the show, having grown cumberso;:o.. sr.:. • You cannot keep down a pig's weight and s' on gin and pickles as if it were a jockey. But we've got another one to take its place. We've educated him up to stage standard and calle4 ! lehibald. He is a dear little thing. It Ir.ere not of the sex he is we should call hint file Porky Venus '—he looks so lovely with nis ribbons on. He is not an old performer. I've just left him not far away. He is at pre- sent living a life of ease and luxury. For several weeks young Archie has shown decided histrionic ability. His grunts have been most dramatic and he has paced his sty in quite a tragic manner. But he will cheer up when he gets into revue, for ha is going to show his skill as a mirth and merriment maker. It is to be his debut night, so good luck to him ?" That pig has a literary tail. Till the Men Corm Back. Miss Shannon-Clyndes is the first lady to be- come a theatrical advance agent. When gr. Kerne started the "All Women" revue the only one connected with it who was a man was the advance agent. He has now been deposed, and our visitor's installation has been the flu- ishing touch to the "All Women" idea. Thef are now really ALL wommi—those behind the stage as well as on it. They are all "esses”-, company manageress, musical directress, au° stage manageress included. "We ladies," added Miss S—C, "are 'carrying on' until the men come back. We are showing the public how to do things. The revue we stage is one of the best, and Warrington audiences will see it ne London audiences have seen it this week. It is absolutely the only show of its kind on the road. The cast is headed by Miss Mona Magnet —a delightful revue artiste; in fact, she is 011 of the most interesting and temperamental vaudeville actresses on the London stage. Ailss Gracie Vicar, who used to be with Fred Einue„i in A Sister to assist 'er,' is coming and she.ti make you laugh as much as the little pig squeal if things don't please ,him. The scenes are beautiful and topical and everything if; quite as good as men could make it." Good business should be done at the Raul Court next week. Miss S—C, who is a daughter of the famous actor, J. H. Clyndes, has cer- tainly laid the foundation for it. She is all admirable advance agent, charming and win' ning. . . . She has even persuaded us 0 write these paragraphs!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>"THE DIAMOND FROM THE SKl''''</head>
        <p>"THE DIAMOND FROM THE SKl''''</p>
        <p>£2,000 Prize Story In Pictures.</p>
        <p>A great serial prize story is being shown at three Warrington picture houses—The Grand, Bridge Foot; The Pavilion, Sankey Green; and the Central, Sanker street. "The Diamond from the Sky"' for its author a prize of £2,000. It is fu' of dramatic interest, the incidents being sensational and masterful to a degree.</p>
        <p>As an aid to full appreciation of the P,ic" tures, "The Examiner is publishing tue story in full week by week. Every Satur- day we publish the instalment of the re- markable plot that is revealed by ill! Pictures in the following week at the "4'; first named houses. We also publish eve„% week a summary, of the story from LP"; beginning to date, so that any patron el any of the houses may know by reading, "The Examiner" exactly the history cn the pictures he is going to see. the "Examiner" readers who patronise houses. mentioned should make this ita' portant fact known. to their friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Civil Service Clerkships.</head>
        <p>Civil Service Clerkships.</p>
        <p>The Civil Sertice Commissioners announce than an open competitive examination for situations as established clerk to surveyors of taxes will be held in October next, commencing o the 31st of that month. Candidates are required to be between 16 and 17 (not 16 and 18) on the Ist November, 1916. No extension of ago (unless under the general regulations) is allowed. Forms of application will probably be ready early in July, and will then .be sent in response to requests by letter, addressed to the Secretary, Civil Service Commission, Bur- lington Gardens, London, W. P. and K. Employes' Charity Fund.</p>
        <p>Through the raising of £lO4 12s. for the Pear- son and Knowles Coal and Iron Company's employes' charity fund during the last six months, a record has been established. It reflects great credit upon the workmen, and gives every encouragement to the collectors. The disbursement of the money is to be as follows :—Warrington Infirmary, £72; Nursing Assmiation, £l5; Manchester General Infirmary, £3 35.; Manchester Eye Hospitali, £3 35.; Prisoners of War Fund, £3 35.; Tobacco Fund, £3 35.; and Blinded Soldiers and Sailors Hostel, 125.</p>
        <p>Exchequer Ponds in the Schools. It was mentioned at the Education Com- mittee's meeting on Monday that a circular letter from the Board of Education had been received asking, on behalf of the National War Savings Committee, for the assistance of the local Education Authority in ,making known through the public elementary schools the in- creased facilities that now exist for saving. The Director had been authorised to make arrange- ments for the distribution of the leaflets referred</p>
        <p>to in the Board's letter. The Rev. Canon Stev- enson said they would be pleased to know that the matter had been 'very warmly taken up in the school. At the Secondary School some of 'rehequer bonds were to be given as prizes.</p>
        <p>,L.Atation from Fellow-Workmen.</p>
        <p>, , The workmen employed at Mersey Mills spent I a, very enjoyable afternoon on Saturday, the occasion being the presentation. of a very hand- some silver rose bowl to Mr. H. Lawton on his retiring from the firm, where he had been em- ployed for 32 years, during which time he won the esteem of his fellow workmen. In making the presentation, Mr. P. Skelhorn referred to the loss which they as workmen had sustained by his retirement, but hoped that he and Mrs. Lawton would live long to enjoy that friendship, which they so well deserved. Mr. H. Lawton, in reply, said it had always been his ambition to encourage the spirit of brotherly love which should exist among all workmen, and, in being given that present, it showed to him that his labour had not been in vain. Mr. G. Millingtor Mr. Gandy, Mr. Challoner, and Mr. Hulse :..so spoke in very appropriate terms of the goo.i ing which existed and which they hoped -- - continue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Change in Management )1 1/ Schools.</head>
        <p>Change in Management )1 1/ Schools.</p>
        <p>A change in the management f tire Roman Catholic Schools was approved by the bducation Committee on Monday. At a meeting of the General Purposes Con,d,,ttze correspondence was submitted from t'l,! inaltagsrs of St. Alban's, St. Benedict's, ana St. Mary's grouped Roman Catholic Schools, from which it was noted that the trustees of the schools named had decided that the group system hitherto in vogue be dis- continued, and that separate managers for each school be appointed; also that in place of the Revs. T. A. Hind, G. C. Jackson and J. P. Whittle, and Mr. J. Smith, whose office as oundation managers of the grouped schools had, been discontinued, the following had been appointed foundation managers of the respec- tive schools: St. Alban's : Rev. Herman Cham- berlain, Rev. Joseph Dawson, Rev. J. P. Whittle, Mr. John Smith; St. Benedict's : Mr. Robert Bloomfield, Rev. G. C. Jackson, Mr. Francis McKinley, Rev. Wilfred Rylance ; St. Mary's: Rev. T. A. Hind, Mr. Edward Massey, Rev. Basil Mawson, Rev. J. P. Willson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AN OLD-TIME FOOTBALLER.</head>
        <p>AN OLD-TIME FOOTBALLER.</p>
        <p>Death Of Mr. Edward Dillon. Those with long memories will especi:. regret the death of Mr. Edward Dillon, ot Pig Hill, off Scotland-road, who did yeomai. work in building up the Warrington Football Club. Mr. Dillon played for the Padgate Club soon after it was started, and after the amalga- mation with Warrington. This was, however, before the days of the Northern Union, and for the services he rendered to the club he was created a life member. Mr. Dillon played for the love of the game. He never went on to the field except as an amateur, and in his day could always be certain of a place in the team. He was a first-class forward, possessing strength and running power in abundance. Although not particularly heavy, his zest and capacity in the "scrum" were very formidable. Most un- assuming, he was yet amongst the most deter- mined of players, saw hope to the last in a game, and never gave in until the whistle blew. His brother, the late Mr. Will Dillon, also had a great reputation, being a Warrington and county player. They did much to enhance the type of football in the district. Mr. Edward Dillon was employed for over forty years in various departments of the Whitecross Works, and testimony of his popularity there and in the football world was evidenced at the funeral, which • took place on Saturday. Some of his fellow workmen acted as bearers, these being: Messrs. J. Lester, D. Poole, C„ • Sudlovr, W. Hutchinson, J. Selland, and W. Baines. The Whitecross Company and staff were represented 1 by Messrs. R. Webster, G. Perry, E. Wright, H. Harris, A. Charnock, A. Perry and R. Hollo- way; and the Warrington Football Club by Messrs. H. Ashton, T. Barnes, T. Davies, F. Turner and T. Creagon. Beautiful floral tri- butes were placed ofl the grave from: "Wife," "Mary, Norah and Gerard," Football Club, "Kate," "Lizzie, Ernie and baby," "Pals, Whitecross," Whitecross staff, "Friends, Blue- back," Mr. and Mrs. Thompson, "Teresa and Nephew, wife and family," 5. Dillon, "Aunt Lizzie and family," r. i laynm ,' d' " ItArusz: t " flimily," Mrs. Cork, MaLth?, Dillon and. family, "Bernard and Polly," Kitty and Abraham," Mr. and -Mrs. Bailey, "Brother John, wife and family," Mr. and- Mrs. Jennings.</p>
      </div>
