      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MILITPiRY TRIBUNALS.</head>
        <p>MILITPiRY TRIBUNALS.</p>
        <p>DNE MAN BUSINESSES: MARINE STORE DEALERS.</p>
        <p>Handling Of China Crates.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Breward, glass and china mer- thant, applied for exemption for an employe, aged 35. He said he was his salesman in the Market for two days a week, and the only man who does the unpacking. The man's elder brother had been formerly in his employment. and he died suddenly. The present man helps the brother's widow and her three young chil- dren. Witness explained in reply to the Chair- man (Dr. Joseph) that a man had to be bAecially trained to the work, which consisted in large measure in lifting of heavy crates— none of these being less than five or six cwt. crate of plates would be about ten cwt. The 'llan himself also appealed on domestic grounds: The Tribunal allowed the employer's appeal and granted three months' exemption. They dismissed the man's application.</p>
        <p>Drapery Valls In Warrington Market. Mr. A.Browne,solicitor, appeared for Mr. John art, retail draper, who sought exemption for an eMploye on the ground of indispensability. The Solicitor explained that Mr. Hart had no less ,than thirteen stalls in different markets, and te had been coming to Warrington for 18 years. Re had three stalls in the Warrington Market and eleven others in Manchester and other Places. The man also had two stalls in War- rington. The latter was the only male employe, and he was also driver of the motor lurry, delivering all the goods to the markets except the " skips --7" titat 'arrived by train. Eleven females were employed, but no female Could possibly undertake the work that Samuel did. Mr. Hart himself suffered from disease of the kidneys, his doctor being Dr. Donnellan, of St. Helens. Samuel had been in the employ- ment for nine years. The employe, who had lodged an appeal on personal grounds, stated that he had four children, and that his wife had recently been under medical care. He also supported his mother.—Fonr months' exemp- tion was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Electrical Fitter.</head>
        <p>Electrical Fitter.</p>
        <p>• In the case of an electrical fitter, whose exemption was asked for by his employer, Mr. 'lagers, gr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, explained that Only temporary exemption was asked for. Tbe firta maintained electrical plants in various Parts of fife town. Mr. Rogers had endeavoured V) get a man to replace him through the Labour Bufeau, and all the bureau could do vrqs to advise him to get a discharged soldier. Mr. Liegers had not appealed for any other man. tie had lost all his ezlployes, and this was the only man he had for this ii-om By the Military Representative (Captain Mathams): This man had been exempted in January.---Mr. Ste-1 added that he thought the Lell2l if exer- ...on applied for was three -wan tbF</p>
        <p>b-P.-Deputy Mayor: For the period of the war! ( ugliter.) Mr. Steel: I hope it does not say that. One month's exemption was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Botanical Beer.</head>
        <p>Botanical Beer.</p>
        <p>The proprietor of a botanical Oee: factory and refreshment house, a single man, aged 28 :Years, sought exemption on the ground that his business would suffer if he had to go. In reply tt°, the Chairman, he said there was a secret in cc making of these drinks. He had made in- quiries and had not been able to find anyone Who would pay the price which he had given for the business. The new taxes on mineral Waters had made a great change. The Military Representative: Were you pre- pared to sell the secret at the same time, as the 15usiness?—I should have to do so, the business WOl ld be no use without 14-. aP(I You actually nffer to do so; did you vertise?—l did not Alvertisc, but I made in- quiri "through the tirms with whom I do —ade. carrYou make an: attempt to get a man to eoul3i; °TI the business, someone to whom von X ,rereal this process?—l think if I did Fo °111(1 he giving it away absolutely. • "u have no immediate relatives or brother, '11.3r046 to you' cella rinCt P—Nime. Captain Mathams suggested that no monetary !Paine entered into it except that his ginger beer had a nicer taste than another.—Applicant triode no reply. The Deputy-Mayor said that applicant would I,)e granted three months finally, and he should e able to dispose of the business in that time. ,c c was not married and that was the utmost concession L'loy had to offer him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Marine Store Dealets.</head>
        <p>Marine Store Dealets.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. 8. Steel, solicitor, appeared on behalf st'f several metal merchants, marine store dealers, who appealed on business grounds, the applicants alleging that they had no one to on their establishments, and rurtber ."ating that they supplied material to the Army Contractors for making khaki, and also broken raetal for munitions, &amp;c. L.°ne man trading in Warrington stated that 1!•is brother was also in the same business, but ?ad started in Earlestown. . They stopped the latter in that place. Steel: It is an offensive trade. (Laughter-) h lbe Deputy-Mayor: We are not so fastidious (Laughter.) lents applicant was 33 years of age and had arted in business twelve years ago. He did all the work himself, although at one time he !La:Ted 40 hands. Twenty-one had joined the m3,r and he was left with female labour and ree cripples. He drove the motor lurry him- and brought the material to the munition Works. 211 the case of applicant's brother, Mr. Steel that applicant was 26 years of age and he gone into business for himself in 1915. He h 48 a married man with three children. When e u. 'lad to leave Earlestown, where a petition o",a's Presented against the business being carried and in which it was described as a nuisance, obtained premises in Warrington. i•Ltit)th cases were adjourned for 14 days, when ‘ge decision will be given. a.Still another applicant in the same business alVealedon personal grounds. He is aged 26 i 8 marked with three children. He had n in business for a good while.—Three oaths' exemptinn was granted. h,!:k. T. S. Steel also appeared for a metal 4,7,1Ler who had been established for nine years, Said that he had been given temporary e,2--uPtion. He now asked for conditional ex- stuPtion. He had a big contract on, which ePuld be carried out. Nine of his men had 41.011 ed the Colours. ta'leinptiou was also sought for this appli- inttipes son, aged 19 years, on the ground of being LsPensable. The father, in giving evidence, hti4d that he could neither read nor write, and had son did all the correspondence. His on been in the business since leaving school. Wile Court granted conditional exemption to hm!: father, but refused the application on be- -4.1t of his son.</p>
        <p>Other Cases. Pavior and contractor, single, aged 22, 'ed for exemption. Asked if none of the nine en he employed would be capable of manag- .trag the business he replied "No, they were only o°4l:l"nen. labourers and mostly Irishmen."— thy, raonth's exemption was granted, this to be a 8 far as the prese-lt. Tribunal was con- ,vrried. t.. 4. decorator, painter an i aged 37, who 111(1 that his business wa r. conducted by 6:l4self, three of his empl‘r, eeing on military 14'ice and two in mul.;;-;..,n works. was given ie°4(lit* ional exemption. :a this and l'ases of a th—olar nature the Deputy Mayor stated that th: aPnlicant would be required either to join V.T.C. or act as Special Constable if egaired, In 4.1 tledtne case of another painter and decorator, 26, the military representative explained tuat two firms had offered to take charge of his vp.„8„1114.?•88 until he returned at the end of the pro',.._.* Applicant appeared dissatisfied with this kft'ition. but was assured by the Deputy- tor that everything would be looked after thi nka het advantage. Dr. Joseph added: "We tha this a very patriotic offer on the part of derns:.6r/na, and we did not think you would grat"' ta it." A month's exemption was A to enable applicant to arrange matters. ehesVl,,,,glejaling man, who is employed on the ftera,;47 Line Railway, asked for further s exenl',",on• Re is aged 34 and got three months' Dort in March last. He is the sole stip- in tliZ' Lwidowed mother and has one brother and another in the Navy. The application was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LAY PREACHER</head>
        <p>LAY PREACHER</p>
        <p>At Working Men's Mission Has Appeal Refused.</p>
        <p>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTORS.</p>
        <p>Two appeals for absolute exemption by conscientious objectors aroused much interest at the sitting of Warrington Rural Tribunal on Wednesday. _ _</p>
        <p>The first application heard was that of Alfred Walker, aged 36 years, 98, Liverpool- road, Sankey Bridges, who claimed exemption first as a minister of the Gospel, and, secondly, as having conscientious objections to military service. He is employed at soap packing and nailing by Messrs.. Crosfield and Sons, Bank Quay, and he is a married man with one child. The grounds of his appeal stated that he regu- larly conducted services at the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay. The statement opened by saying "The Working Men's Mission, of which I have been a member for eight years, is worked on the same lines as the Independent Methodists; in fact, the denomination in ques- tion was responsible for filling our pulpit for 30 years. My life since that great change has been consecrated to my Lord and Master, Jesus Christ, who is the Prince of Peace." The Chairman (Mr. Geo. Mather): You are appealing chiefly as you are a minister? Mr. Walker: Yes, as a minister and as a con- scientious objector.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I do not think we fieed go into the points as conscientious objectnr. I think a minister is certified. The Tribunal, however, decided to hear the case in full, and the rest of the statement was read. In this applicant asserted that the teacling through the Gospel in his heart was love to all mankind, and this virtue had con- trolled and was the basis of his life's work. War was a pregnant challenge to his concep- tion of the Gospel, and he could not reconcile the Gospel of Christ with war. As a minister of the Gospel he was following the path of sacred duty, which was to him of national im- portance. Holding these views as to the sacredness of human life, he was naturally a conscientious objector., Jesus Christ came into the world not to destroy life, but to save it.</p>
        <p>Advocate Of Arbitration.</p>
        <p>Replying to the long list of questions now put to persons pleading conscientious objections before Tribunals, Mr. Walker said he based his objections on spiritual and religio as grounds—. He objected to the taking of a mili- tary oath, as being against the principles of the Gospel. It was inconsistent with the teach- ing of Jesus Christ to use arms—arbitration was the proper course—reason founded on love.</p>
        <p>Askedi as to whether he would-be willing to join some branch of military service engaged not in the destroying, but in the saving of life, he insisted that he could truthfully say that he was doing work of the highest national im- portance in his preaching. It would be agttinst his conscience to take a military oath in that respect. He had held these opinions 'or approximately eight years, and letters were pro- duced in ,proof of that statement. He was a member of the Church of Christ, and was con- nected,with the Working Men's Mission, which was continually engaged in Christian work. In reply to the searching question as to whether it was one of the tenets a that body that no member was to belong to a military organisation, he said it was left to the in- dividual conscience.</p>
        <p>The applicant respectfully declined to answer the question as to whether he was a membn of any other body who objected to any military service whatsoever. He was willing, he said, to undertake some form of national service other than his present work, provided it did not conflict with his conscience. Ile would take farm work, but added that he felt what he was supplying was a valuable national asset—the moral uplifting of the ,nation. Finally, on being asked what sacrifices he was to make to help his country at the present time, he said he was willing to do any- thing that would not conflict with his con- science, and at the same rate of pay as a married soldier in the trenches. • Religious Record.</p>
        <p>Letters in support of Mr. Walker's statements were read from Mr. Thomas Dale, president, and Mr. A. Tilling, hon. sec. of the Bank Quay Mission, the latter having been instructed by the committee to do so.</p>
        <p>Writing from Epworth, Whitefield-road, Mr. Arthur Mounfield, editor of "The Independent Methodist," stated that he had been closely acquainted with Mr. Alfred Walker, who was seeking exemption from military service en conscience grounds. He served their churches without exception as a preacher, and was engaged in constant social work in the churches. The writer was familiar with Mr. Walker's views on the subject of war, and had known hi:: ft.; strongly pacifist and opposed to all war on grounds of religious teaching and conscience.</p>
        <p>The members having consulted in private, Mr. Walker was informed that his application was refused.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walker: I do not feel that justice has been done. I will ask for an appeal. The Chairman: Very well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PENKETH GARDENER'S CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTION.</head>
        <p>PENKETH GARDENER'S CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTION.</p>
        <p>A similar fate attended the application of Peter Davies, of Daisy Bank, Penketh, a gardener, who also claimed absolute exemption from military service on the ground that he had a deep and sincere conscientious objection to, taking part ix warfare. His long statement went on, "I cannot under any circumstances, whatever the penalty, surrender my convic- tions, and I believe that by holding fast to My sincere convictions and remaining true to my- self I am serving my country in the truest sense, believing as I do, that all war is detri- mental to the spiritual and moral well-being of nations, and, above all, contrary to the teaeh- ing of Christ. My belief is such that I cannot be part of the military machine." The Chairman then plied Mr. Davies with a long list of queries already mentioned. He admitted that he was not a member of any religious body, although connected for some time with the Wesleyan Methodists—a state- ment which drew forth the remark from the Chairman that he did not think Wesleyan Methodists as a body were conscientious objec- tors.</p>
        <p>Applicant said he was willing to undertake farm or other industrial work, assuming that his conscience was free to accept it. He fur- ther informed the. ,Tribunal that he is a married man with two children.</p>
        <p>The application was dismissed, and Mr. Davies said he would appeal.</p>
        <p>SON WHO APPLIED FOR A COMMISSION.</p>
        <p>"The military are going to appeal againet our decision to give your son conditional exemption, so now you can be prepared," re- marked the Chairman to a prominent farmer and grocer, of the Vulcan, whose son resigned his position as Deputy lie,gistrar at Warrington in order to work on the farm. The case first came before the Tribunal a month ago, when the young man was exempte for four weeks. His father now applied for an extension.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell recollected that the applicant's son had applted for a commission, and the reverend gentleman asked the father what he would have done had it been granted. The Father: I had a man at that time whom I should have appealed for strongly had my son 'got into the Officers' Training Corps. But that man has now gone into the Army. The Tribunal was a long time in coming to a decision, but at last they called in the appli- cant, and the Chairman said to him: "The Tribunal have considered the case in every way, and from every possible point of view. The verdict is that we give your son conditionll exemption. He must devote the whole of his time to the farm." The Military Representative (Mr. W. Fur- ness): The military, under the advice of the Advisory Committee, are going to appeal against it. The Chairman: Now you can be prepared! It is very kind of Mr. Furness to tell yoa straight. The applicant remarked that he was very pleased to have the information.</p>
        <p>SECRETARY OF FRIENDLY SOCIETY. Through Mr. Arthur Browne, a Hollins Grew.'</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Bullet Pierced His Head.</head>
        <p>Bullet Pierced His Head.</p>
        <p>A 'letter from Sergeant J. E. undo ran as follows:—` It is with deepest regret I write these few lines to inform you of the death of your husband. 'He was, I am sorry to: pay, killed in the early hours of the morning by bullet which pierced his head. You can .est assured that everything possible was done fia him, but he passed away peaceably, being. tot- conscious all, the time. I cannot describe the feeling it has caused among his comrades, for he was a splendid worker, and. was liked by eVeryea.7:.Who. kiiet* hint. You ' bave the Antie- f*ction of knowing that he has given his life whilst bravely doing his. duty on the field of honour. Your husband had been, posted along with several Others of my Platoon for duty up at the trenches, 'and it was whilst digging a new trench that your husband was hit. It will console you to know „1-hat a burial service will be Conducted in the usual manner, and his last resting-place Marked and •well-kept in future. In conclusion, accept on behalf of his comrades and myself, • our . deepest sympathy, and may God help you in your sorrow." -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PRIVATE P. EMERY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE P. EMERY.</p>
        <p>Official News Of His Fate At The Dardanelles.</p>
        <p>Missing since the landing at CI pe Private Peter Emery, a single • man, of 3, 1;n:- ton-bank, and a member of the King's Own Scottish Borderers, is now officially reported dead.</p>
        <p>Private Emery was employed as an appren- tice • fitter by Messrs. Bylands Bros. before join- ing the Army. His brother Stephen is attached, to the machine gun section of the 'Loyal Welsh Fusiliers, and has been at the Front eleven mouths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>Pir-V.VATE J. NOONS.</p>
        <p>Missing Since Beginning Of Last</p>
        <p>Month.</p>
        <p>An intimation has reached Mr. and Mrs. John Noons, 27, New-road, Latchford, to the effect that their son, Private John Noons, a member Of a Canadian Regiment, has been missing since the fighting which took place between June 2nd and 4th. •</p>
        <p>Private Noons, who is 22 years of age, was formerly employed in the netting room of Messrs. Rylands Bros„ Ltd. Eighteen months before the outbreak of war, however, he, along with. his brother Stanley, went out to Canada, where they joined their elder brother, Thomas. Soon after the commencement of the war John and Stanley joined the Canadian Contingent, and have been at the Front twelve months. In his earlier days, Private John was a member of Messrs. Rylands Bros.' football, club, and while in Canada took a keen interest in swim- ming. He was educated at St. Mary's R.C. School, Buttermarket-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MILITARY TRlBUNALS.—Continued.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRlBUNALS.—Continued.</p>
        <p>man applied for exemption, stating that ne was employed at a controlled works, and was secretary ,of the local branch of the Order z)f Druids, had four children, and his wife was in a delicate state of health. Mr. Browne pointed out that some of the men working b the applicant's side had been given badges by the Ministry of Munitions. Y It seemed invidious that he should have been passed over.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Kissing goes by favour. Laughtcr.) Mr. Furness: Not all men in controlled works are given badges at any place. It is just according to whether the Ministry of M.unitions think the work being turned out warrants tb.o badging of a certain number of men. Mr. Browne mentioned that there would be great difficulty in gOtting a successor for the applicant as secretary of the friendly society. In announcing a month's exemption, the Chairman remarked that the applicant could apply to the military for munition work. Mr. Furness: There is no obligation on. the 'military to do it, but they consider these cases sympathetically.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>THREE SONS ON FARM.</head>
        <p>THREE SONS ON FARM.</p>
        <p>A Burtonwood farmer, aged 70 years, who has three sons working for him, now applied.for one son, kit, the case was adjourned for fourteen days so that all the eases may be dealt with together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>. . • • • ' • • ••• ' I - _ . - ,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</p>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRWATE E. TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Local Anzlor Killed By Machine</p>
        <p>Gun Bullet.</p>
        <p>Local angl?rs will be grieved to hear of the death in action of Private Eli Taylor,- a keen and well-known fisherman, whose home. address was 62, Scott-street. He was a married man and a son of Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Taylor, 68, Chorley-street.</p>
        <p>Before enlisting he was employed by Messrs. A. Waring and Co., Ltd., Winwick-road. He was 27 years of age, and was educated at the National School, Church-street.</p>
        <p>News of his death was first .brought by a fellOw member of his regiment, Private 'Jack Weston, who was home on leave, while the next day the following letter was received kora the Rev. H. .F. S. Collier, Brigade Chaplain:— "Once again I have to write and tell one of the wives of our splendid battalion that she has lost her husband in this terrible War. Your husband was shot yesterday morning, June 22nd, by a bullet from. a machine-gun, whilst doing his duty on the top. Ile was killed in- stantly, and suffered no pain 'whatsoever, the</p>
        <p>PRIVATE ELI TAYLOR. Killed in Action.</p>
        <p>going through his head I should like to know what the C.S.M said just after 3 funeral. 'I have known him since October Last, when he was in my platoon, and he was one of the, best fellows I had—an excel- lent soldier, a kind and good chap, always most willing and ready to do any job.' I feel for you more than I can say—l know how bitter the blow will be: You have been :called upon to give, him up in the cause of justice, peace and truth, and he could have died for no greater one."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE A. ROSTRON.</p>
        <p>Wounded In Left Side After Many</p>
        <p>Narrow Escapes.</p>
        <p>Private Alf. Rostron, a married man, of 5, Golborne-street, is indeed a lucky man. After taking part in the Dardanelles campaign, in- cluding the evacuation, through which he came safely, he was transferred to Mesopotamia, where he took part in the efforts to relieve General Townshend at Kut. While here, he was wounded through the side, but previously had several miraculous escapes, as may be seen from the following letter sent to his brother William, at 27, Fothergill-street : "I have been wounded in the side, but it is healing up now, and I am knocking about in a convalescent camp in India. I must consider myself lucky to be alive, for from the sth April to the 18th I had as many narrow es- capes as Sherlock Holmes in the 'Union Jack.' In the morning of the 18th I got a bullet through my helmet, and it took a bunch of hair off my head. I also got another through my sleeve, and on the 17th, when we were making a dash under rapid and machine gun fire, I got another through My top pocket, the same bullet going through the lid of a tin which I carried and then through my pouches. So I have something to be thankful for. I am hoping to be in England before long."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>One of Four Soldier-Brothers.</head>
        <p>One of Four Soldier-Brothers.</p>
        <p>Private Alf. Rostron, who is 34 years of age, is one of the four soldier-sons of the late Mr. George Rostron and Mrs. Rostron, 8, Granville- street. He enlisted in August, 1914, having previously followed his employment as a coal- dealer.</p>
        <p>The eldest of the three remaining brothers is Private Frank, of the A.S.C. He is 38 years of age, and a married man, residing in Flag- lane. He enlisted in August, 1914, and has been at the Front about fourteen months. He also is a, coal dealer by trade. George A. Rostron, stationed in the South of England with the 2/4th Battalion South Lanes. Regiment, is 30 years of age. He was called up under the Group System in March, having previously been employed as a painter. He is ,t single man, and resides with his mothbr at S. Granville-street.</p>
        <p>Richard Rostron, also a single man, 26 years age, has been at the Front 12 months with I;he Royal Garrison Artillery. He was -nployed before the war as a cotton slashor t the Cockhedge Mills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PRIVATE 'IIARLES TURNER</head>
        <p>PRIVATE 'IIARLES TURNER</p>
        <p>Old Grammar School Boy Wounded</p>
        <p>With Canadians.</p>
        <p>Private Charles Turner, on of Mr. Turner, formerly head master of St. Barnabas' School, Warrington, has been wounded in France. News to this effect reached Miss Grounds (his aunt), who lives at Fearnhead, through a letter sent by one of his pals, but it did not state the nature of the injuries. Private Turner, who is 22 years of age, and single, was educated at Padgate C.E. Schools, afterwards proceeding to the Warringt..n Grammar School. After attending the Prestcn Agricultural College he went with his father to Canada. He enlisted in the Canadian Forces in August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>TEST MARCH.</head>
        <p>A TEST MARCH.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Training Corps Under Field</p>
        <p>Conditions-.</p>
        <p>One hundred and forty members of the War- rington Company and the Stockton Heath, Grappenhall and .Frodshara Platoons of the Volunteer Training Corps indulged in a test route march cn Siinday. The idea was that the movement biwuid be self-contained, carry its own field I:11.01in, a.ut approach as near as possible to similifir• conditions obtaining with men in training. The movement was a complete success, being carried out to the scheduled times, and none fell out over the total distance of •17i miles. There was a fair number of Group men on parade. The working party" and field kitchen carried out their duties very creditably Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., and Messrs. Peter Walker and Son lent motor lorries for transporting the men to the rendezvous at Weaverham.</p>
        <p>The orders for the day issued by Mr. W. Peter Rylands, officer commanding, were follows—</p>
        <p>"Field kitchen • squad parade Drill Hall, 9.30, for loading up. Composite company fc assemble at Victoria-square (Stockton Heath) at 10 a.m. Motor Transport Column (three lorries, two ears, one motor-dispatch • cyclist), move off 10.15 to Weaverham. 11 a.m., column forms up. Advance guard and thinkers thrown out and march commences on foot to centre cross-roads, Delamere Forest, via Cuddington, Norley, Hatchmere.. Field kitchen established. Milt and first meal 1.15 to 2.30. Column re- sume march to Fox Hill via Mouldsworth. Manley and Alvanley. Second halt, field kitchen prepare meal 4.45 to 6. Fox Hill to Frodsham. Column will entrain from Frod- harn Daresbury, and will march from Daresbury to Victoria-square, disperse 8.30 p.m. Officer and r subaltern for the day, Platoon Commanders F. Warburton and F. A. Frost; Assistant Quartermasters, Sergeants C. Hoult and H. Leigh, Group B. Reserves."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PATRIOTIC FAMILY.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTIC FAMILY.</p>
        <p>Twenty Members Serving In The Army.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Norton, a widow, in her 60th year, who appealed successfully to the Altrincham Tri- bunal for the total exemption of her eighth son, has many relatives in Warrington. The exen9- lion was granted after it had been shown that seven brothers were already in the Army. One is sergeant-major ill the Grenadier Guards, who served in the Boer War, and was a bearer and one of the bodyguard at the funeral of King Edward. The second son is sergeant-major-in- structor in the 'King's Own Royal Lancashire Regiment in India, and came with the Indian Expeditionary Force to France in January, 1915. The third son is a sergeant-bombardier-instruc- tor, and the fourth is in the Gordon High- landers The fifth at the outbreak of hostilities was in the Army Service Corps, and transferred to another regiment. The sixth has been killed in action, and the seventh is a driver with the 2nd Cavalry Division. These seven brothers are cousins to Quartermaster-Sergeant Percy 'fleas- dale (in France), Private Ernest Bleasdale (in France), and Sergeant Victor Bleasdale, 24. Marsh-street, Warrington, who is a patient at the Lord Derby War Hospital. There are over 20 members of thii family serving in the Army. Several have been killed in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>.... ._„-liSt Ok FIRE.</head>
        <p>.... ._„-liSt Ok FIRE.</p>
        <p>Every Precaution Taken At Whiteeross Military Hospital.</p>
        <p>Whether adequate provision has been made at the Whitecross Military Hospital should a fire break .out, was discussed at the Board of Guardians on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley pre- siding.</p>
        <p>The- Rev. A. M. Mitchell asked what pro- vision had been made for the protection of the soldiers from fire. He did not know whether the Board was satisfied with the existing arrangements, but he .thought outside stair- cases were preferable..</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Shaw: The hospital is a new one, in which all the newest appliances have been introduced. The plans were prepared by the smartest men, and everything that could be done was done. Does Mr. Mitchell mean to say that the building is unsafe? _</p>
        <p>The Reverend 'Gentleman: I never said such a thing.</p>
        <p>Mr. -Shaw • The ibutlding is a most admirable • , . . one.</p>
        <p>By stating thatl every possible precaution had been taken for emergencies, the Chairman re- moved any possible doubts which the reveread gentleman might have prompted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL OLD BOY</head>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL OLD BOY</p>
        <p>Writes A Humorous Article From The Trenches.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Percy F. Boardman, eldest son of Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Boardman, of Norris- street, was married to Miss E. Dunne of War- rington, December, 1914. Last November he went out to , France with the R.A.M.C. He received his early education at the National ISchools and the Secondary School, afterwards spending four successful years at tbe Manches- ter University, where he took his B.Sc. degree with honour, and he • has since received his Master's degree. When war was • declared Mr. Boardman occupied the position of Mathematical Master at the Eccles Secondary School. Writing from France to his school's magazine, he describes in a short humorous article "Active Service in Winter and Spring," which we herewith pro- duce:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>" Mud, Murder and Marmalade."</head>
        <p>" Mud, Murder and Marmalade."</p>
        <p>"You understand, readers, this must be brief. }Jere goes! Mud, murder and marmalade— that's war in winter! The murder is chiefly confined to the fighting area, but the mud and the'marmalade are everywhere in about equal quantities.</p>
        <p>"At night up the line' the impression ig that of a terrible thunderstorm, the flashes and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SERGEANT P. F. BOARDMAN.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT P. F. BOARDMAN.</p>
        <p>sounds from the guns approaching what one might experience—never in waking hours—say, in a nightmare after a heavy supper. "In the Spring—Spring, -the season of hope— the Army lives on rumours. There are many, but the chief. are ' leave ' rumours, and those connected with a ;great Spring offensive. Of course, 'leave' does not trouble some men, be- cause, according to their position on the leave register,' and at the rate at which 'leave' is being granted, they will probably go in 1921. But for those of us who are near the top of the roll the rumours are very exciting. 'Leave' is stopped and ktarted again every , few days. I tried to find the system on which they are working. First it was three days, then they stopped for one, then started again for four. I was quite. excited, for I had a theory that they were using the figure of [ I—you know, the 3.14159—but the next day the 'leave' stopped for a week, and iny theory was shat- tered!</p>
        <p>"The big Spring offensive must be a terribly difficult function to arrange, for already it has been postponed fourteen times! It is becoming a. sort of annual affait, and in 'future • years we shall see it among the Presa.notices about .State dinners, &amp;c., that 'weather . permitting, the B.E.F. will hold its Annual Big OffenSive on March 27th.' The ' Tommies '' call it ..the 'Big Push.'"</p>
        <p>Sergeant Boardman!s many 'friends in War- rington will be 'pleased to hear that he is enjoying good health, and is looking forward lo being home before long on a short leave. •</p>
        <p>.. ~:,..,:,....,.....„....„..:, , • ...... . . .:.,..,.•••••:;:,?.t.,..4:-:•,:,,,,,-. ',l'„, .... . ii.:.,:1:-3:•:::::. ~.,:, ~ - .:.';:t".i:ii4 . :.::::::::.tg::::::•:Hi.E...o=,. . ..,...ti . , ..:i1.1;11.-'.1?.'.:','• ...,, •&lt; . .. ' --. ..... , .;.. ' ' , ...f...i.:.,,....:".:......., . ?- :::::,:::,,•••::.: . :....::::i',:::,...-..fi.,:i..•::?,::,:ii:..;.,.-: ,- s -.--,J:*:„:.::....,::',.0',i:':-...: .....„....,..„......... , .4.15L',:0:K:.t.Lt1ii:0.4-44*.:*:. ..:::::-.....0,:8::,'.',....-'.':;,441:::•":. - :..::°.,,)4 otggig,44,4'!'',..::. :".:1iiii:::.:,i.iTi,i!i,..g1,e::,.;...i..::.:.:g!'...i*k*,f1A0,....:,,,iii.::::-•:;.. 14x5tka11i....:::::':.:.::•..:-.: ii. '':.,:',..:::"l:AiiNtfllgi'..-I,T.',.:,i:::':mbi4.si t.: :41•37:::iiir,::!!:.!!!.,'":!::.'i::-.I;.,.:', ,i..f..i.'-`:,:i:1,:',)::',...T.:,, : . ..',:::.,..4.,„,,,.,1:..i,;,,....:::„:....,.....:5.,.,..i... . ''''l,.. .. -4,.. :::::%. " ' •,:i • ''':.4..:*: , ,:x.: „.:. . - : -;:tz:::*,-.: •••.4's.'" :x1'.1.i7.•• s:''.'.., .4.g-ii,,iigi..:Aißi.': . ~ ...:',..t '.i•:•,?...i: .:-..7 •• -... :.i§.i.:**..:1::;..::.:...:.,. ::::,.1, .?„.,,.i.,i,:::: ~..:::. -.. . :i.:.iii,::.:.iiii,,...:::,• ' . , ::.•:o.iiir .1 ..; ii.:g:.ir,.:.:..:::.::i.::::i: • 1.*.,.•::: ,',i ~. ....:.....:::.]::::::::,::::j:.::i,..:.:::.,a, .. • ...,.,...,.....:,:::::. .... _:, :...,,,ii::::..•-•il:,.:„:il.,:kiip::: ::..,,,... ;,:. ,'...,' -,,!..:!:.!::.•"........--.:' .. :,.....„.......::::.....,:.•...,:-......1.k ~.....,,,„:„ . ...,..:,..............:.:::„..,„:„,:„.t.:,......... 4 ...:::,...i.,,,.,...rwi.: - ~.„.. . ~.... ~.--..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASIBRES. Fit And Cheerful in Spite Of Strenuous Time.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASIBRES. Fit And Cheerful in Spite Of Strenuous Time.</p>
        <p>Captain Sydney T. Boast, of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, writii4 em the Front to the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Ly Whittle) says: • The consignment of cigare s and tobacco mentioned in your letter ( • -ed 27th May, arrived 'safely to-day and has been duly dis- tributed. We have been on the . march for several days, and at the moment we are halted for a couple of days' rest. The smokes have arrived at a very suitable time, the men being rather short and they are all very grateful for the gift. On their behalf I send, through you, 'sincere thanks to those who, have been kind enough to subscribe to the " smokes " fund.</p>
        <p>The men are all very fit and cheerful in spite of the very strenuous time they have had for some time past. lam not permitted to tell you about our movements or present situation; but "all's well " with the Battalion. Thanks For Congratulations.' _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>I take this opportUnity of thanking yourself and my many friends in Warrington for thea kind congratulations in connection with my recent reward. I have received several letters from different people, and will reply to them as soon as possible. While on the move I haven't much time to write, as my time is fully occupied, and I hope my friends Will understand how much I. appreciate their kind letters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>THANK YOU!</head>
        <p>THANK YOU!</p>
        <p>From The Men Of The 4th South</p>
        <p>Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>"Just a few lines to thank the subscribi,r3 for Cigs. We were just run out at the time. '— Pte. E. Weaver, A. Co., 3 Platoon. "Me being a tobao smoker [ tell you I enjoyed your plug; it is grand."—E.. R., C. "Many thanks for parcel n uues, which were very -. ',Come and enioyable. as we were practica.J s run Of cou.roe, „knowing that they e Ale from the .good old town of Warring- ton fey were twice as good. Good luck to Al those friends who have helped to send the cigs. Thanking you once more."—R. E. Park, Boys of the 4th S.L.R.</p>
        <p>" Many thanks. Hoping to see you all soon." —Boys of the C. Co.</p>
        <p>"Pte. Henry Sutton, D. Co., 15 Platoon, sends many thanks for smokes received."</p>
        <p>"we thank you very much for the cigarettes. With best wishes from all the boys of No. :- Platoon, B. Co."—Pte. S. W. Dutton.</p>
        <p>"I can honestly assure you they came once again at the right time. The old cry was heard when they were seen 'Warrington Issue, Lads.' Believe me that is the war cry when they reach their journey's end.—T. Warburton, L.C.I). "They were welcome, many thanks."—Pte. J. Makin 5244, A. Co. .• "On behalf of Batt Bipli?ers we thank you for your kindness in sending cigarettes and wish every success for Warrington people. From the boys in the trenches."—Corporal Bentley, Lce.-Corporal Arond, Lce.-Corporal Butter- worth.</p>
        <p>"We thank the people of Warrington for their kindness in sending us smokes. We were in need of them just at the time. Thanking you one and all."—Pte. P Farrett, A. Co.; Pte. J. Gilberts, C. Co. • "On behalf of my comrades and self I thank you greatly and must confess that the smokes arrived at a most convenient time, as the majority of the boys had 'absolutely run out and found them very difficult to get."--Lce.- Corporal A. Glazebrook, Drums 2 S.L..g. The above are a few of the expressions of thanks from the Front that have reached War- rington via the Town Clerk.</p>
        <p>NAME.</p>
        <p>Brough, Thomas Bell, James Coiling, Charles Cuthbert Murrew, John- • Mockler, Thomas • Connelly, Peter Smith, John Charlotte, Henry George Gilligan, Michael Jennings, M. Thomas McDermotte, John Maxwell, John George Maskin, Michael Murphy, John Naughton, Michael Thornton, Thomas Dobson, John Duffy, Joseph • Faulkner, John William Histy, Michael McDonnell, John Reilly, James Regan, James Samuel, John James Francis, David John Mullhany, James Madden, Dan Flannery, Patrick Heaney, James Lyons, Thomas McDermott, John Charlotte, William Connor' Thomas Dowd, John Geraghty, Thomas Hunter, Wm. Crosby Harley, Bridgeman Burke, Michael Duffy, James Kelly, Michael Mullen, Thomas Pendergast, John Clarke, Edward Charmer, James Connelly, John Kennedy, Martin McHugh, Martin Thompson, Roland Craven, Patrick Egan, Martin Godfrey, Martin Gormelly, Thomas Cox, Ben Hynes, John Houghton; Richard Peter Horan, John Tansey, John Church, Leonard Cusack, Patrick Dawson, William John Dooney, Patrick Hickman, George Magreal, John Egan' Michael, Hunt, Patrick Doyle, Michael • Corley, Patrick Gleason, Michael Keary, Thomas Phillips, Arthur Hargreaves, John Newall, James • Naylor, Harry Geraghty, Patrick Lombar, Thomas Cooke, John Feeney, John Gantley, William Keaveney, Edward Devaney, Martin Murphy, Patrick, jun. Oates, John Pendergast, Willie 'Maxwell, James Edward White, George Burke, Martin Madden, James Dean, John Henry Perry, Ralph Newbold Quinn, Martin McCarthy, Mathew Cullent Patrick Jackson, William Kearns, Patrick Carramore Raftery, William Townsley, Samuel Hands, John Horan, Thomas • ( McCradden, Michael Morgan, Henry George Ward, Martin Conlon, Anthony Cottis, Herbert Etchell, William Fitzpatrick, Patrick Hughes, William Shaugnessy, William Broadhurst, John 'Byrne, Patrick . Cartwright, Joseph Cooper, Christopher Prosser, David John Brennan, James Dutton, James Lee, William Henry Casey, Thomas Barber, Edward Griffiths, Francis McGrath, David McTirrney, Amos Marshall, Edward Conlon, James Feeney, Andrew Graham, Charles Mason, Frederick Stead, William Bryne, Edward Cults, Victor Dean,. William Graham, Michael Hurney, James parr, Henry John Keating, William Kilmartin, Patrick</p>
        <p>Brownlow, Frank Evans, Evan • Malone, Peter Bishop. Harry Flaherty, William Kieven, P. Lewis, Albert Sninks, Alfred Yates, John Tierney, John McDonald, Martin Hickman, William Howarth, Ralph Conway. Albert Green, Peter Sudlow. John William Boyd, William Allen, Robert Duck, Arthur Carter, Ernest Carr, W•iliam Um- James P Herbert</p>
        <p>WEEK-EM) CAMP.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's C.L.B. Have Strenuous But</p>
        <p>Happy Time At Ctileheth.</p>
        <p>Marching out to Culcheth Hall Farm (kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. Lawton) on Saturday afternoon, the N.C.O's of the 2nd Liverpool Cadet Battalion C.L.8., St. Paul's Company, stayed there unfil next day, and had an enjoyable, profitable and strenuous time under the instruction of Sergeant Goodwin, of the Administrative Centre, 4th South Lancs. Regiment.</p>
        <p>After the camp had been pitched tea was served, and at 8.30 Sergeant Goodwin had them on parade and gave a lecturette on ex- tended order drill and guard-mounting. Supper over, the N.C.O.'s were marched out for out- post duty and night reconnaissance and, in addition Sergeant Goodwin illustrated ,how the trenches in France are relieved and manned, two potato hogs which happened to be empty serving as realistic trenches. The lads returned to camp at 2 a.m.- for a well-earned rest, and were. up again at five o'clock for-phyeiost-frill.</p>
      </div>
