      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>, DISTRICT pfitw3 ,</head>
        <p>, DISTRICT pfitw3 ,</p>
        <p>I_4 Y IVI</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>"Who is this 'critic' I wonder?" This was part of a conversation I overheard the other evening.</p>
        <p>Now, boys, come along. Guess.</p>
        <p>Or better still, write the Editor.</p>
        <p>All I can advise you to do is to be very care- fal to whom you address the question. You might ask the culprit himself.</p>
        <p>"One never knows," does one?</p>
        <p>Since the fishing season began the lake has become the principal attraction in Lymm.</p>
        <p>The wounded soldiers from Brookfield still have the privilege of fishing as the guests of the Council.</p>
        <p>What are our Councillors thinking about lately? lardly a single meeting goes by with- out a row of some description.</p>
        <p>1 , to 'I</p>
        <p>Why don't they settle their personal griev- ances in the straight and manly way?</p>
        <p>My advice to them is to remember what they are there for, and whom they represent</p>
        <p>And to think a little bit more of the lads that have brought honour the village, and give what they have well-earned—" Public recognition,"</p>
        <p>I am sorry to say that one of our heroes has just passed away. the victim of wounds received in action.</p>
        <p>He was home on furlough a few weeks ago, and nothing was done to show that the village appreciated the honour that he had bestowed upon it.</p>
        <p>What is the use of having leaders if our leaders want to be led?</p>
        <p>A memorial service was held at the Congre- gational Church on Sunday night to the memory of all Lymm men who have fell during the great war.</p>
        <p>We are at last awakening to our sense of duty.</p>
        <p>I hear that at a near date all Volunteers are to be re-enrolled, subject to the new conditions.</p>
        <p>I am informed that a jumble sale will take place in the Wesleyan Schoolroom this after- noon. '</p>
        <p>More summer fashions.</p>
        <p>Private James Jennings and His Pal.— Private James Jennings, the Manchester Regi- ment, who is at present serving in Egypt, writ- ing to his sister, Miss Alice Jennings, Newfields, says: "You will be sorry-to hear that William Taylor (another Newfields soldier) and I are parted again, for how long I cannot tell, but they moved from here under very short notice last -week, and, I have not seen or heard from 'him since." These two men, though bosom pals in peace time, joined two different branches of His Majesty's Army, and got separated, but, by a strange coincidence, both men were drafted out to Egypt and get linked together again under somewhat different surroundings. Wounded Soldiers At Play.—Sport has reigned supreme at the Brookfield Hospital this week, and interesting billiard and cricket matches have taken place. The billiard match in the• billiard hall 'attached to the Brookfield Hospital created considerable interest, for the visitors were a team of seven wounded " Tommies " from the Thelwall Heys Military Hospital. Of the seven games, Thelwall secured four victories and Lymm three, and the total of points obtained were :—Thelwall, 573 points; Lymm, 522 points; which gave the visitors a decisive win by 51 points. The cricket match between the Hospital XI. and the Grammar School team, took place on the Grammar School ,ground on Wednesday afternoon, and' resulted in a win for the Hos- pital by five runs. The Brookfield XI. are to meet the Warring- ton Recreation team on the Lymm Cricket Grotind at 3 o'clock this afternoon.</p>
