      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LANCE-SERGT. H. BROOKES</head>
        <p>LANCE-SERGT. H. BROOKES</p>
        <p>Possessor Of Russian Honour Dies Of Wounds.</p>
        <p>It is with profound regret that we announce the death of Lance-Sergeant Henry Brookes, Cheshire Regiment, which has taken place at the 2nd Canadian General Base Hospital, some- where in France. This gallant soldier was wounded about three weeks ago, shortly after returning from home, where he had been spending a well-earned furlough. • The sad news wa-3 first conveyed to his wife, who resides at Heald Green, Cheadle, in a letter sent by the Regimental Paymaster, which in- formed her of her husband's death, and in- structed her what to do as regards hei allow- ance, &amp;c. This came as a tremendous shock, for it was believed that the gallant Servant was only slightly wounded. Believing that a mistake had been made, several friends both at Heald Green and Lymm took tlie matter in hand, and telegrams were at once dispatched to the War Office asking for confirmation. Early on Sunday morning the news spread round the village that a telegram had been received from the War Office which gave confirmation to the Regimental Paymaster's report. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Decorated For Heroic Ambuiance Work.</head>
        <p>Decorated For Heroic Ambuiance Work.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Henry Brookes, being a member of the Cheshire Regiment, was mobilised at the cutbreak of war, and after training for several months in different parts of the country, he was drafted out to France along with about forty</p>
        <p>Died of Wounds</p>
        <p>or fifty Lymm men. This took place early in February of 1915. About half through his training he took up the position of company stietcher-bearer, and for the remaining period studied first-aid, attaining a high state of efficiency. At the time of being wounded, he actually gave instructions as to how his own wounds ought to be dressed. His devotion to duty knew no bounds, and his whole period of active service was full of brave deeds, and many were the lads relieved through the un- failing bravery. His deeds will always occupy a prominent place in the memory of his com- rades. At last his "gallantry came under the eyes of some high authority, and in August, 1915, intimation was received that the gallant Sergeant had been awarded the much coveted Russian medal, The Order of St. George. This was the first war decoration won by a Lymm man.</p>
        <p>$,;.;:d;;;;:::::::''.:'%1:*.'477-:,,tig4.•.",i,'..4.!-:V...;**4f,516414'44,10;,...•,:4.-',1,(1.41:44:1 .&lt;...itt::,L,..:, ii*.).,4;3,', ::1‘';0;';: *'. .' ' , . ''''-4.11,1t4:1P;'.,:', :ix. 1 'e ;,..0..,,,g.T.,,,i,g.:/i4 .4.:,,,:. Y. -.P.,•::::;',,P4 - 1.P40.,,,:e1itp-V i':::....:... :'.g414.7.4,#.4k.;.. ';.:.7i"...:.:_0tg.::..g:- •-:-.7.:,.1:i,:,--?..,. -. -....:.tt:::::,.4&lt;g -?.?:;,,.' v.:-....-i,--:;::4:::iifief... ..,...-..,....:.: ::,:&gt;..,,z.1...-::4,...t:',:".: ' g1.;'..:..7-. ... -:'''. -.::,:::::':iC;I:f-i'?;.SNi:*-5ii ' 1147:Itiatkitii';.!;'-?!.4.1......,. ~.• ' '', 4:....,-. ;. ~..., ,t .. 1N ....-.... ..:.mk.-...; . 44e.;..-':-..::..fii.:.;..:14.;',..;;.;.4. 4.;:a0R0401:"; ';,...,........., --'...........*:iiti.Waic'5,..w•,..;,:?,74 •.!,:;,: --.0,. ji 1 •••••• -...-:-. ,- ~..-„,&amp;-,:-:::,--,,,;.,,,,,efflifig:f..141110:' .......,,,.,:::,..:;.i•::::::-....,.....,,:::::,::::,-„,.-9.,,,,:s.„.:411.fie„,,45,:.p.:,:.,:vi-.T.:5,:,,....-.".,:,;::::.:hotiv;:?,,11 4, .--- .. -....0).- :,...:„..:.i.:.;.:.:...1i.::::qii1,1*-.......,,,::::,...,:gig:::,...,.,:,-tr,.--.1:g..,..:41 . . ..:-.40 ...........,.,,,„.1 ..,..1 .....,..,...e',..'.A 1 ...„........,, &gt;.,4 .P' ' ..',"'":::2::::;:d</p>
        <p>LANCE-SERGEANT H. BROOKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Comrade' Testimony.</head>
        <p>A Comrade' Testimony.</p>
        <p>Sergeant J. H. Moston, one of the late Sergeant's comrades, whO has this week been home on furlough, speaking to our representa- tive, said: "The deceased soldier will be a missed man, both at home and out there'; for in him we had a man who had a strong sense of duty, willing to give a helping hand to anyone, and assist in any way he possibly could. All the good work and brave deeds he performed in this war will never be told, and the decoration he was recently awarded is only a fraction of what he really deserwd. He was a man that was always in the right place at the right moment. He did not know what fear was when he was assisting a wounded comrade. He was a thorough man, a goad soldier, and a hero in every sense of the word." An cx-Lymm Gardener.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Henry Bro()les was 26 years old last January, and had been married just over two years. He was educated at Lymm School, and worshipped at the Primitive Methodist Chapel. Prior to the war he was employed at a gardener by Mr. H. Champ, of Heald Green, Cheadle, late of Foxley Lodge, Lymm. Besides his wife and child, he leaves a father and mother, one sister and two brothers to mourn his loss. One of his brothers—ex-Private James Taylor—has lost the use of his left arm through a wound received in France. He is now engaged making munitions at the Lancashire Tool Works, Henry-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>HALF-PAY.</head>
        <p>HALF-PAY.</p>
        <p>Allowances To _Soldier-Employes</p>
        <p>Discussed By Council.</p>
        <p>The following notice of motion appeared on the agenda paper of Monday's meeting of the Lymm Urban Council, standing in the name of Mr. J. H. Holt, vice-chairman: "That all allowances to Council employes now serving with H.M. Forces be discontinued, and that each case be decided on itsi merits and dealt with accordingly." The Chairman (Mr. F. Lockyer) ruled the motion out of order as it did not express the intention of rescinding the resolution already in existence giving allowances to soldier employes. Mr. Holt protested against the ruling, and said there was too much of this straining at gnats and swallowing camels. If the notice of motion was not in order why had it oeen put on the agenda, or why had it not been corrected?</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Cowper, seconded by Mr. Parker; the motion was allowed to stand for discussion. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Holt, in moving, said they knew there were great differences in the cases they had before them from time to time. One or two employes had expressed their determination not to come back to the service of the Council. They seemed to think little of what was done for them, and treated it as a bagatelle. His proposal was that each case should be treated on its merits. That work could be done in com- mittee. They should start on fresh lines. Mr. Critchley seconded, -</p>
        <p>Mr. Ingham thought if they were to discuss each case separately it would take up a great deal of time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gotobed moved that the matter be re- ferred to the General Purposes Committee. The proposition which gave to employes of the Coun- cil half wages might have been an unwise one, but it was done with a good intention. Their soldier employes had gone out to the Front and were fighting for them. One had, indeed, laid down his life for them. Coula they fairly break faith with these men; and if they could break faith with them, what would be the saving to the ratepayers if the present resolu- tion were passed? Mr. Ingitain seconded the amendment, and the -matter was referred to the General Pur- poses ;Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</p>
        <p>TRIBUNAL SCENES.</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker Objects To</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed.</p>
        <p>Councillor G. Parker, a well-known member of the local Council, who was before the Tri- bunal to answer an appeal, but had previously been granted a medical certificate, stated that he objected to Councillor R. H. Gotobed being a member of the Tribunal on the grounds that, being an ex-N.C.O. of the Cheshire Regiment, he was liable to service, and no man ought to be on a Tribunal who was liable to service.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. T. J. Ridgway) quickly disposed of the objection, and remarked that he (the Chairman) had nothing td do with the election of the members, and could not hold himself responsible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETY SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>An assistant secretary to a friendly society was granted temporary exemption until the Ist of August.</p>
        <p>MR. H. S. SAVILLE DEMANDS A HEARING.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. S. Saville, an ironmonger and taxi proprietor, appeared to ask that further time should be granted to his son, who was indis- pensable to his business, and he was his only man of military age left. This case had been heard before the Appeal Tribunal, and according to Act_of Parliament, no case that has been heard before an Appeal Tribunal could afterwards be dealt with by a local Tribunal. On this being pointed out to Mr. Saville, he remarked that, he had made an application for his case to be heard at the Appeal Tribunal, and had been instructed Iv the Clerk of that Tribunal that he would have to apply to Lymm, and that his case would be heard there.</p>
        <p>The Chairman remarked that the Tribunal were very sorry, but they could not go con- trary to Act of Parliament.</p>
        <p>Mr. Saville then left the room remarking: "If this sort of thing goes on much longer it will jua about finish me. I have had enough of it."</p>
        <p>After being absent for a few. minutes Mr. Saville retprned and flourishing his Tribunal summons in his hand stated: "I have here a letter from the Clerk of this Tribunal sum- moning me for a hearing, and in the name of justice I demand a hearing." The Chairman explained that the Clerk only did what he was told. Proceeding, Mr. Saville said: It is an un- fair proceeding, and I feel very strong about it. There are men on this Tribunal who ought to be serving their country to-day. There are men who have had total exemption from here who have not half the business obligations that I have. It is not fair play, not justice, and I think that I have a right to demand to be heard.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: If I am wrong no doubt 1 shall be put right. M. Saville: It's all very well for men who have no sons to be patriotic. The Chairman Have Ino sons? You had better complain to the House of Commons about the Act.</p>
        <p>Mr. Saville: Have I to write to the House of Commons?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I am very sorry, and I am sure that the Tribunal are very sorry, and we will try to do all we can. If I had not known you so long and respected you I should not have taken the trouble to explain. Air. Saville: There is not much respect when you give me a month in which to settle up my business.</p>
        <p>Councillor J. H. Holt: We have considered the case, Mr. Saville, and I personally sympa- thise with you.</p>
        <p>Mr. Saville: Well, gentlemen, I am very sorry but I could not help it. What am Ito do now? My son's time is up on the 24th. The Chairman: I cannot advise you, but I understand that if you get an appeal in with the Appeal Tribunal they are botaid to let your son stay while the appeal is pending. You are not the only one in the country. Mr. Saville: It has been in print that small businesses would be protected. and not be given over to large operat;ve'firms. The Chairman • I am sorry, Mr. Saville, but I cannot see the end of the war yet. Mr. Saville: Can I appeal to London? The Chairman: I think that you will have to get the. Appeal Tribunal's consent</p>
        <p>Talking to "The Examiner " representative, Mr. Saville said: "I have made investigations as regards the Tribunal scene, and I learn from high authority that the Lymm Tribunal were wrong?'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>" PADDY'S " SEND-OFF.</head>
        <p>" PADDY'S " SEND-OFF.</p>
        <p>Patriotic Scenes At Lymm Station.</p>
        <p>Patriotic scenes were witnesSed at Lymna Station on Thursday morning, when Private J. Geavehan, more commonly known by the vil- lagers and his comrades as "Paddy," departed from Brookfield on ,sick leave prior to returning to the front. All his hospital comrades assembled dt the station, and the time prior to the train' coming, which was a full fifteen minutes, was spent in singing patriotic and popular airs. One of the principal features of the programme was a song by Sapper Goodwin entitled "Where does Paddy go, when he goes out?" which caused con- siderable amusement, and brought forth rounds of applause. As the train steamed in the party sang "Auld lang syne," and as the guard waved his flag three hearty cheers were given for the departing comrade. Although " Paddy " has been at Lymm only four weeks recuperating from gas poisoning, he had made himself a great favourite, his gentlemanly ways and. jolly dis- position having won the hearts of all.</p>
        <p>District Council.—. The Councillors present at Monday's meeting of the Urban Council were Messrs. F. Lockyer (chairman), J. H. Holt (vice- chairman), W. G. Groves, C. E. Cowper, F. Ingham, S. Critchley, R. H. Gotobed, G. H. Parker and A. .Mclntyre. Officials attending were Messrs. Cecil Mort (assistant clerk), D. Mort (surveyor), T. H. West (gas manager), C. I. Barnes (water manager) and D. Burrows (acting temporary medical officer): As Mr. Cecil Mort is joining the Colours and was called for Wednesday, the Council issued advertisements for assistant clerk to take over the duties until Mr. Mort returns. Applica- cations for the position were considered at Mon- day's meeting, and it was decided to write a couple of the applicants to attend at noon the following Wednesday for an interview. Mr. V. G. Graham, of Statham, was appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PERSONALITIES!</head>
        <p>PERSONALITIES!</p>
        <p>Councillors Parker &amp; Gotobed At War In Council.</p>
        <p>COMPLAINT OF TRIBUNAL'S PARTIALITY.,</p>
        <p>Personal interchanges of a 'rather warm nature took place at Monday's meetine of the Lymm Urban Council, where some members of that body aired what they termed "grievances," and spoke rather heatedly. When the meeting was discussing the terms of appointment of an assistant clerk, Councillor Gotobed made some remark which elicited from Councillor Parker the remark, "You should be in khaki," adding something else as to "being of military age," and also referring to the Military Tribunal. The incident was of sucn a momentary nature that it passed almost unnoticed, but be- fore the Council separated, Councillor Gotobed, risintr, said: "I want to make a personal observation. This Council appoints the Military Tribunal for this dis- trict and has appointed me amongst the num- 12)er. At the last meeting of the Tribunal a member of this Council applied for exemption. He hadn't any need to come, because he had in his pocket a medical certificate which carried exemption. He took the Opportunity of object- ing to myself as a member of the Tribunal, saying that I was of military age, and he has done the same to-night. It is not a very nice thing for him to say. If I were of military age I would not have to be fetched,. I would be serving my King and country. I was serving my King and country when the gentleman who made the remark was a child of fourteen years old. I was fourteen years in the Army and rose to the rank of Sergeant. At the time of the Boer War I volunteered for service, but I was a married man, and only single men were taken. I am not of military age, I am sorry to say.</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker (who spoke somewhat heatedly) : I did not accuse him of being of military age, but he is available for the Army. I am not available, I have been rejected four times. I object to him being on the Tribunal. A man who has been a Sergeant in the Army is available until he is 55. The reason I object to him is that for the last two or three months he has been most insulting to me. We elect the members of this Tribunal, and what would you say when a chap meets you in the street and says, "I shall put you in khaki; have you got your papers ?" I say he is not a right man to be on the Tribunal. I was going to move that he be removed from the Tribunal. A man on the Tribunal is supposed to be level- headed, just, and impartial, and I repeat that Councillor Gotobed is not impartial. • Councillor Cowper "Also Insulted."</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper said he had also been in- sulted. s, " This man," he said, looking at nis left-hand neighbour, "said I had no sons fighting. I have two sons, one is h Sergeant and the other is in the Red'CrosS. Members of this Council have not shown im- partiality." Councillor Ingham interjected that a Coun- cil meeting was not the place for airing per- sonal grievances. If there was a question of the action of a member on the Tribunal notice ought to be given. Councillor Cowper: I was insulted in the Council room.</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker: I give notice that the name of Mr. Gotobed be removed, as he is not a fit and proper person to sit on the Tribunal. Councillor Gotobed : I object to such a thing —it is absolutely offensive. I tried to curb my temper and control my remarks. Councillor Parker: Didn't you challenge the certificate and almost suggest that I had forged it?</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed : The certificate was passed round, and any member had a right to look at it. I did see it, and I was satisfied that it was a proper certificate. It shows the bias he has. He is a nice man to object to my seeing the certificate, and I challenge him as to the truth of the statement.</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker: I never say a thing that I cannot substantiate. Councillor Gotobed intimated that ho did not wish to .say any. more on the subject.</p>
        <p>Councillor Holt said he wished to ventilate a grievance that he had. There , was a state- ment that the Tribunal had not been alto- gether impartial, but he could say that every ease that came up for consideration could not have been dealt with more justly or equitably than it was by the Lymm Tribunal. "There is only one grievance," Mr. Holt added, "and it is this. We gave a farmer's 'son up to October Ist. There is only one son, and the farm is 52 acres in extent. The mili- tary representative' said, 'You are giving this man too long. I shall appeal against the de- cision.' He did so and took the case to Stock- port, with the result that the young man got leave up to the 30th June. However, the young man appealed again and had his application not been successful he would have had to go at the end of the week, and the father would have been left absolutely helpless with 52 acres to look after. He cannot get men, and, there- fore, I feel very strongly about the ease." At this stage the Chairman (Councillor F. Lockyer) mildly interposed and said he did not think these matters had. any connection with Council business. The members then gradually filed from the room, apparently none the worse for the recent high temperature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OUR FALLEN HEROES.</head>
        <p>OUR FALLEN HEROES.</p>
        <p>Memorial Service At The Congrega-</p>
        <p>tional Church.</p>
        <p>A memorial service was held at the Congre- gational Church on Sunday evening for all Lymm men who have made the supreme sacri- fice in the present crisis. The service was con- ducted by the Rev. S. Gill, the Congre- gational minister, and a large congregation was present. After the hymns "Brief life is here our portion" and "My God, my Father, while I stray" had been sung, the names of all the fallen Lymm men were read, and conclud- ing with that of Lance-Sergeant Henry Bi•Ockes, the minister remarked that this man had been decorated for gallantry, but had this week passed away, the result of wounds received in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Best Christian, Best Citizen.</head>
        <p>Best Christian, Best Citizen.</p>
        <p>The reverend gentleman addressing the con- gregation, said: Let your citizenship be worthy of the Gospel of Christ; let all of us be worthy citizens, not only of Britain, but of the Lord Jesus Christ, for he who is the best Cliristian i 4 the best citizen of this world. There can be no untimely death to him or her that be- lieves in Christ. If we, oould only look at things in the light of heaven we would possibly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WEA lc 510 i-FT</head>
        <p>WEA lc 510 i-FT</p>
        <p>3ee the youth of' God in his prime. In the moment of death we should have no vain regrets, for out of the heavens comes a spirit, and as the angel form bends it imprints a kiss on his brow. There are many warriors who have swiftly reached this goal. It is not for you or me to complain and mourn the loss of those we shall not see again, for we have reason to believe that these men came into the world simply to die for sinners. The divine purpose of the war is redemption. While ,on the Cross Christ said "It is finished." His 'work was completed on the Cross. Finished Work At Noon.</p>
        <p>Turning to the bereaved ones present, the reverend minister proceeded : I ask you to think that your dear one's work was done. He left off at mid-day, but his work was done. Your husband, your boy, has died for you and me. Is not this a high calling P We live in thoughts, and there will collie a time for our thoughts. They have lived until their work was done. Christ to the end, for it was Christ in the be- ginning. Let your citizenship be worthy of the love of Christ. Faith is not to be had for the mere asking, for it demands obedience to the laws of Christ's kingdom. Life and death are God's demand, and it was His wish that they were released from the toils of life.</p>
        <p>At the couclusion of the service Miss M. J. Appleton, a member of the choir, sang with great effect "Only remember?' Sunday School Roll of Honour.</p>
        <p>A pleasing ceremony took place in the after- noon, when Miss Laura Seymour, a life-long scholar, presented the school with a roll of honour, on which is inscribed all the names of past and 'present scholars who have answered the call to arms, who number about 52. Of these at least 10 or 12 have made the great sacrifice.</p>
        <p>Mr. Geo. Willan, the school superintendent, received this magnificent gift on behalf of the school.</p>
        <p>Clerk of Council 111.—At Monday's meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council, Mr. Cecil Mort, assistant clerk, said that the Clerk of the Council, Mr. William Mallard, had been advised by his 'medical attendant, Dr. Burrows, not to resume his duties for a month, as he needed entire rest. The Council expressed their regret at Mr. Mallard's illness and hoped that he would soon be better again. They also directed the Assistant Clerk to write a letter couched in sympathetic terms. On inquiring yesterday We were informed that Mr. Mallard is a little better.</p>
        <p>War Savings Associations.—Up to the pre- sent date the Lymm War Savings Association have 'purchased 102 certificates. • This shows remarkable progress, for the scheme has been in progress only a little over two months. The headquarters are at the schools, Pepper-street, and anyone desirous of obtaining information as to how they can help their country and make a profit at the same time are asked to apply at the headqueters at 8 o'clock Saturday night, or to the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. T. .W. Dixon, or the Hon. Sec., Mrs. Thurston. The Rev. Tudor. Evans registered an associa- tion for Oughtrington during the present week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>GRAPPENTIALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENTIALL.</p>
        <p>Choir Sermons at Independent Methodist Church.—On Sunday, at the Grappenhall Inde- pendent Methodist Church, the choir sermons were preached by Mr. Mort, of Lymm. In the morning the anthem "What are these?" was given, and in the evening two anthems were ren- dered, the first being "Shout for joy" (when Messrs. J. Clayton and G. Gough, choirmaster, each contributed a solo), and "The Radiant Morn." The special feature of the day was the cantata entitled "The City of Gold," which was given in the afternoon, when the Mayor Pre- sided. The soloists were: Misses Marion Lewis and Elsie Holt (sopranos) and Miss Olive Bacon (contralto), Messrs. Mason and Arthur Griffiths (tenors), and Mr. Plant (bass). Numerous quar- tets were render4d, the party being Misses 0. Bacon and M. Lewis, and Messrs. Plant and A. Griffiths. Mr. George Gough conducted and Mr. Fred Atkinson very pleasingly accompanied on the organ, Mr. Cyril Clayton being at the piano. The day was a great success, the collections reaching ..t8 16s. 4(1. It is all the more a success when pne realises that the choir is a compara- tively new on, and that this is its first attempt at a cantata.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment. Orders for week ending July Bth, issued by Mr. F. A. Frost, commanding officer:— Monday.-7.15 p.m., Musketry at Rifle Range; 8 p.m. Competition for Society of Miniature Rifle Clubs' Medal. Range Officer, Sect.-Com. 'Fox.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.-7.45 p.m., Company Drill; fall-in at Headquarters.</p>
        <p>Friday.-7.15 p.m., Musketry at Range; 8 p.m., Competition for S.M.R.C. Medal. Range Officer, Sect.-Com. Tipping. Saturday.—Company Drill (Uniform). Meet at Headquarters at 3 p:m. Orderlies.—Sect.-Com. Tipping and Private Jeans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>SWORN IN.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Forces Attest Before Magistrates.</p>
        <p>"I do sincerely, promise and swear that I will be faithful and bear true allegiance to His Majesty King George the Fifth, and that I will faithfully serve His Majesty in Great Britain for the defence of the same against all his enemies and opposers whatsoever, according to the conditions of my service." There was not a single conscientious objector at the Church Room on Thursday evening, when fifty-two patriotic members of the Stockton 'Heath V.T.C. took this oath of allegiance before Mr. F. A. Frost, justice of the peace for the county of Cheshite, and were sworn in as mem- bers of the newly-formed Volunteer Force. There were some seventy members altogether in the old V.T.C., the remaining number being nimble to attend on Thursday, but will be sworn in as opportunity offers. It was very gratifying to find such a good re- sponse to the conditions laid down by the Army Authorities for the Volunteer Forces. All the men expressed their willingness to undertake the duties, which are not irksome to any Volun- teer. The form of enrolment has some dozen questions of a personal nature, which the appli- cant is required to answer, and this document having been filled in, the oath is administered,</p>
