        <p>8</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>UNIONS' DISUNION.</head>
        <p>UNIONS' DISUNION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Guardians 'Shame' Runcorn Rural Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>AN OLD LADY'S NEED.</head>
        <p>AN OLD LADY'S NEED.</p>
        <p>The two neighbouring Boards of Guardians, Warrington and the Runcorn Rural District, both consider they have a grievance against each other,.</p>
        <p>A fortnight ago at Dutton the Chairman of the Runcorn District characterised a certain action of the Warrington Union as "a slur." At Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians the bone of contention was again picked. The circumstances gener- ally are that in the Warrington Union area there is an old lady who is in receipt of ss. per week and who, they think, should receive 10s. A recommendation to this effect was submitted some little time ago to the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Board of Guardians, who are responsible for the payment of relief to her.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. A. ftottomie-Y) recollected to the Warrington Board on Wednesday that owing to the refusal of the Runcorn Distri.tt Guardians to adopt their recommendation (without giving any reasons), they had sub- mitted the matter to the Local Government Board. Since then he (the clerk) had written to the Runcorn District Guardians asking for their reasons why they would not grant this amount, and they had received a reply from the Acting Clerk (Mr. W. E. Hough), who, acting on the instructions of his Board, said they "would be very pleased to supply all the information the Local Government Board required, with respect to their action, but us the Guardians of the Warrington Union hasi complained to the Local Government Board of such action, they did not think the information should be furnished in the way suggested." Mr. Bottomley added that the reason he wrote to the Runcorn Guardians for further particulars was because Mr. Elias, the Local Government Board Inspector, had asked him to supply further information. That he could only obtain from the Runcorn Guardians, whom• he approached.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>"Not A Proper Answer."</head>
        <p>"Not A Proper Answer."</p>
        <p>. - -- • Mr. Bottomley explained that the reason the trouble arose was because in deciding not to grant an increase in any shape or form, their neighbours had not given them any reason for their action. "I think you will agree that that is not a proper answer to give to you as a Board when you are acting on their behalf," commented Mr. Bottomley. He had site° learned that the Runcorn District Board under- stood that the old lady's husband, who resided in Runcorn, was in receipt of 275. to 28s. per week, and that he not infrequently paid visits to his wife and sent her money, but that only made the case worse, for the reason that it was the duty of the Runcorn Guardians to call upon the husband and ask him to repay the money allowed and not call upon the ratepayers of the Runcorn Union to continue doing so. He (Mr. Bottomley) and Mr. Shearer (relieving officer) had called upon the woman, and she assured them that within twelve months she had only received 14s. and that her husband had only been to her on two or three occasions, his business being to try and persuade her to go and live with him. That she refused to do. Mr. Shea‘er had seen the old man at Runcorn and he declared that he was only in receipt of 255. 5d., but proposed to allow his wife 3s. 6d. per week. The old lady was living in a house with a rent of 6s. Bd. per week because she could not get a smaller house. It was wonder- fully clean and respectable, and how she man- aged to live could be judged by the way the furniture was being depleted. '" Shame The Runcorn Guardians."</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) said that these facts ought to at least shame the Run- corn Guardians.</p>
        <p>Mr. Elias, Local Government Board Inspec- tor, who was present, remarked that the ,mat- ter was in the hands of the inspector for the Runcorn district.</p>
        <p>Closing the discussion, the Chairman re- marked that the influence of the Local Government Board had very frequently raised the standard of administration of the Poor Law, and that 'Board lived in hope that the Runcorn Guardians might rise to a little higher conception of their duty, which was to grant adequate pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>VALUABLE WASTE PAPER.</head>
        <p>VALUABLE WASTE PAPER.</p>
        <p>Authorities Advocate Its Systematic Collection. Sir Thomas P. Whittaker, M.P., chairman of the Royal Commission on Paper, in a letter to the Press, points out that, while the shortage of supplies of paper-making materials is causing great inconvenience and seriously increasing the price, a large quantity of waste paper, nor quite valuable, which paper-makers would be glad to buy, is being thrown away or destroyed. Ile has already suggested that local municfloi authorities should organise the collection 01 waste paper, and while something has alreadY been done in some localities there is still large quantity of useful and valuable material lying waste or being destroyed. All that i.a required is that some person or persons in each locality should organise the collecting, sorting, and forwarding of this badly wanted Inaterial Considerable sums may thus be obtained for, deserving local war funds, and he suggests that local authorities and local societies should Task° an effort to do what is necessary.</p>
        <p>That German- Fleet. ' "The German destroyers retire to Pork."'" Provincial paper. " Saving. their bacon."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>DOA</head>
        <p>DOA</p>
        <p>L----Backache Kidney Pi</p>
        <p>and the attestation form at the end signed by the magistrate. Later on, when the certificate of enrolment is completed, the regimental num- ber allotted to each Volunteer will be inserted on the form at the Battalion headquarters. Mr. Frank Warburton, platoon commander, and Mr. H. B. Houghton, hon. secretary of Stockton Heath detachment, attended to the details of the function, which took about an hour and a half. Foot rules and inch tapes were provided for the record of heights and chest measurements, and it must be said that tlie average figures were very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 3rd July, 1916, issued by Mr. Frank Warburton, O.C. Monday..--Drill at Headquarters, Fall-in 7.45.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Members of No. 3 Section aro requesttd to attend at Appleton Rifle Range for Class Firing.</p>
        <p>Thursday.---Recruits' Class at Headquarters, 7.45.</p>
        <p>Saturday.—Uniform Parade. Pall in, Victoria.. square, 3 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Successful Student.—Miss Dorothy Higham, elaest daughter of Kr. William Hignam, Farn- worth-road, Penketh, a former scholar of Pen- keth School and People's College, but now at Widnes Technical School, has gained further honours by winning a bursarship, Class 1., 1916, of the Lancashire Education Committee. Not yet seventeen years of age, and the youngest candidate of her Farm, 68, she has shown ex- ceptional merit in obtaining the requisite num- ber of marks.</p>
        <p>Accident to Penketh Soldier.---Private Walter Foden, R.A.M.C., second son of Mr. John Foden, Warrington-road, arrived home last Saturday, after' seventeen weeks in hospital. Last Febru- ary, while en route for France, an unfortunate slip on board a transport so damaged the nerves of one knee that it incapacitated him up to the present moment from taking any further active part in his country's service. Eleven weeks in Netley, followed by six weeks in Epsom, has, however, worked wonders with him, and soon be will, in all probability, be able to carry out his one set Purpose of helping to succour our wounded warriors. His other brother, Wilfred, a lance-corporal in the Motor Transport of the A.S.C., is still at the Front doing his bit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>PRIVATE A. OWEN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE A. OWEN.</p>
        <p>Suffering From Shell Shock And Bruised Back.</p>
        <p>Private Alfred Owen, third son of the late Mr. Alfred Owen and Mrs. Owen, of The Pollards; Penketh, is lying in a hospital near London suffering from shell shock and a bruised back, received whilst serving with a Canadian Regiment.</p>
        <p>Private Owen, who is a married man, 37 years of age, emigrated to Canada when thirteen years of age, returning after a sojourn of eight years. Two years later, however, he again returned to Canada, where he married. He belonged to the 90th Winnipeg Rifles before the war, some time after the outbreak of which he came over with a Canadian contingent. It was whilst training in this country that he was able to visit Pen- keth. His brother, Captain G. W. Owen, was killed at the Dardanelles in June whilst serv- ing with the Manchester Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Sunday School anniversary (always a notable event in Moore) took place on Sunday, and, as usual on these. occasions, bright and cheery weather prevailed. This enabled a great number of people from some distance to hear their former very able minis- ter, the Rev. F. W. Kernick, who preached two inspiring sermons. All hearts were moved by the preacher's most sympathetic and kindly words regarding the numerous brave defenders Of their country, who had gone from our church and village, and who were now, many of them, remembering these annual services ;.:t home. He then read the long list of names a those with the Colours from the church—the roll of honour—the congregation all standing. The singing of choir and scholars, under the direction of Mr. H. Cartwright, the organist, was a special and impressive feature of the services, and was rendered with much taste and' effect. It was scarcely to be wondered at in these time of almost perpetual demands for money for philanthropic purposes that the offer- tories fell rather below the usual amotint, but they reached £l6 is. 7d.</p>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Orders issued by J. B. Starkey, officer com- manding, week ending July 7th, 1916:</p>
        <p>Parades.</p>
        <p>-- ----- Sunday.—Church • Parade, Christ Church, Latchford. Fall-in at 10 a.m. prompt. A good parade is expected. Tuesday.—Fall-in at 7.30 prompt. Uniform. Semaphore Class, Band Practice. Friday.—Uniform. ' Fall-in at 7.30 prompt, and march-to- a-selected- spot for -a- Tactical Exercise. Officer in charge, Lieut. IL Kitchen. Order'.4es.—Corporal W. Rigby and Lance- Poral X.' Garlick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS</head>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS</p>
        <p>Colonel Arthur Stanleigh and Judge Lamar Stan- leigh, cousins, of Virginia, are bitter enemies over the possession of The Diamond from the Sky, a massive jewel heirloom found centuries ago in a fallen meteor. Colonel Stanleigh, when a daughter is born, buys the male heir of a gipsy king, that he may keep the jewel. He plans to rear Esther, the girl, in secrecy. Esther and The Diamond from the Sky are stolen by Hagar, the gipsy mother. Years later Hagar returns Esther and the diamond, but of the old group only Dr. Lee is living. Blair Stanleigh kills Dr. Lee and steals the jewel. Then Blair Stanleigh and Arthur Stanleigh, the false heir, fight a duel. Arthur, thinking he has killed his cousin, flees in a racing motor car. Arthur is found by Esther and Hagar and nursed back to health. Hagar tells him his real identity. Arthur swears to make himself worthy of Esther, and leaves, taking the diamond with him. Needing money when in Richmond he pawns the diamond and meets Blair Stanleigh. Blair takes him to a „.rgrand ball, where they meet Vivian Marston, a 'evf York belle, wearing tha diamond.' Hagar is there, too, telling fortunes. Vivian has just seated herself when a brawny forearm clutches The Diamond from the Sky.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. FOR THE SAXE OF A FALSE FRIEND,</p>
        <p>As the hand parted the curt Ons and gripped Vivian's neck Hagar jumped forward, but so Quickly was the theft done that she was still some distance away when Vivian, catching her breath With a great gulping sob, shrieked in wild alarm and pain, for the hands that had so mysteriously gripped her fair neck and snatched away the great borrowed jewel had been no gentle ones. In an instant the ballroom was in an uproar, and . Luke Lovell scuttling away in the darkness heard the frenzied whistling for the police. While wait- ing outside Luke had glanced through the window and found his gaze fixed by the blazing jewel. He 41waYs remembered the whispered gipsy gossip that Matt Harding, the dead husband of Hagar, had made the fortune now possessed by his widow by scone bold coup among the aristocrats, a coup of great daring, but one the exact why and way of Which no living gipsy now save Hagar knew. Here, thought Luke, was his chance for fortune. He was quick to follow out the evil impulse. He gained the street with the idea of hiding the diamond in the first safe nook or cranny he could ThLI, and then return to bear the brunt of suspicion &amp;ltd. of search. It would be difficult now to carry out the intended programme. Policemen were corning in all directions. Luke ran first one way and then the other, all to no purpose. Whichever way he looked or moved a police officer was run- ning. Luke was trapped. There was an iron mail box on a lamp post. Quick as thought he dropped the jewel with its locket and chain into the mail hoz and ran towards the policeman shouting, "He crossed over there and went through that hedge." "Oh, no, he didn't; that's Much too thin a yarn," Said the officer. "I've got him here. Come along, Wilson, here's the man they want." Together the officers dragged the protesting Luke to the Ran- ;lola house, and up the steps into the entrance all amongst the guests, all chattering and pro- '''•runding theories. •I,uke insisted upon his innocence, and Hagar I3llolod for her man. Luke also insisted upon Ine,7lg searched. The search was made, but the if -Bei4g jewel was not found. As fate would have .v an even greater contretemps was to occur, an " roore unenviable notoriety was to attend Rich- ji:2°nd's most fashionable function. The sheriff of _airfax appeared in the doorway, accompanied by Uetective Tom Blake. "I want Arthur Stanleigh over there for the murder of Dr. Henry Lee, of Pairfax," cried the sheriff. The face of Blair Stanleigh blanched. "Remem- ber your promise, Arthur," he whispered. "You cannot go to the gallows for me. You must tell the truth if you are tried. But you can save me if You escape." Arthur nodded and made a bold bid for freedom. Right at the sheriff he charged. With a blow Blake knocked him down, but he was Up again and fighting like a wild cat. The terrific struggle swept through the ballroom right to the front door. Arthur was being slowly but surely overpowered by Blake and the sheriff when Hagar, realising that it was better Arthur should escape, Dulled Blake away and gave him a significant sign. 'raking advantage of the pause Arthur wrenched taking loose, broke through the ring of his would- e Captors, and was gone—gone for the sake of a false friend.. Straight for the railroad he made, and the pursuing police were in time to see him ?lanaber into a van of an outgoing goods train. The %Wye, seeing that he had been tracked, jumped out, and eluded his pursuers by boarding a train g°ing in an opposite direction. a Ifeanwhile what had become of the The Diamond '.Forn the Sky? Bob Adams. mailman, was doing Iluty clearing mail-boxes. As he drove up to the I'ox near Mrs. Randolph's he heard sounds of a considerable commotion. With head averted in the ,f.iirection of the noises he automatically cleared the ecr without noticing that the Stanleigh "charm itgainst harm" was among its contents. The col- ection completed. Bob returned to the post office, and the cobtents of his bags were dumped upon the je:Muters. With deft and practised rapidity the es were cleared, the emptied mail bags being Ved upon the floor. The inspector went over 4fero, marking with a piece of chalk Repair" on '4;l Be needing mending and renovation. All night `606 bags for repair lay in a heap on the floor. t,2 Three knights of the road were in possession of 6 van into which Arthur hurriedly climbed. They nre considerably astonished at seeing a stranger evening dress adopting their particular method of travelling. l,"elt's a plant, Strap," whispered one of the '7-e to his mate. .3 'snatcher mean, a plant?" asked the one ad- %tressed as Strap. Can't you see? It's a railroad 'tec. Would any "rx% .our crowd be wearing the 'fish and soup'?" znting to Arthur's dirty evening dress. Garn, can't you see them rags is hissen? They e dirty, but they fits him." .. " believe you are right, Scotty," said Strap eceerotnotne.comev</p>
        <p>)</p>
        <p>-QQ—c.l-74---, c2l-</p>
        <p>McGee. the leader of the trio, and without ado he struck Arthur • behind the ear, and the next in- stant the young man was fighting desperately with the three tramps. Against such odds Arthur could not stand long. In the empty freight car as it sped along the com- bat was soon over. Spent, worn and battered, Arthur lay bound en the floor of the freight car. Having taken his money, the tramps stripped Arthur and proceeded to gamble for his clothes. They were won by the more fortunate Mr. Strap McGee, more fortunate in the fact that the dice were his and he knew how to manipulate them. Bruised and battered as he was, Arthur coiild not restrain a laugh at the ludicrous aspect the tramp leader presented in the dirty and dishevelled even- ing clothes, with his own old hat jauntily stuck on his head. The goods train stopped at a water tank and the three tramps slipped off with many caustic remarks at parting. Arthur freed himself from his bonds, and with some repugnance donned the frowsy gar- ments of the tramp. Then he. too, sprang from the car and made through a cornfield across the coun- try. He reflected, with a smile, that the actions of a tramp would be his salvation. He was right in this, for shortly afterwards Mr. Strap McGee, despite his indignant protests and explanations, was seized by railroad detectives and held on a telegraphic description of his attire as " Arthur Stanleigh, wanted for murder." At sunset a good-looking young tramp stopped in the dooryard of Robert Smith's farm and asked for work. The. farmer put him to chopping wood for his supper, and so well did he acquit himself and so soon did he gain the good graces of the farmer's wife and the farmer's little girl that, despite the rags of his attire, " John Powell "—for such was the name Arthur gave—was greeted in the morning, after a restful night in the haymow, with a proffer of steady employment. Where was The Diamond from the Sky? Held by its clasp in the ravellings of the inner seam at the bottom of the bag, the heirloom of the Stan- leighs rested in the darkness of the soiled interior of the service-worn old mail-bag. Down the city street from his humble lodging- place came Quabba. With him was his orchestra and his " collector of external revenue." True, his orchestra was but a barrel organ, and his collector a monkey, but the organ was a fairly good one, ,1 sweet of tune, and the monkey was a simian of sorts, and his name was Clarence. So it was that Quabba, gay of heart, sang to himself as he trudged along. Only a poor hunch- back organ-grinder with his monkey was Quabba. But his heart was light, his conscience untroubled, and his appetite, alas! only too good. The whole wide world was his, and no man was his master. and so Quabba, the hunchback, sang' and winked at the monkey, Clarence, as if to say. "'We haven't a penny, Clarence, but what an appetite we'll have for breakfast as soon as we pick up the price of one."</p>
        <p>Sitting down to munch a crust, which he was sharing with Clarence, Quabba heard a truck com- ing down the street. A jolt of the vehicle threw an empty mail-bag marked with chalk "Repair!" from a pile of those at the back of the truck.</p>
        <p>The hunchback picked up the mail-bag and called after the driver. But that worthy failed to hear the cry, and Quabba waved the old mail-bag after him. Then he felt something the size of a walnut in his hand under the dirty canvas of the bag. His sensitive fingers felt along the side seam of the bag, and he could feel, even through the thickness of the canvas, that the object in the bag was a chain and locket. The hunchback wheeled into a nearby alley, between two warehouses. No one had seen the mail-bag fall from the truck, none noticed him turn up the deserted alley. It was only the work of an instant to turn the mail-bag inside out. There, on the inside, hung the diamond, its upper clasp en- gaged in the seam of the bottom of the bag. The itinerant musician thrust the diamond and its chain into his bosom and popped the mail-bag into a dustbin. Then, his heart beating high, Quabba winked to the monkey and ceased whistling to whisper: "We are rich now, Clarence." That same night, by the wayside, Quabba and Clarence camped beneath a tree. Again Quabba showed his companion the diamond, and again he said to the monkey. "We are rich men now, Clar- ence!" Then he placed it in his breast and the monkey snuggled close to him and they fell asleep, and had such dreams as man and monkey may. For Clarence it was a restless night. Maybe his dreams were disturbing. The fact, however, stands on record that in the early dawn he awoke. What was that his master had shown him with such pride? It looked good to eat, but his teeth found it hand when he tasted it. Any way, the find over which Quabba chuckled so much was worth closer examination, and carefully searching for it Clar- ence found the diamond in its hiding-place, and without waking his master, climbed the tree be- neath which they had spent the night.</p>
        <p>Up the tree he could examine his find undis- turbed. Had the expedition been carried through as Clarence planned this story of the diamond's wanderings would have had another set of adven- tures. But the monkey was irritable. He knew he was doing wrong, and was anxious to finish in- vestigations before his master awoke. Poor Clar- ence ran foul of an angry owl. The bird brooding her eggs was annoyed by the chattering of the early morning visitor, and pecked at him. That decided him. He would pay out the bird by stealing an egg and leaving the diamond in its place. Fair exchange, he might have thought, was no robbery. Then he felt a tug at his chain. His master was awake, and, perhaps for the first time in his life, Clarence realised the advantage of no understand- able speech. Something was plainly the matter down below. Quabba was yanking viciously at the</p>
        <p>chain. "Clarence. you imp, have you taken the diamond?" The monkey chattered an indignant denial. "Well, it has gone, and I believe you had it. ,Help me to search." But Clarence was too much upset. So enraged was he that he would not help the frantic Quabba to search high and low upon the ground for the missing jewel, so soon found, so soon lost again.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
