      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BO WLING.</head>
        <p>BO WLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Club Championship.—Second Round.</p>
        <p>Two return games were down for decision on Saturday, viz., Rylands's Recs. v. Senior Conserva- tives and Latchford Conservatives v. Junior Con- servatives. Rylands's had lost by 79 points the previous week, and now, on their own green, were looking forward to just gettlng their own back. It was a tall order, however, to wipe off a deficit of 79, and they could not manage it. Yet they bowled very well and only allowed the visitors to return four winning cards, Rylands's finally topping the bill by 52 points. Thus on the home and home games the Conservatives were the victors by 27 points.</p>
        <p>The game between J. Newton, of Rylands's, and A. Broady was particularly interesting, although almost from the start the former showed superiority, going from one to fifteen points without ,his oppo- nent making any addition to -his original three. Thenceforward, however, the players both pro- ceeded steadily, but Broady was unable to lessen the distance between them, and the game finished 21-12 in Newton's favour.</p>
        <p>W. Barnes, of the Conservatives, peeled with G. Monks at three, but then left him well, and was ten while his opponent stood at six. Monks, how- ever, got a run of four and got level. They also peeled at twelve, and one suspects that Monks was hopeful of gaining his end, but Barnes' luck was in, and he ran out victor by 21-17.</p>
        <p>D. Richardson, of the Conservatives, made a splendid recovery against J. Woods. With only one point to his credit when Woods had nine, he regis- tered seven before Woods could make any advance. Woods then had another spell, however, and was six ahead at eighteen, but Richardson stuck man- fully to the game, peeled at twenty, and then, on a narrow end, lost by the odd point.</p>
        <p>S. Massey, of Rylands's, fairly trounced W. Pritchard by 21-7, whilst T. Clare also scored an easy victory, 21-9. J. Langley indicted almost as severe a defeat upon W. .Millington, the respective scores being 21-10. This was a little surprising, for there was only a difference of one when :the game was half through.</p>
        <p>H. Haslam, of Rylands's, had a great occasion for rejoicing. His opponent, T. Brookes, only scored two points in the whole game. All except one of the heavy defeats were inflicted on the visiting team. The game in which the Conservatives showed up well was that between J. Lodge and J. Gleave, their player starting with a rush and finishing 21-7.</p>
        <p>By the narrow margin of one point Latchford Conservatives proved victorious over the Junior Conservatives at Latchford. The first-named team were 94 points behind from the previous Saturday's match, so that their chances were anything but rosy. The match was a very interesting one, close games being the order throughout, as may be judged from the score of 213 points to 212.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECREATION v. SENIOR CONSERVATIVES. At Gorsey-lane on Saturday. Scores:—</p>
        <p>Rylands's,</p>
        <p>Conservatives.</p>
        <p>I. Newton G. Monks 5. Woods S. Massey J. Langley 5. Gleave</p>
        <p>R. Tilling W. Cottam .. ...... .. 16 E. Gleave W. Roughley ........ 21 W. Thomason H. Langley -........ 21 J. Spilsbury H. Haslam T. Clare</p>
        <p>.224 Rylands's won by 52 ooints.</p>
        <p>On the home and home games Senior Conservatives won by 27 points. •</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CONSERVATIVES v. JUNIOR</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVES.</p>
        <p>At Latchford on Saturday. Scores:— Latchford. Junior Conservatives. W. Caldwell 21 R. Holbrook . 18 H. Green ..... 2l J. Monks J. Orford . ...... 18 J. Jolley</p>
        <p>G. Harrison S. Bradshaw A. Knight A. Houghton R. Savory H. Garton J. Ashnrst ' 21 T Finch</p>
        <p>F. Cooper A. Jones .</p>
        <p>21 D. Clayton 11 F. Lashford</p>
        <p>Latchford Conservatives won by 1 point. On the home and home games the Junior Conserva-</p>
        <p>tives won by 93 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS AMATEUR</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS AMATEUR</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>" Finlayson " Cup Competition. KEEN PLAY AND CLOSE FINISHES. There were some very close games in the con- tinuation of the first round of the " Finlayson " Cup Competition on Saturday, so close indeed that the highest victory of the day was only one of twelve points, while others were of two points, and one game ended in a draw.</p>
        <p>The first teams on the green were Whitecross Net- ting and Rylands's No. 1. The first card handed in was in favour of Rylands's. W. Cottam having beaten T. Jackson rather easily 15-8. A close game was seen between R. Tilling (Rylands's) and J. Heneghan. Play was very even up to the eight mark, when the players peeled, and from then to the end. Tilling was only once behind, and finally won 15-10. W. Roughley and W. Savory also fought a stiff game. At the fifth end RoughleY was in arrears 3-9, but he kept quite cool, and ran out a good winner, 15-12.</p>
        <p>In the Longford Wire, No. 2 v. Howley Tannery match E. Kearns gained an easy victory of 15-6 over F. Clare. E. Aspinall (Howley) fought a very keen game with J. Wall, the final- score being 15-14- in Aspinall's favour. Both men started with an ad- vantage of six, and at one period of the game Aspinall was leading 13-6. Wall, however, kept manfully to his guns, and at 13 and 14 peeled with his opponent; only to see the latter run in the win- ning wood. Close finishes were witnessed in the same match between J. Simister and W. Ditchfield, and J. Clare and M. Gilligan, the scores being 15--13 in each case.</p>
        <p>In the Crosfield's V.B. Department v. Crosfield's Soap Department match, T. Holcroft and W. Law- ton fought a very even game. They peeled at eleven, but by means of two singles and a double Lawton "got home" by the odd chalk. By a similar score P. Wright triumphed over F. Little- wood.</p>
        <p>J. Bowyer (Penketh Tannery) triumphed by one point over W. Taylor (Naylor's). The last-named player was in arrears at one point, of the game 9-4, but he played steadily afterwards and peeled at 14, only to see his opponent obtain the necessary singlo and win.</p>
        <p>The most pronounced victory of the afternoon was that of W. Ashton (Locker's) over W. Randles (Crosfield's, Cheshire). The first-named player had a start of five, and obtained his fifteen in eight ends, whilst his opponent was making one. M. Llewellyn (Locker's) was just beaten 14-15, as also was A. Payne (Crosfield's, Cheshire).</p>
        <p>Tu the Greening's Weaver's v. Longford Wire No. 1 match, C. Holt (Longford), after being in arrears 12-4 against W. Alcock, made a fine re- covery, and finally accounted for his opponent 15-14. By the 'same score W. Clare (Longford) beat H. Hughes.</p>
        <p>W. Dutton (Rylands's) beat J. Speight (Lord Derby War Hospital) 15-7, the last-named player only adding two to his start of eve. Good vic- tories were gained for the Hospital, however, by Rowlands, Harper, Warburton, Hambleton and Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
