      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>IN THE COluairs.</head>
        <p>IN THE COluairs.</p>
        <p>FINED £lOO.</p>
        <p>Well-known Merchant And</p>
        <p>Unsound Currants.</p>
        <p>The full penalty of £lOO was imposed at the Borough Police Court on Thursday on Mr. William Scott, Victoria-road, Grappenhall; merchant, for removing food which was unfit- for human consumption, contrary to the pro- visions of the Unsound Food Regulations in operation under the Manchester Port Authority. There was a similar summons against Mrs. Scott, but the case was withdrawn. The magistrates sitting were Mr. J. Rymer Young (chairman), Dr. Naden and Mr. A. J. Jolley. Mr. W. Greaves Lord (instructed by Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., Warrington), represent- ing Mr. Scott, said his client admitted the offence.</p>
        <p>Dr. T. Eastham, who (instructed, by Mr. Arthur Holmes, Salford), appeared for the com- plainant. Dr. William Francis Dearddn, M. 0.11., Manchester Port Sanitary Authority, explained how the prosecution was brou-glit. The defendant had removed a large number of cases of currants contrary to the order of the Medical Officer of Health. The case was one of great public importance. The legislature made certain regulations to prevent unsound food being distributed to the public, and these precautions were very necessary. The Man- chester Port Authority had jurisdiction over all the wharves on the Ship Canal, and could make restrictions and issue detention orders. Having referred to the section of the Act, Counsel said that on the 10th December one of the food inspectors examined a very large con- signment of currants at Trafford, which con- sisted of 779 quarter and 213 half cases. lie opened a number of them and found the cur- rants were very wet—most of the cases were saturated with water and he discovered that the lighter that brought them up the canal had a serious leak, and that the currants had been saturated by dirty canal water. They were unfit for human consumption. He took a sample to the medical officer, who issued a detention notice. The consignees rejected the consignment and left the currants at the docks. On the 3rd` January the defendant appeared on the scene, and on the 10th January showed the medical officer a sample of the damaged cur- rants, which he had treated by a process of steaming. The medical officer thought that steaming would hardly be a proper method, as it would not get the dirty water or microbes out of the currants, but that if they were treated by running water and dried quickly by heated air some of them might be saved. But in no case might they be removed unless the inspector knew of the process carried out and had the opportunity of examining the currants before they were put out for human consumption. Defendant's Undertaking.</p>
        <p>Counsel then read some letters from defenda at in which he undertook not to remove the cur- rants without notifying the medical authority. But the cases were moved on the 14th and 15th February to Warrington, where they were traced by the inspector. The medical officer subsequently found that some of the currants had been sold to a firm at Leigh and some sent to Liverpool. It was a clear breach of the regulations. It was a very, serious case, be- cause the consignment was one of 29 tons lf currants, and had it not been for the measures 'taken by the inspectors and by the medical officer they would have been distributed and used by the public. He asked for an ex- emplary penalty. Mr. Greaves Lord did not think the ease called for exemplary punishment, but for ordinary justice. Mr. Scott freely admitted he was wrong, and he thought there were mitigating circumstances. He had done his best to 1;4 proper plant made for dealing with these cur- rants' but the firm to whom he gave the order I were engaged with munitions. With regard to 'what he had disposed of, he had got an under- taking that the currants would be dealt with in the manner required by the medical officer. Currants were very valuable as food—a fact which the Government had recognised, and Mr. .Scott had used some of the consignment for six weeks in his own household. The magistrates deliberated in private, and then the Chairman said they considered the offence so serious that they had decided to- impose the full penalty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>WHAT IS SOCIETY LIFE?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS SOCIETY LIFE?</p>
        <p>Rag Gatherers Have A Tilt At Wiredrawers.</p>
        <p>A sentence of two months' imprisonment each was imposed upon James and Mary Lee at the Borough Police Court on Thursday, for having. assaulted a man named Ashton, of EgertOn- street, on Sunday night, in Brook-street. They had also been guilty of disorderly conduct. De- fendants live in Howley-lane. Acting-Sergeant Hayes said that the female prisoner used language of a disgraceful kind, and when he saw Mr. Ashton the latter had a cut about an inch long on his shin and bruises on his right arm. A crowd of some 200 people had gathsred in Brook-street. Rev. H. Ogden, 31, Orford-road, a Baptist minister, who was cycling along the street° where defendants live, said Mrs. Lee, who was in her doorway, was using the most fear- ful, foul and horrible language. Witness con- cluded "I don't know that I can say any more." _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lee: You've said enough. (Laughter.) Mr. Ashton said that While he was talking to someone in the street Mrs. Lee called him a bobby's spy, and hit him with a shoe on the shoulder. Her husband came running out in his bare feet and said "Cut off," at the same time calling to Mrs. Lee, -" Fetch me the - poker." Witness went away. Lee followed him to Brook-street, up-ended him and cut his elbow and' shin.</p>
        <p>The male prisoner said that some of the witnesses were put up for the job. Because they (defendants) were only rag-gatherers and the others were wiredrawers, the • latter thought they were in Society. (Laughter.) Sentences as above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>CONSCRIPT'S COURAGE FAILS.</head>
        <p>CONSCRIPT'S COURAGE FAILS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, at the Borough Police Court, a sturdy-looking and respectably dressed young man, named John Richard Millar, of 12, Nora- Ntreet, gas range fitter, was charged by Detee-</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>11)11,E'l ON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>Monthly Competitions.</p>
        <p>ORTHOPTIC SIGHTS.</p>
        <p>Competition I.—Ten shots deliberate at 25 yards. Score. Ireap. Total. *A. Shepherd J. Towers</p>
        <p>*J: F. Ferguson * Tie for first three places.</p>
        <p>Competition 2.—Ten shots deliberate at 50 yards. Score. H'cap. Total. *J. F. Ferguson *L. R. Rowson 94 4 98 tJ. Towers tW. Howard</p>
