      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>OPEN SIGHTS.</head>
        <p>OPEN SIGHTS.</p>
        <p>Competition I.—Ten shots deliberate at N.R.A. Figure targets, 25 yards range, open sights. Score. H'cap. Total. A. Shepherd R. M. Swinton E. L. Robinson Competition 2.—Ten shots deliberate at 50 yards, open sights.</p>
        <p>Score. H'cap. Total. J. H. Fletcher *R. M. Swinton *L. R. Rowson * Tie for ;:ccond and third place.</p>
        <p>tive Anderson with failing to report himself for military service. The officer stated that that he accosted defendant and asked him if he had attested, and he replied " No." He admitted that he got his papers, but that "his courage failed him." lie should have reported in April last. Defendant was fined 40s. and handed over to the military authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>LOW LANGUAGE HIGH TONED.</head>
        <p>LOW LANGUAGE HIGH TONED.</p>
        <p>When Mrs. Winifred Cullerton, of 3, Lither- land's-yard, was rebuked by Inspector Forster for using filthy language in her residence in such a high tone of voice that it could be heard in an adjoining thoroughfare, she turned upon the officer with some choice phrases for his par- ticular benefit. Defendant denied having used the _language complained of, but the magis- trates at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday. imposed a fine pf five shillings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>CASE FOR "JOHN BULL!"</head>
        <p>A CASE FOR "JOHN BULL!"</p>
        <p>Mr. Peter Longton, farmer, Winwick, was summoned at the Borough Court on Thursday for not having a light on his trap in Winwick- road, Police Constable Taylor stating that de- fendant had a lighted lamp in his hand. De- fendant said the lamp was good enough for a motor car, but apparently not so for a trap. When fined 10s. he remarked, "It's good enough for 'John Bull,' this is."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>HOMING PIGEONS.</head>
        <p>HOMING PIGEONS.</p>
        <p>A lad named- Harold Willcock, 24, Orford- lane, was fined ss. at the Borough Court on Thursday for having ten homing pigeons which he had failed to register. He had told Inspec- tor Forster that he thought the regulations only referred to prize pigeons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PLAYING BANKER.</head>
        <p>PLAYING BANKER.</p>
        <p>Charged by Constable Taylor with playing the game of cards called " banker" for money in Greenall-street on Sunday, three youths named Peter Ifilton, 15, Brook-street; William Newell, 13, Willington-street; and John Over- ton, 1, Greenall-street, were each mulcted Jn five shillings.</p>
        <p>OFFENDING MOTORISTS.</p>
        <p>John Jackson, 46, Birchall-street, who had no</p>
        <p>light on a motor cycle while riding through Sankey-street after lighting-up time, was promptly hekl up by Police Constable Taylor, who made the further discovery that defendant had no licence in respect of the machine. He had a licence, but it bore another person's name. Fines of 15s. were imposed in respect of both offences at the Borough rourt on Thursday. For driving a, motor car along Sankey-strek on market day at 6.30 p.m., at a. speed of about twenty miles an hour, John T. Blackburn, Padgate-lane, was fined '2os. at the same Court. A still further case coming under the category of motoring illegalities was that in which Special Inspector Mason showed commendable activity. The officer was riding his bicycle along the Manchester-road when Joseph Smith, of 33, Spark-street, Liverpool, 1910 was driving a motor lurry, swept past him at the rate of 18 miles an hour, much to the, peril of pedestrians crossing the roadway.—Defendant was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>STRINGENT LIGHT REGULATIONS.</head>
        <p>STRINGENT LIGHT REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>For allowing a brilliant light to shine through a fanlight at his residence, 50, Wilders- pool-road, and which drew the attention of Police 'Constable Bartley, Charles Bickerstaffe was fined 10s. at the Borough Court 'on'Thurs- day. A• similar penalty was imposed upon Thomas Prince, Horsemarket-street, in whose business premises Acting-Sergeant Whitley saw an incandescent light burning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>MONEYLESS AND CARDLESS.</head>
        <p>MONEYLESS AND CARDLESS.</p>
        <p>Sentence of three months' hard labour was passed at the Borough Police Court yesterday on Arthur Smith Clough, of no settled abode, who was charged with begging, and also with failing to produce a National Registration Card. Superintendent Williamson said that the prisoner had given his age as 29, but the Lon- don police had got his age as 52. He had been convicted in all parts of the, country for beg- ging and stealing.</p>
