      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON XI. V. EARLESTOWN 2nd.</p>
        <p>The Warrington XI. commenced their programme in decisive fashion last Saturday, defeating a strong Earlestown 2nd team by 121 runs. In fielding, bowl- ing and batting the XI. gave a sound display. The committee are quite satisfied with results, and hope with a little more practice to give the stiffer pro- positions a good game.</p>
        <p>- The batting honours were shared by W. H. Bell (29), F. Tole (y), G. Mann (19), F. Lemon (10, B. Taylor (14), G. Duckworth (12), and J. W. Travis (12). The latter was dismissed by a bril- liant piece of fielding, A. Sanders stopping a hard drive and hooking the ball into his hand with his foot at the second attempt. W. IL Bell (5 for 16) and W. Bennett (5 for 19) bowled finely, and the home team's captain, H. Mayer (19), was the only batsman to present any opposition.</p>
        <p>The feature of the match, however, was the promising display given by G. Duckworth, the diminutive son of A. J. Duckworth, the well-known Warrington wicket-keeper. This sixteen-year-old youngster kept wicket like a veteran, and opening the innings with W. H. Bell gave the side an en- couraging start, scoring 30 runs for the first wicket, when he was adjudged to be 1-b-w. He received his training at the Boteler Grammar School. Scores:— Earlestown.—A. Smith c and b Bell 4, H. Marsh b Bennett 0, H. Mayer c Taylor b Bell 19, A. San- ders c Taylor b Bennett 4, J. Riley c Taylor b Ben- nett 0, R. Thane b Bell 0, E. Sanders c Bennett b Bell 0, H. Hodgson b Bennett 4. W. Houghton c Taylor b Bell 0. W. Bates not out 4, Gee c Allen b Bennett 0, extras 2; total 37. Warrington.—W. H. Bell c and b Smith 29, G. Duckworth. 1-13-w b Mayer 12. G. Mann c Gee b Marsh 19, F. Lemon c Sanders b Thane 18. F. Tole c Bates b Riley'-24;-T. 'Allen-c- Mayer-b-Bates 7, W. Bennett c and b Bates 8. B. Taylor c Bates b Hodgson 14, J. W. Travis c Sanders b Riley 12, H. Warburton c Gee b Riley 1, J. Constable not out 0, extras 14; total 158.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. WARP.INGTON XI. v. LYMM MILITARY HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. WARP.INGTON XI. v. LYMM MILITARY HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>This match will be played at Lymm to-day, corn mencing at 3 p.m.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—J. Constable (captain), W. H. Bell, F. Lemon, B. Taylor, W. Bennett, T. Allen, G. Mann, J. W. Travis, W. Howard, G. Duckworth, F. Tole and T. Knight. Train leaves Bank Quay at 2.26 p.m. Players meet at 2.15 p.m. 'Umpire. S. W. Holden.</p>
        <p>SERGT. STANLEY PARTRIDGE BOWLS WELL.</p>
        <p>Playing with the 21st King's Liverpool Regiment against the 22nd Battalion on Wednesday, Sergt. Stanley Partridge, of Warrington, obtained three wickets for 37 runs—this despite the injuries to the right shoulder he has just recovered from. The 22nd scored 114 runs, and the 21st 125 for two wickets, The Sergeant was eight not out when the game was declared closed and won.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>"A GREAT BURDEN."</head>
        <p>"A GREAT BURDEN."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Smith, M.P.'s, Hopeful Reply</p>
        <p>To Guardians' Resolution.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. along with the Prime Minister and the Presidents of the Board of Trade and Local Government Board, recently received a resolution from the Warrington Boarg of Guardians remarking on the increased cost of living, and hoping that the Government would endeavour to ease the situation as it was causing a. great drain on the ratepayers. Formal acknowledgments were received from all excepting Mr. Smith, who said: "I have to acknowledge receipt of your letter of the 14th inst., and to express my regret at the delay in replying thereto. This delay is partly explained by the fact that I knew that the Government contemplated taking some definite step, and. pending their decision, I postponed writing. I hope that the committee of inquiry will speedily conclude their labours and will be able to make recommendations which may result in reducing the great burden which the high price of food imposes on the people and on the Poor Law Guardians."</p>
      </div>
