      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Son's Sad Story Of Home Life.</head>
        <p>Son's Sad Story Of Home Life.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND COMMITTED FOR TRIAL FOR</p>
        <p>MANSLAUGHTER.</p>
        <p>Jury Expresses Sym</p>
        <p>Mrs. Katherine Robina Cunningham Picken, forty-six years of age, 183, Wellfield-street, wife of William Picken (53), ironworker, succumbed in the Infirmary shortly after noon on Wed- nesday, in circumstances which formed the sub- ject of a Coroner's inquest yesterday. At the Borough Police Court on Monday the husband was remanded for a week on a charge of wounding, but yesterday, the graver accusa- tion of feloniously causing his wife's death was entered against him. A verdict of manslaughter was returned by the Coroner's jury yesterday, their finding ex- pressing " regret " that they had no alterna- tive, and stating that they had "deep sympathy with the husband in the conditions of the case.' Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, conducted the inquiry, and the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Alderman Smethurst and Chief Con- stable Nicholls were present. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for Picken, and said it was not the intention of the latter to give evidence at the inquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>SON'S SAD STORY.</head>
        <p>SON'S SAD STORY.</p>
        <p>The first witness was Francis Picken, puddler, aged 24, who, with his wife, also lives at 183, Wellfield-street. He said that last Satur- day, the 24th ult., he- came home about five o'clock. His .mother, brother, father and wife were in the kitchen. His mother was lying )n a sofa and his father sitting at the table about three yards away from her. He could not say if his father was under the influence of drink, but his mother seemed to be drunk. Witness was in the house about five or seven minutes when his mother, addressing his father, said, "If you go to see William (another son at Walsall) you will do him no good." His father replied, "You are doing a lot for your children, the condition you are getting in." His mother then started using .bad language and called his father an "Old Gorman." The latter retorted, "You act more like a German than me: why don't you do ..something for your country and keep steady ?"1(' His mother used foul names, got up from the sofa, and taking a tin box from the table, Said, I will knock your face in with this" and rushed towards his father. The box had originally held about a pound of tobacco. Witness took the box from her and pushed his mother back to the sofa. His father, at this time was standing with his back to the fire. Witness Said to his mother, "Why don't you be quiet?;' You are upsetting all of us; every day alike." His mother took up the box again and ran towards his father. The latter took hold of the poker, held it across his face, and struck the box from her hand. The poker also struck her on the head.</p>
        <p>The weapon (produced) is a heavy piece of iron over two feet long, and the tobacco box was dented at the side.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Was that the only time the poker was used F—Yes. Did your father say anything after the poker had hit your mother?—He said "Oh !" Witness added that his mother went towards the stairs, and fell in filo lobby. He went to her assistance. She was bleeding from the right side of the head. He put her on the sofa, got a cloth and put it round her lead. She tore it off, went upstairs and locked herself in a bedroom. Dr. Nadu was brought to the house, and when his mother was told the doctor was there she shouted back, " the doctor." They did not think she was so bad, and left her there. Not getting any answer the next morning the door was broken open and they found her lying on the floor. His father "sent for Dr. Adams, and his mother was taken to the Infirmary. The Coroner: From the Saturday night when this occurred did your father ever say to you that he did not intend to hit her on the head, but to hit the box?—No. The Chief Constable: Was there any need to take up a formidable weapon like that to knock a little tin box out of her hand ?—She was rush- ing at 'him. Witness added that when he told the police that his father raised the ,poker when his mother ran at him first he was upset.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>AN HABITUAL DRUNKARD.</head>
        <p>AN HABITUAL DRUNKARD.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Steel : His mother had not been a very temperate woman, and was in the same condition on Saturday last as she had been for many weeks past. She had been drinking heavily for seven or eight years. His father was employed as iron-roller at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co. for about nine years, and was held in respect by all the employes there. His father had obtained a separation order on the ground that his wife was an habitual diunkard. That order was granted about two years ago. She begged to come back, and said she would be different. In consequence of her drunken habits some of the children had left home. His moth.3r started the conversation when his father was reading a book at the table. His mother was in a violent temper. His father was very much upset, and, when she went upstairs, he sat down on a chair with his head between his hands.</p>
        <p>In reply to further questions, witness said that when they saw his mother on the Sunday morning she leaned up on her elbow and started to undress herself. They got some brandy and his father helped to undress her and did all he could for her. . _</p>
        <p>The tforoner: Where did you get the brandy from on a Sunday morning?—We had some in the house.</p>
        <p>—„ Another son, Horace Picken, aged 16, gave similar evidence, and when the Coroner asked why his father did not get hold of his mother's wrists, said he had to do soMething, very quickly to prevent her from striking him with the box. A daughter of deceased who saw her 'mother in the Infirmary on Wednesday, said she was unconscious the whole time, and died at 12.35 o'clock. By Mr. Steel: In consequence of her mother's drunken habits she had to lea' home. THE DOCTOR'S EVI DENC E.</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox, who made the post-mortem examina- tion, deposed that death was due to fraCture of the skull and injury to the brain. The cut on the scalp was about three inches long, reaching from about the forehead to the level of the ear. There was a fracture of the skull extending for 51 inches, a clot of blood over the brain, and the brain was bruised. There was also lacera- tion of the brain about three inches long. Dr. Fox was handed the poker, and said it would not require a very heavy blow, because</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>pathy With Him.</head>
        <p>pathy With Him.</p>
        <p>the implement was so heavy in itself that it would not need much violence. If it fell on the head it would be sufficient to cause the in- juries. Dr. Binns corroborated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>PICKEN AND THE SECOND CHARGE.</head>
        <p>PICKEN AND THE SECOND CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Detective Anderson stated that when he saw William Picken at Wellfield-street on 25th June and said it was alleged he hit his wife on the head he replied, "I am sorry to say I hit her on the head; but you know what sort • f woman she is." He had charged him that morning with feloniously causing the death of his wife, and Picken replied, "I shall say nothing at present." By Mr. Steel: Witness served a summons t Mrs. Picken in August, 1914. Her husband was applying to the Justices for a separation order. She was then in a drunken state.</p>
        <p>The Coroner, in directing the jury, said they should consider as to whether there was sufficient provocation for the husband acting in self-defence in such a way as to justify him in using the heavy poker as a weapon of defence. The jury unanimously returned a verdict as stated, and the Coroner put their findings into the usual form.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>PICKEN COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</head>
        <p>PICKEN COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>Addressing Picken, who had been brought into Court in custody, the Coroner explained that the jury had found hinA responsible for the death of his wife, and had added a vote of sympathy with him, saying they had no alternative. _</p>
        <p>The Coroner then committed him for trial to the next Liverpool Assizes on a charge of man- slaughter, and said if the magistrates were pre- pared to accept bail, he (the Coroner) would receive bail in the same amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>GOOD NEWS OF MR. DAVY.</head>
        <p>GOOD NEWS OF MR. DAVY.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Davy, of Sherwood, Wylde Green, Warwickshire, have received news through the British Red Cross Society that their son, Lieutenant Davy, who was officially reported missing about a month ago, is safe and has rejoined his unit, Mr. Davy was formerly works' manager at Messrs. Richmond's, War- rington.</p>
