        <p>CYWAL=S22ALL,..=. '. ' , ).' J t i q• . V I 114 if ~, iE. , p . f 0 -4' . Ats„ ,i'' .$ ' . / wt). 4.. ) I 0 r. j'' c • ,P4' I P .4 i• , .), ' . A # &amp;• ....14— li:; j il4, , 0 : a .• ^ , • Extract from the t • Warrington Guardian, ' June 24th, 1916 At Runcorn Rural,, District Tribunal, •r,,,0 • Mr. H. J. Westbrook applied for the conditional exemption of Mr. Charles t f J uckes, a partner in the County ; Motor Co., of Stockton Heath. He • ; . presented a strong appeal signed by . i nearly all the local medical men urging that it was advisable, in the ' • intere6ts of the community, that Mr: . Juckes should not be taken from his ipresent employment. Mr. J uckes was , the only highly-skilled .motor , ' 1 mechanic in the district, and he also supplied the military purchasing officers with motors. Further, he , I worked for Colonel Hunt, who had . charge of the purchasing in the • eastern district, and who had given 1 permission for his name to be men- tioned in connection with this case... The Tribunal granted conditional ' ' exemption. . , E . . ' ; THE COUNTY MOTOR CO. . i STOCKTON HEATH GARAGE, , , , WARRINGTON. . _ , . Automobile Engineers. Telephone 63 Stockton .Heath.</p>
        <p>stove, warm enough to keep the fat liquid for a couple of hours. Then the chain was taken out, hung up to drain, wiped with a rag, and refitted to my machine. Incidentally, before placing the chain in the fat it is advisable to wash it thoroughly iu paraffin. HOW TO KEEP NICKEL BRIGHT. A cycle with plenty of nickel plating upon it looks very attractive, provided that it is alwaYs bright. But what looks worse than nickel plating in bad condition? It is an. excellent plan to treat all the nickel parts of a machine in the manner described below, for the•joy of being able to take out a machine on the wettest of days without aul fear of rust disfiguring the bright parts has to he experienced to be thoroughly appreciated. The process is quite a simple one. You make a solu- tion of loz. scrap celluloid, pint acetone, and 1 pint of amyl acetate. The celluloid is cut up into tiny pieces and placed in a bottle. The acetene is poured into it. and the bottle is given a good shak- ing until the celluloid has partially dissolved and somewhat resembles glue. Then the amyl acetate is added and another good shaking given. If the mixture is painted on the nickel it will dry s°,„ hard and rapidly that neither water nor air wi,„ll, have any effect on it, and the bright parts will retain their lustre until it wears off. Always keeP the solution in a tightly corked bottle, or it will rapidly deteriorate. 1 PACE THAT KILLS. Taking all in all it is a very good performance to average 25 miles an hour for several consecutive hours. I have done it myself, but it means that every minute is of importance, and there is no tirae 10/ for dawdling on the road. Quite apart from ill! questions of the danger to other road users, a higu speed means two things. The real pleasure el f motor cycling is done away with, and the engine will rapidly be worn out with such continued strain. On good roads the wear .and tear would be con siderable; the present condition of the highwaYo increases this enormously. Under suitable circuin- stances a burst of speed will do no harm. It is the continued strain that does the damage. "t SOME CONTROL WIRE TIPS. • Rust is the greatest enemy of control wires and f must be guarded against by dressings of vaseline at all exposed places. A strip of valve tubing over the cable is an effective safeguard against rust. if you have to cut the wire first solder the strands iogether. In fitting a new nipple on to the cut end, spread outythe strands of wire like the petals of ' flower and drop a small blob of solder into the centre, seeing that the solder runs right through the nipple. In refitting control wires, keep them am tight as possible with the adjuster at its hig,hest level. This will allow for taking up any slack. Avoid all sharp angles in control wires. A right angled bond is just asking for trouble. 1 SPOKES.</p>
