        <p>"An Army FiOhts On Its Stomach" If men arc to fight well they must feed well. And not only must they have access to good food but they must have digestive systems that can easily assimilate it. An army of dyspeptics would be little more efficient than an army of starving men. The same principle holds good in other spheres of human endeavour. In business, for example, where competition grows keener every year, no man can hope to make a permanent success who labours under the handicap of indigestion. The successful man is just the average man plus some extra energy, well directed, and energy is maintained by good food well digested. if you are conscious that your food is not so easily and thoroughly digested as it should be, you may, in the ordinary course, take it for granted that your stomach, liver, and bowels need a special corrective medicine, than which none is better than Beecham's Pills. This famous specific exerts a very marked stimulating effect upon the digestive organs and thereby tones up a,nd Invigorates the general health. ' BEECH_AM 11° S PILLS Sold everywhere in boxes, labelled Is. 3d and 3s. Od.</p>
