        <p>HIPPODROME, Warrington. TWICE NIGHTLY. BAS. MONDAY NEXT, JULY 3rd, AND DURING THE WEEK. Geo. Barclay presents the Laughing Cyclone of the Century— "DON'T CRUSH ! " One of the Finest and Best Revues on the road. "DON'T CRUSH ! " was specially "DON'T CRUSH ! " with a First- constructed for laughing purposes. Class Company and Funny Come- dians. "DON'T CRUSH !" is beautifully "DON'T CRUSH ! " has " SOME " staged and dressed. Beauty Chorus! Ring up 500 and Book your Favourite Seat!</p>
        <p>WELLFIELD STREET TRAGEDY,</p>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA. Royal Court. The part which Mr. Leonard Mortimer takes in "When Love Creeps in Your Heart" is not far off being a replica of what happened about 16 years ago in his own career. Here he appears as the 'central figure in a strike. Sixteen years ago he held a similar po-sition on the stage 'of life, for he was one of the leading spirits in the music hall revolt against. proprietors. "I lost £5,000 over that strike," he told me. That sounded a, lot, so I asked him to repe•it it. It still seemed a lot, so I asked him to account for it. "I was boycotted by the Len. don managers" he said. For seventeen years I had had a run in their musical halls—and that means good 'honey, you know—and through this strike I saw the provinces for the first time for 17 years. Lost contracts in London battered my fortune for the time being." Mr. Mortirn.er is the author of 140 London suc- cesses. He is prolific without being prolix. He knows how to catch the .public imagination. He is as topical as an evening paper—as for instance when Mr. Chamberlain launched his Tariff Reform campaign, Mr. Mortimer was so seized with the idea of it that he produced a sketch which was read and highly approved by Mr. Chamberlain. In another respect Mr. Mortimer has been frankly propagandist on the stage. Last year he produced a revue called 1,.` The Glorious Day '—the day of peace—and reeruiting ser- geants have estimated that he and his company were the means of getting at least 1,000 recruits. Hippodrome. The posters which told us to "Go to Jericho !" meant "Go to the Palace and Hippo- drome!" We've been. The principal " star " is George Bass, who is rapidly entering that Nirvana of comedy in which those three other Georges, Formby, Robey and Lashwood, dwell. Mr. Bass sings that he coines,from "Ode'ani,- but behind the scenes he admits he hails from Manchester. Oldham sounds more Lancastrian. He, has only been on the stage five years and has, therefore, made rapid progress in popular favour. Humour such as his finds everyone vulnerable to laughter. He is only 28 years of age, and before taking to the music hall was a professional sprinter. That perhaps accounts for the ease with which he dribbles himself across the stage. He be- gan professional entertaining as a pierrot Morecambe at 28s. per week—unless the takings did not touch that amount, of course—but as soon as he strolled on to the music hall stage pleasing portents of success flashed before him. His standing in the profession one may conclude from the fact that he has been booked for pantomime in London from Christmas onward at £l2O a week. Mr. Bass regards pantomime as his favourite hunting ground. It was, in fact,' in pantomime that he met his wife, Miss Phyllis Deane, the lady with the beautiful hair, the one who has the contretemps with him while he conducts the orchestra. They have a boy 12 months old —a small Bass! Since the posters of "Don't Crush!" have been on the walls I have had some pleasant recollections of this show. It is several months since I saw it, but I hope to renew its acquaint- ance next week, for it ranks as one of the best revues on tour. "Don't Crush!" is beautifully staged with' scenery, ladies, and comedians. Its several scenes are artistically magnificent, its chorus contains some winners in beauty competitions, land its comedians are of the first class. Jack Gallagher, the principal humorist, is a striking I and grotesque figure with a wonderful capacity I for making folk smile broadly. lie is not the sort that hurry about the stage—drollery is I his forte, and loud laughter our portion. ' I may mention that Mr. Gallagher is an intimate friend of Mr. Bass's. The Kinentas. Big audiences were attracted to the Star Kinema during this week, when Hall Caine's great novel, "The Christian," is being pre- sented. Mr. Derwent Hall Caine takes the lead- ing part. Next Monday, Tuesday and Wednes- day a striking play, "Her Hidden Past," will be produced, while for the remainder of the week</p>
